window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Axioms, inference rules, and proofs", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Axioms, inference rules, and proofs § axiom · rules of inference A proof separates two jobs: choosing assumptions and drawing consequences from them. The assumptions are axioms ; the permitted steps are rules of inference . From \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” for example, we may infer \\(Q\\). In the even-integer argument from the introduction, a reader can check each step without discovering the argument again. Make the notation and the permitted steps precise enough, and a machine can do that checking too. This is the connection to computation: a proof becomes a finite object that a procedure can inspect. Checking establishes that the conclusion follows from the chosen axioms. Whether those axioms capture what we intended is a separate question. Predict first. Generate a new proof. Before checking it, decide which lines the checker must accept without justification, and what it must confirm about each of the others. A proof as a network of justified steps New random proof Check it step by step Back to the example Each arrow shows a dependency between proof steps. In the example, the same inference rule first gives \\(Q\\) from \\(P\\), then \\(R\\) from \\(Q\\). A new random proof uses only modus ponens, starting from randomly chosen axioms. The numbered list is the same proof written as a sequence, which a checker reads line by line. After trying it: compare your explanation Only the axioms are accepted as given. For every other line, the checker finds two earlier lines of the forms X and “X implies Y” and confirms that the line is Y. It compares symbols; it never has to discover the proof. Problems From the axioms \\(P\\), “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” and “\\(Q\\) implies \\(R\\),” with modus ponens as the only rule of inference, write a proof of \\(R\\). Give the justification of each line. Prove that the sum of an even integer and an odd integer is odd, giving the justification for each step. A checker accepts every line of a proof. Show by an example that the conclusion can still be false, and say exactly what the accepted proof establishes."}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program and consistency", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Hilbert’s program and consistency § consistent · finitary methods Writing rules explicitly lets us check whether an argument follows them. But are the rules themselves reliable? Could they allow us to prove both a statement and its opposite? A system that never does this is called consistent . Hilbert wanted to put mathematics on a secure foundation. First, its assumptions and proof rules would be made explicit. Then one would show that those rules could never produce a contradiction. He hoped to give this justification using reasoning about finite objects and concrete operations on them. These were called finitary methods . Even when a mathematical statement concerns infinitely many numbers, its written proof is finite. So, proofs and the rules for constructing them could themselves be studied. This was a question about the reliability of mathematical reasoning. A related question concerns its automation: once the rules are explicit, can a procedure always determine whether a statement has a proof? To see the difficulty, we must distinguish checking a proof from searching for one. Hilbert’s aim was to study the finite proofs allowed by a system and show that none could lead to a contradiction. Problems A system has the axioms \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies not \\(P\\),” and the rule modus ponens. Is it consistent? Suppose a system also has the rule “from \\(A\\) and not \\(A\\), infer any statement \\(B\\).” Show that if the system is inconsistent, it proves every statement. Hilbert hoped to prove that systems are consistent. Show that a consistency proof carried out inside the same system \\(S\\) could not settle the doubt, by showing that an inconsistent \\(S\\) of the kind in the previous problem proves the statement “\\(S\\) is consistent.”"}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decision", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decision § proof search · decision procedure Suppose a proposed proof fails a check. We have rejected that argument, not its conclusion. Another argument may succeed. Now suppose no proof is supplied. If candidate proofs use a fixed finite alphabet and can be checked mechanically, list strings by length and test them. Every finite proof eventually appears. If a proof exists, this search finds one. But if no proof exists, there is no final candidate whose rejection settles the matter. The search simply continues. A decision procedure must do more: finish with a correct yes or no on every input. Could a cleverer procedure succeed? Failure of this search strategy does not rule out all strategies. To ask that larger question, we need a precise account of what a mechanical procedure is. That is why models of computation enter the story. Checking tests one proposed proof. Searching tries candidates until it finds a proof. Deciding must also finish when no proof exists. Problems Strings over the alphabet \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) are listed by length, starting with the empty string, and alphabetically within each length. Write the first seven strings, and give the position of \\(ab\\). A proof search lists candidate strings over a two-symbol alphabet in this order and checks each one. If the shortest proof of a statement has length \\(5\\), at most how many candidates are checked before a proof is found? Why can such a bound not be computed in advance for an arbitrary statement? Suppose a theory is consistent, its axioms can be listed by an algorithm, and it is complete: for every sentence \\(P\\), it proves \\(P\\) or it proves not \\(P\\). Show that searching for a proof of \\(P\\) and a proof of not \\(P\\) in alternation is a decision procedure for provability."}, {"title": "Algorithms and termination", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Algorithms and termination § Euclid’s algorithm · invariant Take a task we can finish by hand: find the greatest common divisor of \\(48\\) and \\(18\\). Euclid’s algorithm repeatedly replaces a pair by its second entry and the remainder on division: \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] It returns \\(6\\). The example suggests a rule, but two questions remain: why is the answer right, and why must the procedure stop? Write \\(a=qb+r\\), with \\(0\\leq r invariant: it stays fixed while the pair changes. Meanwhile the nonnegative second entry strictly decreases. It cannot do that forever. Eventually the pair is \\((g,0)\\), whose greatest common divisor is \\(g\\), so we return it. These are separate arguments. Repeating the original pair forever would preserve the correct answer but never deliver it. An algorithm needs both a reason to trust its result and a reason to expect that result. Predict first. Before stepping, predict how many steps the run from (48, 18) takes and which quantity stays fixed. Then try (89, 55): will it need more steps or fewer? Run Euclid’s algorithm First entry Second entry Next step Run Reset Each row is one configuration of the run. The greatest common divisor stays the same in every row, while the second entry, drawn as a bar, decreases through nonnegative integers until it is zero. These are separate reasons for correctness and termination. Try other entries, such as \\(89\\) and \\(55\\). After trying it: compare your explanation The run reaches (6, 0) after three steps, and the greatest common divisor 6 is the same in every row. Consecutive Fibonacci numbers such as (89, 55) need nine steps: every quotient is 1, so the second entry falls as slowly as possible. Problems Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((91,35)\\), writing each division and each pair. Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((21,13)\\). How many divisions does it take, and why does this pair make the second entry decrease slowly? Give a procedure on pairs of positive integers that keeps the greatest common divisor unchanged at every step but never stops, and another that always stops but can return a wrong answer. What does this show about the two arguments for Euclid’s algorithm?"}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Configurations and transitions § configuration · transition · run Suppose we pause Euclid’s algorithm at the pair \\((18,12)\\), just before the next division. What must we record in order to continue? We need the pair and the instruction that comes next. We do not need the earlier pairs. The information needed to resume a computation is called its configuration . What must we include? That depends on where we allow the computation to pause. At the start of Euclid’s repeated step, we need only the pair: the next instruction is already fixed. Halfway through a division, the pair alone would not be enough. We would also need the temporary values and a record of how far the division had progressed. A transition is one step from a configuration to the next. Starting with a particular input, we obtain a sequence of configurations. This sequence is called a run . The algorithm supplies the transition rules and tells us when to stop. Proof search can be described in the same way. We record the candidate being checked, the line currently under examination, and any other information needed by the checker. Each operation updates this configuration. For a deterministic procedure, the current configuration determines the next step. Does it also determine the previous step? In Euclid’s algorithm, both \\((30,18)\\) and \\((48,18)\\) lead to \\((18,12)\\). So, knowing how to continue does not necessarily tell us how to go backward. We will return to this distinction when we consider reversibility. A configuration includes enough information to resume the computation. A transition updates that information; a run is a sequence of these updates. Problems Euclid’s algorithm divides by repeated subtraction. It is paused in the middle of dividing \\(48\\) by \\(18\\), after one subtraction has given \\(30\\). What must the configuration record so that the computation can resume? Write the run of Euclid’s algorithm from \\((30,18)\\) as a sequence of configurations. Find two other pairs whose runs reach \\((18,12)\\) after one step. Describe all pairs from which Euclid’s algorithm reaches \\((18,12)\\) in one step. What does the answer imply about running the algorithm backward?"}, {"title": "Turing machines and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Turing machines and universal computation § Turing machine · tape · head · control state · rule table · halting state · universal machine An instruction such as “divide” still hides a computation. A Turing machine gives us elementary steps explicit enough to study what any program can do. Its tape consists of cells holding symbols from a finite alphabet, including a blank. A head reads one cell. A finite control state records the stage of the instructions. For each state and read symbol, the rule table specifies what to write, which way to move, and the next state. A designated halting state ends the run. For example, start at the left end of a finite block of ones. Move right on a one; halt on a blank. The same finite rule table handles blocks of every finite length. There is no fixed tape bound, but any finite run visits only finitely many cells. A configuration records the tape, head position, and control state. The decisive observation is that a rule table is itself finite data. Encode it as a string and another machine can read it and imitate its steps. A universal machine takes a program description together with an input and performs this simulation. Changing the supplied description changes the program being simulated. A program can now receive another program—or its own description—as input. No infinite object or mysterious self-awareness is involved: it receives a finite string. This will let us turn a proposed predictor of program behavior into a program that defeats that prediction. Predict first. Generate a new problem. Before running it, answer the question yourself, then predict how the machine will reach the answer: which arrow does each step use, and in which state does it halt? For the opening example, how many steps does a block of n ones take? A Turing machine at work New random problem Next step Run Reset Back to the example The pointer marks the cell under the head; shaded cells have been visited. Below the tape is the machine’s finite control drawn as a state diagram. Circles are control states, a double circle is a halting state, and each arrow is one rule. An arrow labeled \\(1\\to1,\\ R\\) means: when reading 1, write 1, move right, and follow the arrow. □ is a blank; L, R, S mean left, right, and stay; “same” means the symbol read is written back unchanged. The highlighted arrow is the rule just used. New random problem builds another machine, such as a pattern search, a divisibility test, a palindrome or parenthesis checker, or a copier, with a fresh input. The line under the diagram writes the same rules as a string. A universal machine reads such a string together with an input and imitates these steps; it need not halt if the simulated run does not halt. After trying it: compare your explanation Each step uses exactly one arrow: the one leaving the current state whose label begins with the symbol under the head. The opening example takes n + 1 steps: n moves right over the ones and one step that reads the blank and halts. Machines that mark symbols, such as the palindrome or parenthesis checkers, sweep back and forth, so their step counts grow much faster than the input length. Tracing the diagram this way is what a universal machine does with the encoded rules. Problems A machine has a running state \\(q\\) and a halting state \\(h\\). In \\(q\\), reading \\(1\\), it writes \\(1\\), moves right, and stays in \\(q\\); reading a blank, it halts. Started on the leftmost \\(1\\) of a tape holding \\(111\\), list its configurations until it halts. Change the machine so that, reading a blank in state \\(q\\), it writes \\(1\\) and halts. What does the new machine compute on a block of \\(n\\) ones, and how many steps does it take? Design a machine that, started on the leftmost symbol of a block of ones, halts in a state marked “even” if the block has an even number of ones and “odd” otherwise. Give its rule table and trace it on \\(111\\)."}, {"title": "The Church–Turing thesis", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 The Church–Turing thesis § lambda calculus · Church–Turing thesis We have defined a model using a tape, a head, and a finite rule table. Why use this model to discuss the limits of effective calculation? Could another reasonable model compute functions that Turing machines cannot? Other mathematical models describe computation in different ways. Lambda calculus uses rules for substituting expressions. Recursive-function models build functions from specified basic functions and construction rules. Despite these differences, they compute the same functions as Turing machines. A function computable in one model is computable in the others. This equivalence is a mathematical result that can be proved. The Church–Turing thesis makes a further claim: any calculation that can be carried out by an effective mechanical method can be carried out by a Turing machine. The phrase “effective mechanical method” is an informal idea. A Turing machine is a precisely defined object. The thesis connects the two. The agreement between different formal models supports it, but does not turn it into a theorem about an already defined class of every possible mechanical method. This distinction matters when we establish a limitation. We can prove that no Turing machine solves a specified problem. Applying that result to effective procedures in general uses the thesis. The thesis concerns whether a function can be computed at all. It does not say how much time or memory the computation needs. This matters for quantum computing. In the standard quantum model, the allowed operations must themselves have effective mathematical descriptions. Under this condition, quantum computers do not compute functions that are uncomputable by Turing machines. They can change the resources needed for some tasks that are already computable. We will return to this difference when studying quantum algorithms. The solid arrows connect formal models that can simulate one another. They compute the same functions, though they may take different amounts of time. The dashed arrow marks the further claim of the Church–Turing thesis: these models capture what we mean informally by an effective method. Problems A programming language \\(L\\) can simulate every Turing machine, and a Turing machine can simulate every program of \\(L\\). Which functions does \\(L\\) compute? Is this conclusion a theorem or an instance of the Church–Turing thesis? Classify each statement as a theorem or as the Church–Turing thesis: (a) lambda calculus and Turing machines compute the same functions; (b) every function computable by an effective mechanical method is Turing-computable; (c) every recursive function is Turing-computable. Show that some functions from nonnegative integers to \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) are computed by no Turing machine. Turing machines can be listed as \\(M_0,M_1,M_2,\\dots\\), since each has a finite description."}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 The halting problem § halting problem Given a program and its input, can we always determine whether the computation will stop? We know that Euclid’s algorithm stops for its permitted inputs. We also know that a program instructed to repeat forever does not stop. Can one procedure settle this question for every program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is a program description and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to finish and answer correctly on every valid pair. Let us use \\(H\\) to construct another program, \\(D\\). Its input \\(p\\) is a program description. First, \\(D\\) calls \\(H(p,p)\\): it asks what happens when the program described by \\(p\\) receives its own description as input. Then \\(D\\) takes the opposite action. If \\(H\\) answers “halts,” \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If \\(H\\) answers “does not halt,” \\(D\\) stops. If the input is not a valid program description, we can simply let \\(D\\) stop. Now let \\(d\\) be the description of \\(D\\), and give \\(d\\) to \\(D\\). What should \\(H(d,d)\\) answer? If it answers “halts,” the instructions of \\(D\\) make this run continue forever. If it answers “does not halt,” the instructions make the run stop. In either case, the prediction is wrong. We assumed that \\(H\\) was an algorithm that always finished and answered correctly. That assumption allowed us to use it as part of \\(D\\). But the resulting program contradicts the assumption. Therefore no such general \\(H\\) exists. This result is called the undecidability of the halting problem . This does not prevent us from determining the behavior of particular programs. We already proved that Euclid’s algorithm terminates. It also permits an analyzer that sometimes answers “unknown.” What is excluded is one algorithm that always finishes with a correct definite answer for every case. Assume \\(H\\) always finishes and predicts correctly. Program \\(D\\) follows its prediction and takes the opposite action. Now give \\(D\\) its own description \\(d\\). \\(H(d,d)\\) is predicting this very run, so either answer is contradicted by what \\(D\\) does. Problems Decide, by inspecting each program, whether it halts on the input \\(x=5\\): (a) while \\(x\\gt 0\\), set \\(x:=x-1\\); (b) while \\(x\\neq0\\), set \\(x:=x-2\\); (c) repeat forever: do nothing. A proposed halting test runs a program for a million steps. It answers “halts” if the program stops and “never halts” otherwise. Give a program on which this test is wrong. Would replacing a million by a larger fixed number repair it? Show that no algorithm decides, for every program \\(P\\), whether \\(P\\) halts when given the input \\(0\\). Use the undecidability of the halting problem: from a pair \\((P,x)\\), construct a program that ignores its own input."}, {"title": "Undecidability and incompleteness", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Undecidability and incompleteness § undecidable problem · incomplete theory · second incompleteness theorem There are two distinct limits here. An undecidable problem has no algorithm that always terminates with the correct yes-or-no answer. An incomplete theory leaves some sentence neither provable nor refutable from its axioms. One concerns procedures; the other concerns what follows from fixed assumptions. The Gödel–Rosser theorem applies to consistent theories whose axioms can be effectively enumerated and which contain enough arithmetic. Peano arithmetic is a familiar example of the required arithmetic strength. Such a theory has a sentence \\(P\\) for which neither \\(P\\) nor \\(\\neg P\\) is provable. We state this theorem rather than attempt its proof here. Adding \\(P\\) as an axiom settles that sentence. But if the enlarged theory still meets the theorem’s conditions, another sentence remains unsettled. The result is not that every theory is incomplete; its conditions are essential. Gödel’s second incompleteness theorem addresses Hilbert’s consistency question. Under its arithmetic and proof-representation conditions, a consistent theory cannot prove its own standard formal statement of consistency. A stronger theory may justify a weaker one, but the stronger theory’s assumptions then become part of what we trust. For this course, retain the distinction: making rules explicit enables mechanical checking without guaranteeing that every mathematical question can be settled. We can now turn to tasks that do admit algorithms and ask what information their implementations preserve. On the left, we ask whether an algorithm can decide a problem for every input. On the right, we ask what a particular theory can prove. The incompleteness result requires the stated conditions on that theory. An unsuccessful search alone establishes neither result. Problems A theory has the single axiom \\(P\\) and the rule modus ponens, and its sentences are built from \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). Is \\(Q\\) provable? Is not \\(Q\\) provable? Is the theory complete? A sentence \\(G\\) is neither provable nor refutable in a consistent theory \\(T\\). Show that both \\(T\\) with \\(G\\) added as an axiom and \\(T\\) with not \\(G\\) added are consistent. You may use the fact that if \\(T\\) with an added axiom \\(A\\) proves a contradiction, then \\(T\\) proves not \\(A\\). Suppose an algorithm extends a consistent, effectively axiomatized theory of arithmetic step by step, each time adding a sentence that the current theory leaves undecided. Show that the combined theory, with all the added axioms together, is still incomplete if it is consistent."}, {"title": "Deterministic and reversible transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Deterministic and reversible transitions § deterministic · one-to-one · bijection · logical reversibility In a deterministic computation, each configuration has at most one next configuration. A halting configuration may have none. When the computation can continue, the current configuration fixes what happens next. Does knowing the later configuration also tell us which earlier one produced it? Determinism does not require this. For example, Euclid’s algorithm includes the transitions \\[(30,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12),\\qquad(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12).\\] Knowing the pair \\((18,12)\\) lets us continue the calculation, but it does not identify which earlier pair occurred. Different configurations have merged into the same one. To undo a step, we must be able to recover the earlier configuration from the full resulting configuration. This requires different inputs to have different outputs. Such a transition is called one-to-one . Logical reversibility requires this extra condition. Suppose the transition also reaches every configuration in a fixed state space. It is then a bijection : each configuration has exactly one predecessor. We can define the reverse transformation everywhere on that space. So, knowing what happens next does not necessarily tell us what happened before. We now ask whether useful computations can preserve enough information to be reversed. On the left, each input has a unique successor, but \\(C\\) has two possible predecessors. On the right, each output identifies one predecessor. Reversibility imposes a condition on the backward direction as well. Problems A rule on the configurations \\(A,B,C,D\\) sends \\(A\\to B\\), \\(B\\to C\\), \\(C\\to D\\), and \\(D\\to B\\). Is it deterministic? Which configurations have more than one predecessor, and which have none? Run the same rule for six steps from each of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). Which starting configurations end at the same configuration after six steps? Prove that a deterministic, one-to-one rule on finitely many configurations is onto, and that every run of such a rule eventually returns to its starting configuration."}, {"title": "Information loss in computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Information loss in computation § many-to-one · unitary transformation AND returns zero on three inputs: \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\). If we keep only the answer, we cannot recover which input occurred. But obtaining an answer need not mean throwing away its input. The pair \\((x,f(x))\\) still contains \\(x\\), even if \\(f\\) is many-to-one. This suggests extra storage, but storage alone is not a construction. We must choose reversible elementary steps, specify the initial values of the extra bits, and clear temporary results without losing information. Chapter 2 supplies those ingredients. The reason to pursue this is physical as well as logical. Closed-system quantum evolution is described by a unitary transformation, which has an inverse. A quantum circuit must therefore obtain useful answers while respecting reversible evolution between measurements. We have moved from “Can this task be computed?” to “How can its computation preserve the information that quantum evolution requires?” Predict first. Before checking a new function, look at its sizes. If it maps 2 bits to 1 bit, must it be irreversible? Then try to name two inputs that share an output, and predict what Keep the input too does to them. Does a Boolean function lose information? New random function Check next input Run Reset Back to AND Keep the input too Each arrow sends an input to its output. The checker visits the inputs in order and stops as soon as two arrows reach the same output: then the output does not tell us which input occurred, and the function is irreversible. If no two arrows meet, every output identifies its input. If, in addition, every possible output is reached, the function is a bijection. Keep the input too replaces \\(f(x)\\) by the pair \\((x,f(x))\\), which never merges two inputs. Chapter 2 turns this idea into a reversible construction that uses an extra target bit and XOR. After trying it: compare your explanation A function from 2 bits to 1 bit has 4 inputs but only 2 possible outputs, so two inputs must share one: it is irreversible whatever its table. With equal numbers of input and output bits, it is reversible exactly when no two arrows meet. Keeping the input gives each x the output (x, f(x)), whose first part already differs between different inputs, so no two arrows can meet. Problems Which of these operations on two bits lose information: \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\land y\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\oplus y\\)? Keep both inputs and store the AND in an extra bit \\(t\\) that starts at \\(0\\): \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\land y))\\). List the outputs for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\), and show that the map can be undone. A computation maps \\(n\\)-bit inputs to a single output bit. Show that at least \\(2^{n-1}\\) inputs share an output. If the computation keeps some bits besides its output, how many must it keep, at least, to become one-to-one, and how many always suffice?"}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-1", "text": "A proof search over a three-symbol alphabet has checked every candidate of length at most \\(10\\), including the empty string, without finding a proof. How many candidates is that, and what does the failure establish? \\(1+3+\\dots+3^{10}=(3^{11}-1)/2=88{,}573\\) candidates. The failure shows only that the statement has no proof of length at most \\(10\\). A longer proof may exist, and no finite search can establish that none does."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-2", "text": "A Turing machine is paused mid-run. You are told the tape contents \\(\\square111\\square\\) and that the head is on the second \\(1\\), but not the control state. The machine has states \\(q_0\\) (moving right over ones) and \\(q_1\\) (moving left back to the start). Can you resume the computation? What must a configuration contain? No. In \\(q_0\\) the next step moves the head right; in \\(q_1\\) it moves left. The tape and head position alone leave the next step undetermined. A configuration of a Turing machine is the triple of tape contents, head position, and control state, which together are exactly enough to resume. For Euclid’s algorithm, paused at the start of its repeated step, the pair alone sufficed because the next instruction was always the same. What a configuration must include depends on where the computation is allowed to pause."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-3", "text": "The rule \\(n\\mapsto\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (halve and round down) acts on the numbers \\(0,1,\\dots,7\\). Is it deterministic? Is it one-to-one? Show how to keep one extra bit so that the step can be undone, and say what that bit records. It is deterministic: each \\(n\\) has exactly one result. It is not one-to-one: \\(2k\\) and \\(2k+1\\) both go to \\(k\\), so \\(0,1\\mapsto0\\), \\(2,3\\mapsto1\\), and so on. Keep the remainder \\(n\\bmod2\\) as an extra bit: \\(n\\mapsto(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\,n\\bmod2)\\). Now \\(n=2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+(n\\bmod2)\\) recovers the input, so the extended step is one-to-one. The extra bit records exactly the information the original step discarded, the last binary digit of \\(n\\)."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-4", "text": "Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((377,233)\\). Count the divisions, record each quotient, and explain separately why the answer is correct and why the run stops. The remainders are \\(144,89,55,34,21,13,8,5,3,2,1,0\\): twelve divisions, with quotient \\(1\\) every time except the last, which is \\(2\\). The greatest common divisor is \\(1\\). It is correct because each step keeps the set of common divisors unchanged, an invariant. The run stops because the second entry strictly decreases through nonnegative integers. Quotients of \\(1\\) make that decrease as slow as possible."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-5", "text": "Design a Turing machine that adds one to a binary number. The head starts on the rightmost digit, in state \\(q\\). Reading \\(1\\): write \\(0\\), move left, stay in \\(q\\). Reading \\(0\\) or a blank: write \\(1\\) and halt. Trace it on \\(1011\\) and on \\(111\\). How many steps does an \\(n\\)-digit input take in the worst case? On \\(1011\\): read \\(1\\), write \\(0\\), move left (tape \\(1010\\)); read \\(1\\), write \\(0\\), move left (\\(1000\\)); read \\(0\\), write \\(1\\), halt (\\(1100\\)). Three steps, and \\(1100\\) is \\(12=11+1\\). On \\(111\\): three steps turn the ones into zeros, then the head reads the blank to the left, writes \\(1\\), and halts, giving \\(1000\\), which is \\(8=7+1\\), after four steps. In general the machine takes one step per trailing \\(1\\), plus one, so the worst case is \\(n+1\\) steps, for an input of all ones. The same finite rule table handles inputs of every length, which is the point of the model."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-6", "text": "Euclid’s algorithm stops because the second entry decreases. Show something stronger: after any two steps, the second entry is less than half of what it was. Use this to bound the number of divisions for a pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le233\\), and compare with the run on \\((377,233)\\). If \\(r_1\\le b/2\\), then \\(r_2<r_1\\le b/2\\). If \\(r_1>b/2\\), then \\(r_1\\) goes into \\(b\\) once, so \\(r_2=b-r_1<b/2\\). Either way \\(r_2<b/2\\). So every two divisions at least halve the second entry. Starting from \\(b\\le233<2^8\\), after \\(16\\) divisions the second entry is below \\(233/2^8<1\\), so it is \\(0\\): at most \\(16\\) divisions. The run on \\((377,233)\\) uses \\(12\\), close to this bound, because consecutive Fibonacci numbers have every quotient equal to \\(1\\), the slowest case. The bound grows with the number of digits of \\(b\\), not with \\(b\\) itself, a first example of measuring the resources an algorithm needs."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-7", "text": "Show that no algorithm can decide, for every program \\(P\\) and input \\(x\\), whether \\(P\\) ever prints the word “hello” when run on \\(x\\). Use the undecidability of the halting problem: from a pair \\((P,x)\\), build a program \\(P'\\) that prints “hello” exactly when \\(P\\) halts on \\(x\\). Let \\(P'\\) simulate \\(P\\) on \\(x\\), discarding everything \\(P\\) would print, and print “hello” once the simulation halts. If \\(P\\) halts on \\(x\\), then \\(P'\\) prints “hello”. If not, the simulation runs forever and \\(P'\\) never prints anything. Building \\(P'\\) from \\((P,x)\\) is a mechanical rewriting of a finite description. Suppose an algorithm \\(D\\) decided whether a program prints “hello”. Then “build \\(P'\\), then run \\(D\\) on \\(P'\\)” would always finish and would answer whether \\(P\\) halts on \\(x\\). No such procedure exists, so \\(D\\) cannot exist. This pattern, turning a halting question into a question about another property, shows that many questions about program behavior are undecidable."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “The halting problem is undecidable, so no algorithm can tell whether Euclid’s algorithm halts on \\((48,18)\\).” Explain what is wrong. Then describe an algorithm that answers correctly for one fixed program and input, and say why this does not contradict the theorem. The theorem excludes one algorithm that finishes with the correct answer for every program and input. It says nothing against settling particular cases. Euclid’s algorithm halts on \\((48,18)\\); we proved it halts on every pair of positive integers. More strikingly, every single question “does \\(P\\) halt on \\(x\\)?” is answered correctly by one of the two programs “print yes” and “print no.” One of them is right, even if we do not know which. Undecidability means that no single procedure, given \\((P,x)\\) as input, always finds the right answer. The difficulty lies in uniformity across all inputs, not in any one instance."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-9", "text": "Make each step of Euclid’s algorithm reversible by recording its quotient. Show that \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\) determine the previous pair, and find how many quotients a run on \\((377,233)\\) must keep. From \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\), the previous first entry is \\(a=qb+r\\), so the previous pair \\((a,b)\\) is recovered. A run that keeps all its quotients can be undone step by step. The run on \\((377,233)\\) keeps twelve quotients, eleven \\(1\\)s and one \\(2\\). Reversibility costs extra memory that grows with the length of the run."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-10", "text": "Suppose, contrary to fact, that a halting decider \\(H(P,x)\\) existed. Show that it would turn proof search into a decision procedure for provability in a theory whose proofs can be checked mechanically: given a statement \\(S\\), build the program that searches for a proof of \\(S\\) and halts when it finds one. What would that imply for the incompleteness result of Section 1.3.3? Let \\(Q_S\\) list candidate strings by length, check each, and halt on the first valid proof of \\(S\\). It halts exactly when \\(S\\) is provable. Running \\(H(Q_S,\\text{empty input})\\) would always finish and report whether \\(S\\) is provable, so provability would be decidable. This is consistent with Section 1.1.3: the search alone may run forever, and \\(H\\) would supply the missing ability to recognize that it never ends. It would not remove incompleteness. Deciding whether \\(S\\) is provable is different from proving \\(S\\): with \\(H\\) we could recognize a sentence that is neither provable nor refutable, but it would remain unsettled by the axioms. The argument shows that provability is no harder to decide than halting, so the obstacle met in proof search is the same kind of obstacle the halting theorem identifies."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § encoding A computer acts on bit strings. An encoding tells us what those strings mean: \\(00,01,10,11\\) might represent the integers zero through three, while \\(01000001\\) can represent either the integer 65 or the character A in ASCII. The choice matters because computational cost is measured against input length. An integer \\(N\\) needs about \\(\\log_2N\\) binary digits, but \\(N\\) marks in unary. The same numerical task can therefore have very different size measures. For a fixed length \\(n\\), a computation acts on \\(2^n\\) possible bit strings. An algorithm handles all permitted lengths by one systematic procedure; we do not get to hide a separately invented answer table for every size. An encoding supplies meaning. The bits alone do not tell a machine whether to treat \\(01000001\\) as a number or a character. Problems Write \\(13\\) in binary with four bits, and decode \\(1011\\) as an unsigned binary integer. An integer \\(N\\) is written in binary and, alternatively, in unary, as \\(N\\) marks. For \\(N=1000\\), compare the two input lengths. How does a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps compare with each length? Show that any encoding of the integers \\(0,1,\\dots,M-1\\) by bit strings of one fixed length \\(n\\) needs \\(n\\geq\\log_2M\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § Boolean function · truth table What exactly do we want to compute? Take a yes/no question about an \\(n\\)-bit input. Its answer is a Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), which assigns \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) to every input. An answer of several bits is a list of such functions, one for each output bit. The most direct way to specify \\(f\\) is to list every input together with its output. This list is the truth table of \\(f\\). For AND and OR on two bits: Input \\(xy\\) AND OR \\(00\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) \\(01\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(10\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(11\\) \\(1\\) \\(1\\) Does a truth table tell us how to compute \\(f\\)? It tells us what the answer is, not how to obtain it. It also grows quickly: a function of \\(n\\) bits has \\(2^n\\) rows, so the table alone is exponentially large. We want a description built from a few simple operations. The truth table lists four possible inputs. Only the input \\(11\\) produces one; a circuit must implement all four rows. Problems Write the truth table of the majority function on three bits, which outputs \\(1\\) when at least two of its inputs are \\(1\\). How many Boolean functions of two bits are there? List those that output \\(1\\) on exactly one input. Show that there are \\(2^{2^n}\\) Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits. For \\(n=10\\), show that fewer than one function in a thousand can be described by a string of fewer than \\(1014\\) bits."}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § arithmetic modulo two · XOR · detector Can a truth table be turned into a formula? Use arithmetic modulo two, in which \\(1+1=0\\). Addition modulo two is then XOR, and multiplication is AND. The idea is to build, for each row \\(s\\) of the table, a detector that equals \\(1\\) on the input \\(s\\) and \\(0\\) on every other input. The factor \\(x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=1\\), and the factor \\(1+x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=0\\). Multiplying one such factor for each bit gives \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] For two bits \\((x,y)\\), the detector of the row \\(01\\) is \\((1+x)y\\). Now add the detectors of the rows on which \\(f\\) equals \\(1\\): \\[f(x)=\\sum_{s:\\,f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x)\\pmod 2.\\] Why is this \\(f\\)? On any input \\(x\\), exactly one detector, \\(\\delta_x\\), equals \\(1\\). If the row \\(x\\) is among those added, the sum is \\(1\\); otherwise it is \\(0\\). For OR, the rows with output \\(1\\) are \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\): \\[(1+x)y+x(1+y)+xy=x+y+3xy=x+y+xy\\pmod 2.\\] Check the row \\(11\\): \\(1+1+1=1\\) modulo two, as required, whereas ordinary addition would give \\(3\\). Expanding the detectors in this way writes every Boolean function as a polynomial in its input bits. All additions are modulo two. The polynomial \\(x+y+xy\\) reproduces OR on Boolean inputs; its interpretation depends on this arithmetic. Problems Evaluate \\((1+x)(1+y)\\) modulo two for all four inputs. Which function is it? Build the polynomial of XOR from its truth table using detectors, and simplify it. Show that the polynomial of the three-bit majority function is \\(xy+yz+xz\\) modulo two."}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § circuit · gate · wire How do we evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) one operation at a time? Compute \\(u=xy\\) with an AND gate, compute \\(x+y\\) with an XOR gate, and combine the two results with a second XOR gate. This arrangement is a circuit . Its gates are elementary operations, and its wires carry bits from one gate to the next; here a wire carries \\(u\\) from the AND gate to the last XOR gate. A wire may also carry a fixed bit, \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). No wire leads back to an earlier gate, so the gates can be evaluated in order. The same method works for every polynomial: multiply factors with AND gates and add terms with XOR gates. So every Boolean function of a fixed number of bits has a circuit. Does it have a small circuit? Not necessarily: the polynomial built from a truth table can have exponentially many terms. That every function can be built is one claim; that it can be built economically is another. Circuit for \\(z=x+y+xy\\). The AND gate computes \\(u=xy\\), the first XOR computes \\(v=x+y\\), and the final XOR gives \\(z=u+v\\). Each input branches to both first-stage gates. Problems Evaluate the circuit for \\(x+y+xy\\), built from one AND gate and two XOR gates, on the input \\(10\\), giving the bit on every wire. Build a circuit for majority, \\(xy+yz+xz\\), from AND and XOR gates. How many gates does it use? The parity function \\(x_1+x_2+\\dots+x_n\\pmod2\\) equals \\(1\\) on half of its inputs. How many detectors does the truth-table construction add, and how many XOR gates does the simplified polynomial need?"}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § NAND · universal · fan-out Do we need AND, XOR, and NOT as separate kinds of gate? One kind is enough. NAND outputs the negation of AND, \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)=\\neg(a\\land b)\\); it is \\(0\\) only when \\(a=b=1\\). Tying both inputs to the same bit gives NOT: \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,a)=\\neg a\\). Negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR takes four NAND gates: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs confirms the last identity. Since NAND builds AND, XOR, and NOT, it can evaluate the polynomial of any Boolean function. A gate with this property is universal . In this construction one bit often feeds several gates: \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) each feed two NAND gates, and so does \\(t\\). Copying a bit in this way, called fan-out , is free in classical circuits. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation. Problems Compute \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\) for all four inputs. Use the table to check that \\(\\operatorname{NAND}\\bigl(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\bigr)=a\\land b\\). Build OR from NAND gates, and verify the construction on all four inputs. Show that circuits of AND and XOR gates, with no constant inputs, cannot compute NOT, but that adding the constant \\(1\\) makes the set universal."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § An AND output of one identifies its input: it must have been \\(11\\). An output of zero does not: \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) all fit. Reversibility must work for every allowed input, so unrestricted AND has no inverse. Always specify which registers are kept. A circuit can discard an intermediate copy while still retaining enough information elsewhere to recover its original input. Conversely, an output that looks reversible need not have been produced through reversible elementary steps. For a quantum implementation, we need the stronger construction: a sequence of steps that each preserves distinctions in the full state. The next sections show how to obtain the same Boolean answer without using AND as a two-input, one-output erasure. The AND gate maps \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) to the same output. Output zero cannot identify which of these inputs occurred. Problems For OR on two bits, list the inputs that share each output. Can the inputs be recovered from the output? A circuit computes \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\land y,\\;x\\lor y)\\). Is this map one-to-one? Which inputs collide? The map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,\\,x\\oplus y)\\) is computed by copying \\(x\\) and feeding \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) into an XOR gate, which on its own loses information. Show that the map is one-to-one, and explain where the information discarded by the XOR gate survives."}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § Landauer’s principle A reset sends either initial bit value to zero. In the standard isothermal setting, erasing one equally likely bit without retaining its value elsewhere releases at least \\[Q\\geq k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\] of heat into the environment on average. This is Landauer’s principle ; \\(T\\) is absolute temperature and \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The bound is about an entropy reduction under stated physical conditions, not a fee charged whenever a gate runs. A bit already known to be zero can be left untouched. Bias or usable side information changes the uncertainty being removed; if that information lives in another physical memory, its eventual treatment matters too. We will not need a thermodynamic derivation. The computational question is enough for now: can a useful calculation avoid merging distinct logical states? If it can, it avoids forcing erasure at those steps, though its physical implementation may still dissipate energy. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation . Both possible logical inputs are reset to zero. For erasure of one equally likely bit in the standard isothermal setting, the minimum heat delivered to the environment is kBT ln 2. Problems Compute \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) at \\(T=300\\) K, with \\(k_{\\mathrm B}=1.38\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K. A processor erases \\(10^{18}\\) bits per second at \\(300\\) K. What minimum power does Landauer’s principle require? Compare it with a processor that uses \\(50\\) W. Erasing a bit that is \\(0\\) with probability \\(p\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1-p\\) has the minimum cost \\(H\\,k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\), where \\(H=-p\\log_2p-(1-p)\\log_2(1-p)\\). Compute \\(H\\) for \\(p=0.9\\), and compare the cost with that of an unbiased bit and a known bit."}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § logically reversible If a computation never erases information, does it produce no heat? Not necessarily. A logically reversible map, one that is one-to-one on its allowed states, avoids the cost that erasure forces. A real device may still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, imperfect control, or the speed at which it runs. Conversely, can a gate that loses information be made reversible by operating it slowly? No. Running AND slowly does not make its output reveal its input. Reversibility is a property of the map, not of how the map is carried out. Two questions must therefore be kept apart: whether the mathematical map can be inverted, and how much energy its physical realization uses. For quantum computation the first question is decisive, because the operations of an isolated quantum system can always be inverted. A reversible truth table settles whether the input is recoverable. It does not specify the energy dissipated by a particular physical device. Problems Which of these are logically reversible: NOT; AND; \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); a reset to \\(0\\)? A device implements NOT but dissipates \\(10^{-18}\\) J per operation, while the Landauer bound at \\(300\\) K is about \\(2.9\\times10^{-21}\\) J. Does the dissipation contradict the reversibility of NOT? Does Landauer’s principle require any dissipation for NOT? Show that a reversible map on \\(n\\)-bit strings is a permutation of the \\(2^n\\) strings. For \\(n=2\\), count the reversible maps and compare with the number of all maps from two bits to two bits."}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § target bit Can every function be computed without erasing information, even one such as AND that merges its inputs? It can, provided we keep the input. Add a target bit \\(t\\) and replace \\(f\\) by the map \\[U_f:(x,t)\\mapsto(x,\\,t\\oplus f(x)).\\] The input \\(x\\) is kept, and \\(f(x)\\) is added to the target modulo two. With \\(t=0\\), the target becomes the answer \\(f(x)\\). Why is this map reversible? Apply it twice: \\(t\\oplus f(x)\\oplus f(x)=t\\), because adding the same bit twice modulo two gives zero. The map is its own inverse. For AND it is \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\): \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\(000\\) \\(000\\) \\(001\\) \\(001\\) \\(010\\) \\(010\\) \\(011\\) \\(011\\) \\(100\\) \\(100\\) \\(101\\) \\(101\\) \\(110\\) \\(111\\) \\(111\\) \\(110\\) Has AND become invertible? No: \\(f\\) itself is unchanged and still merges its inputs. What is invertible is the larger map, whose output keeps \\(x\\). The extra bits are not mere bookkeeping; they are what makes the transformation one-to-one. Configurations are written \\(xt\\), here with \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\). \\(U_f\\) flips the target exactly when \\(f(x)=1\\), and applying it twice returns every configuration. Problems Write the outputs of \\(U_f:(x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\) for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\). For OR, apply \\(U_f\\) to \\((1,0,1)\\), then apply it again. A programmer expected the target to contain \\(f(1,0)=1\\) after the first call. What assumption did they overlook? For a function \\(f\\) from \\(n\\)-bit strings to \\(m\\)-bit strings, define \\(U_f(x,t)=(x,t\\oplus f(x))\\), where \\(t\\) has \\(m\\) bits and \\(\\oplus\\) acts bit by bit. Prove that \\(U_f\\) is its own inverse for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § control · target · CNOT How can a gate act on one bit depending on another without losing anything? Keep the deciding bit unchanged, and let it choose a reversible update of the other bit. The kept bit is the control ; the updated bit is the target . The simplest example is controlled-NOT, or CNOT : \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a,b\\oplus a)\\). When the control \\(a\\) is \\(0\\), the target is unchanged; when \\(a\\) is \\(1\\), the target flips. So \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). Applying CNOT twice restores both bits. Why does this work? The control survives the gate, so we know which update was applied, and each update, doing nothing or flipping, is its own inverse. Would it be enough to make the target update reversible but discard the control? No: without the control we would not know whether to flip the target back. CNOT keeps the control, the first bit, and flips the target when the control is \\(1\\): \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) exchange. Exactly one arrow enters each configuration, so the step can be undone. Problems Apply CNOT with the second bit as control and the first as target to \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Apply three CNOT gates in turn to \\((a,b)\\): control the first bit and target the second, then control the second and target the first, then control the first and target the second. Show that the result is \\((b,a)\\). A controlled operation keeps a control bit \\(c\\) and applies a map \\(g_c\\) to the target. Prove that it is reversible when \\(g_0\\) and \\(g_1\\) are both reversible, and give its inverse. Show by an example that it is not reversible if \\(g_1\\) is a reset."}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Toffoli · Fredkin NOT and CNOT only produce XOR combinations of input bits, possibly with a constant added. They cannot produce the product \\(ab\\). To obtain a reversible AND, give the target two controls: \\[\\operatorname{Toffoli}(a,b,c)=(a,b,c\\oplus ab).\\] With \\(c=0\\), the target becomes \\(ab\\), while both inputs survive. Fixing one control to one gives CNOT; fixing both to one gives NOT. Together with clean workspace, these operations let us reversibly implement any Boolean circuit. This is a construction claim, not a promise that every circuit is small. Fredkin uses a different operation: it swaps two targets when its control is one. On \\((a,b,0)\\), the last target becomes \\(ab\\); with targets \\((0,1)\\), its outputs include \\(a\\) and \\(\\neg a\\). These constructions also provide universal Boolean computation with suitable constant bits and workspace. In the drawings, a filled dot is a control on one, a circled plus is an XOR target, and a vertical connector makes them parts of the same gate. Toffoli and Fredkin are both their own inverses. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right. Problems Apply the Toffoli gate to all eight inputs \\((a,b,c)\\). Which inputs change? Using Toffoli gates and extra bits set to fixed values, compute \\(a\\land b\\), \\(\\neg a\\), and \\(a\\oplus b\\). Specify the inputs of each gate. Prove that every output bit of a circuit of CNOT and NOT gates is a sum modulo two of input bits, possibly plus \\(1\\). Conclude that such circuits cannot compute \\(a\\land b\\)."}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § workspace · uncomputation · garbage Toffoli gates compute every function reversibly, but a longer computation produces intermediate results along the way. What becomes of them? Compute the AND of three bits \\(x,y,z\\) with Toffoli gates, using two extra bits that start at \\(0\\): a workspace bit \\(w\\) and an output bit \\(t\\). The first Toffoli gate writes \\(w=xy\\); the second, with controls \\(w\\) and \\(z\\), writes \\(t=xyz\\): \\[(x,y,z,0,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,xyz).\\] The answer is in \\(t\\), but \\(w\\) still holds \\(xy\\). What should we do with it? Resetting \\(w\\) to \\(0\\) erases it, with the cost of erasure. Leaving it means that the workspace keeps a record of the computation’s history, and every further computation adds more. There is a third option: apply the first Toffoli gate again. It is its own inverse, so it returns \\(w\\) to \\(xy\\oplus xy=0\\): \\[(x,y,z,xy,xyz)\\to(x,y,z,0,xyz).\\] This is uncomputation . In general, a reversible computation of \\(f\\) produces its answer together with intermediate values \\(g(x)\\), often called garbage. Copy the answer into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0;0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] Only the input and the copied answer remain. Why go to this trouble? Classically, it avoids the cost of erasure. Quantum computation adds a stronger reason: leftover records of a computation’s history prevent the interference on which quantum algorithms depend. Compute the answer, copy its value to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. \\(A\\) may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown. Predict first. A circuit has g NAND gates. How many extra bits does its Toffoli version need, and what do they hold after the conversion? What do they hold after the answer is copied and the gates are run backward? From NAND gates to Toffoli gates New random circuit Next step Run Reset Check all inputs Inputs (click to flip): With scripting enabled, this figure converts a NAND circuit gate by gate into Toffoli gates: each \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\) becomes \\(\\operatorname{Toffoli}(a,b,1)=(a,b,1\\oplus ab)\\). Each NAND gate becomes a Toffoli gate whose target starts at \\(1\\), because \\(1\\oplus ab=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\). Thick teal wires carry \\(1\\) and thin grey wires carry \\(0\\). The example circuit is the four-gate XOR of Section 2.2.2. After the conversion, a CNOT copies the answer and the Toffoli gates run in reverse: this is the uncomputation described above, applied to every intermediate result. After trying it: compare your explanation One fresh target set to 1 for each NAND gate, so g extra bits, plus one bit set to 0 for the copied answer. After the conversion the targets hold every intermediate NAND value, which is garbage. Because each Toffoli gate is its own inverse, running them backward returns every target to 1, so only the inputs and the copied answer remain. Problems In the three-bit AND computation with workspace \\(w\\) and output \\(t\\), list the five bits \\((x,y,z,w,t)\\) after each of the three Toffoli gates for the input \\((1,1,0)\\). Compute the AND of four bits \\(x,y,z,u\\) with Toffoli gates, two workspace bits that start at \\(0\\), and one output bit, and return the workspace to \\(0\\). List the gates in order. A reversible computation \\(A\\) maps \\((x,0,0)\\) to \\((x,f(x),g(x))\\). Show that applying \\(A\\), then copying the answer register into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then applying \\(A^{-1}\\), produces \\((x,0,0;f(x))\\) for every input \\(x\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\lor y,\\;x\\land y)\\) reversible? No. The inputs \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) both give \\((1,0)\\). Two output bits are not enough when the map merges inputs."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Write the binary-field polynomial of each function and check it on all four inputs: (a) \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(x,y)\\); (b) the “equal” function, which is \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x=y\\). (a) \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(x,y)=1+xy\\). On \\(00,01,10\\) the product is \\(0\\), giving \\(1\\); on \\(11\\) it gives \\(1+1=0\\). (b) “Equal” is \\(1+x+y\\): on \\(00\\) it is \\(1\\), on \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) it is \\(1+1=0\\), and on \\(11\\) it is \\(1+1+1=1\\). Both agree with the truth tables. Negating a function always means adding \\(1\\) modulo two, because \\(1+0=1\\) and \\(1+1=0\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "Classical circuits copy bits freely by fan-out. Show that a CNOT whose target starts at \\(0\\) copies its control bit, and that the copying map \\((a,0)\\mapsto(a,a)\\) is part of a reversible map. What does a Toffoli gate do to \\((a,1,0)\\)? CNOT gives \\((a,0)\\mapsto(a,0\\oplus a)=(a,a)\\): the target becomes a copy of the control. The full map \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a,b\\oplus a)\\) is reversible, since applying it twice restores both bits. Copying a bit is therefore reversible, provided the copy is written into a bit known to be \\(0\\). A Toffoli gate on \\((a,1,0)\\) gives \\((a,1,0\\oplus a\\cdot1)=(a,1,a)\\), another copy. Reversible fan-out costs a gate and a fresh bit, rather than being free. In quantum computation the same gate copies basis values, but it will not copy an unknown superposition, a distinction Chapter 6 develops."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute the three-bit majority function reversibly: using three Toffoli gates, turn \\((x,y,z,t)\\) into \\((x,y,z,\\,t\\oplus\\mathrm{maj}(x,y,z))\\). Check the construction on the inputs \\(110\\) and \\(100\\) with \\(t=0\\). Apply Toffoli gates with controls \\((x,y)\\), \\((y,z)\\), and \\((x,z)\\), each targeting \\(t\\). Together they add \\(xy\\oplus yz\\oplus xz\\), the polynomial of majority. On \\(110\\): \\(1\\oplus0\\oplus0=1\\). On \\(100\\): \\(0\\oplus0\\oplus0=0\\). The inputs are kept, so the map is reversible; it is its own inverse."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Find the binary-field polynomial of the three-bit function that is \\(1\\) exactly when exactly two of its inputs are \\(1\\). Build it from detectors, simplify, and check it on \\(111\\), \\(110\\), and \\(100\\). Compare it with the majority polynomial \\(xy+yz+xz\\). The detectors are \\((1+x)yz=yz+xyz\\), \\(x(1+y)z=xz+xyz\\), and \\(xy(1+z)=xy+xyz\\). Their sum is \\(xy+yz+xz+3xyz=xy+yz+xz+xyz\\) modulo two. On \\(111\\): \\(1+1+1+1=0\\), correct because three ones is not exactly two. On \\(110\\): \\(1+0+0+0=1\\), correct. On \\(100\\): all terms vanish, correct. The function differs from majority only on \\(111\\), where majority is \\(1\\), and the polynomials differ by exactly the term \\(xyz\\), the detector-like term that is \\(1\\) only on \\(111\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Convert the four-NAND XOR circuit of Section 2.2.2 into a reversible computation that leaves only the inputs and the answer. (a) How many Toffoli gates, CNOT gates, and extra bits does the compute–copy–uncompute construction use? (b) Trace every wire for \\(a=1\\), \\(b=0\\), and confirm that the workspace returns to its starting values. (a) Four Toffoli gates compute \\(t,u,v\\) and the XOR value into four bits that start at \\(1\\). One CNOT copies the answer into a fresh bit that starts at \\(0\\). Four more Toffoli gates, in reverse order, uncompute. That is eight Toffoli gates, one CNOT, and five extra bits. (b) For \\(a=1,b=0\\): \\(t=\\operatorname{NAND}(1,0)=1\\), \\(u=\\operatorname{NAND}(1,1)=0\\), \\(v=\\operatorname{NAND}(0,1)=1\\), and the answer bit is \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(0,1)=1=1\\oplus0\\). The CNOT copies \\(1\\) into the fresh bit. Running the Toffoli gates backward restores the answer bit to \\(1\\), then \\(v=1\\oplus(0\\cdot1)=1\\), \\(u=0\\oplus(1\\cdot1)=1\\), and \\(t=1\\oplus(1\\cdot0)=1\\). All four workspace bits are back at \\(1\\), and only the inputs and the copied answer \\(1\\) remain."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-7", "text": "A half adder adds two bits, producing a sum bit \\(a\\oplus b\\) and a carry bit \\(ab\\). Build the reversible map \\((a,b,0)\\mapsto(a,\\;a\\oplus b,\\;ab)\\) from one Toffoli gate and one CNOT. Show that the order of the two gates matters by computing what the other order produces. Apply the Toffoli gate with controls \\(a,b\\) and target the third bit: \\((a,b,0)\\to(a,b,ab)\\). Then a CNOT from \\(a\\) to the second bit: \\((a,b,ab)\\to(a,a\\oplus b,ab)\\). Check: \\(00\\to(0,0,0)\\), \\(01\\to(0,1,0)\\), \\(10\\to(1,1,0)\\), \\(11\\to(1,0,1)\\), giving sum and carry correctly. In the other order, the CNOT first makes the second bit \\(a\\oplus b\\), and the Toffoli gate then writes \\(a(a\\oplus b)=a(1+b)=a\\land\\lnot b\\) into the third bit. On input \\(11\\) that gives carry \\(0\\), which is wrong. A reversible gate reads its controls at the moment it acts, so the order of gates is part of the circuit’s meaning."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “Uncomputation is just a roundabout way of erasing the workspace, so it must cost at least \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) per cleared bit.” In the three-bit AND example, explain why returning \\(w=xy\\) to \\(0\\) by a second Toffoli gate is not an erasure in Landauer’s sense, and what makes the difference. Landauer’s bound concerns maps that merge distinct states, such as a reset that sends both \\(w=0\\) and \\(w=1\\) to \\(0\\) without keeping the value elsewhere. The second Toffoli gate maps \\((x,y,z,xy,xyz)\\) to \\((x,y,z,0,xyz)\\), and as a map on all five bits it is one-to-one: it is its own inverse. It can clear \\(w\\) only because \\(w\\) is not independent information: it equals \\(xy\\), and \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are still present. Uncomputation removes a redundant copy by undoing the step that made it, so nothing is lost. A reset would destroy the value of \\(w\\) even if \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) had already been discarded."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Build the reversible embedding of OR, \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\), from CNOT and Toffoli gates, and show that the circuit is its own inverse. CNOT from \\(x\\) to \\(t\\), CNOT from \\(y\\) to \\(t\\), and Toffoli from \\((x,y)\\) to \\(t\\) add \\(x\\oplus y\\oplus xy=x\\lor y\\) to \\(t\\). Since none of the gates changes \\(x\\) or \\(y\\), running the circuit twice adds \\(x\\lor y\\) twice, which cancels. The circuit is its own inverse, and with \\(t=0\\) it leaves the answer in \\(t\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-10", "text": "A classical processor runs a computation \\(10^9\\) times per second, and each run leaves \\(1000\\) bits of intermediate values that are reset rather than uncomputed. (a) Using \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\approx2.9\\times10^{-21}\\) J at \\(300\\) K, find the minimum power Landauer’s principle requires for these resets. (b) Compare it with a typical \\(50\\) W processor. (c) If the energy cost is so small, why does this course insist on uncomputation? (a) \\(10^{12}\\) bits per second times \\(2.9\\times10^{-21}\\) J is about \\(2.9\\times10^{-9}\\) W, roughly three nanowatts. (b) That is about \\(10^{10}\\) times smaller than \\(50\\) W. Present-day processors dissipate far more than the Landauer bound for reasons unrelated to logical erasure: resistance, charging wires, and switching speed. (c) The reason for uncomputation here is not energy. In quantum computation, leftover intermediate values are records of the computation’s history, and such records prevent the interference that quantum algorithms rely on. Uncomputation is required for the computation to work at all, whatever it costs in heat."}, {"title": "States", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 States § configuration · state · weight · definite Take a register holding one bit. Its possible contents, \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), are its configurations . At any moment the register holds exactly one of them. Do we always know which? We may have set the bit ourselves, and then we know it. We may know only that either configuration is possible. Or the bit may have been set by a fair coin, so that each configuration has probability \\(1/2\\). What we know about the register at a given time is its state . All three kinds of knowledge take the same form: a number \\(v_j\\) for each configuration \\(j\\), its weight in the state. To keep each weight beside its configuration, we write configuration \\(j\\) as \\(\\ket j\\), read “ket \\(j\\),” and the state as a weighted sum of these kets, \\[v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1.\\] For now this is bookkeeping: each term pairs a configuration with its weight. Terms for different configurations stay separate, while two terms for the same configuration combine by adding their weights, as in \\(\\tfrac14\\ket0+\\tfrac14\\ket0=\\tfrac12\\ket0\\). What we know \\(v_0\\) \\(v_1\\) State The bit is \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(\\ket0\\) Either value is possible \\(1\\) \\(1\\) \\(\\ket0+\\ket1\\) A fair coin set the bit \\(1/2\\) \\(1/2\\) \\(\\tfrac12\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\) The weights mean different things in different rows. A weight \\(1\\) or \\(0\\) records whether a configuration is possible; a probability records how often it occurs. A state with weight \\(1\\) on one configuration and \\(0\\) on the others is definite , and we write it simply as that configuration’s ket, such as \\(\\ket0\\). Each configuration carries a weight. The state is the pair of weights \\(v_0\\) and \\(v_1\\). Problems A two-bit register has configurations \\(00,01,10,11\\). Write the weights of its state when (a) the register is known to hold \\(10\\); (b) the first bit is known to be \\(1\\) and the second could be either; (c) both bits were set by independent fair coins. Someone describes a bit by the weights \\(v_0=v_1=1\\). Can these weights be probabilities? What do they say about the bit, and what do they leave unsaid? Give two different probability states that are consistent with them. A register has \\(k\\) bits. A possibility state gives each configuration weight \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), with at least one weight \\(1\\). How many possibility states are there? How many probability states are there? Which probability states are consistent with the possibility state in which every configuration is possible?"}, {"title": "Transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Transitions § transition rule · state diagram · weight · run · time-expanded diagram · path A computation changes the register step by step. A transition rule says which configurations may follow each one. As an example, take NOT, which flips the bit. We draw a rule as a state diagram : a circle for each configuration, and an arrow \\(i\\to j\\) whenever the rule lets \\(j\\) follow \\(i\\). Each arrow carries a weight . For a step \\(S\\), we write the weight of the arrow from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) as \\[\\bra jS\\ket i,\\] with the final configuration on the left, the step in the middle, and the initial configuration on the right. Read it from right to left, in the order things happen: start at \\(i\\), apply \\(S\\), arrive at \\(j\\). A missing arrow has weight zero. Writing \\(X\\) for NOT, \\(\\bra1X\\ket0=\\bra0X\\ket1=1\\), because the step certainly follows those arrows, while \\(\\bra0X\\ket0=\\bra1X\\ket1=0\\). Applying a rule step by step produces a run \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\), where \\(c_t\\) is the configuration after \\(t\\) steps. From \\(0\\), NOT gives \\(0,1,0\\). To see a run in a diagram, copy the configurations once for each time \\(t=0,1,2\\) and join the copies by the arrows of the rule. In this time-expanded diagram , a run is a path from time \\(0\\) to time \\(2\\). Rules differ in how many arrows leave each configuration and in what their weights mean. These two differences separate the three kinds of rule that follow. NOT: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The only path is \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). Problems A rule on the configurations \\(0,1,2\\) sends \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to2\\), and \\(2\\to0\\). Draw its state diagram and write the run from \\(0\\) for four steps. For the same rule, draw the time-expanded diagram for the times \\(t=0,1,2\\). How many arrows does it contain, and which path is the run from \\(2\\)? A rule on \\(m\\) configurations has \\(a\\) arrows in its state diagram. How many arrows does its time-expanded diagram have for the times \\(0,1,\\dots,t\\)? Show that the runs of \\(t\\) steps are exactly the paths from time \\(0\\) to time \\(t\\), and explain why the time-expanded diagram never contains a cycle, even when the state diagram does."}, {"title": "Deterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Deterministic transitions § deterministic · reversible Compare NOT with RESET, which writes \\(0\\) whatever the bit holds. Configuration Next under NOT Next under RESET \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) From \\(0\\), NOT gives the run \\(0,1,0\\) and RESET gives \\(0,0,0\\). Under both rules, exactly one arrow leaves each configuration: for each \\(i\\), one weight \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\) is \\(1\\) and the rest are \\(0\\). The start therefore fixes the whole run, and both rules are deterministic . From a definite state, the state stays definite at every step. Can a run also be traced backward? Under NOT, exactly one arrow enters each configuration, so the previous bit is always known. Under RESET, two arrows enter \\(0\\), and a run that reaches \\(0\\) may have come from either bit. A deterministic rule with exactly one arrow entering each configuration is reversible . RESET sends both configurations to \\(0\\). A run that reaches \\(0\\) does not record where it started, so RESET is deterministic but not reversible. Problems On two bits, the rule SWAP exchanges the bits: \\(01\\to10\\) and \\(10\\to01\\), while \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) stay as they are. Write the two-step run from \\(01\\). Is SWAP deterministic? Is it reversible? On the configurations \\(0,1,2,3\\), the rule INC adds \\(1\\) modulo \\(4\\), and the rule HALVE replaces \\(n\\) by \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), the integer part of \\(n/2\\). Draw both state diagrams, write the two-step runs from \\(3\\), and decide which rule is reversible. Prove that a deterministic rule on finitely many configurations is reversible exactly when every configuration has at least one incoming arrow. Then show that the statement fails for the rule \\(n\\mapsto\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) on all nonnegative integers."}, {"title": "Nondeterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-4", "text": "3.1.4 Nondeterministic transitions § nondeterministic · reachable Nondeterminism relaxes the requirement of a unique successor. A configuration may have several permitted successors, so specifying the present no longer specifies a unique future. The rule describes a set of possible runs rather than a single run. For a bit, consider KEEP-OR-FLIP: from either configuration, both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are permitted. Its state diagram has all four arrows. Starting from \\(0\\), two steps admit four paths: \\[0\\to0\\to0,\\qquad0\\to1\\to0,\\qquad0\\to0\\to1,\\qquad0\\to1\\to1.\\] A path is possible when every transition along it is permitted. A configuration is reachable when at least one possible path ends there. Both zero and one are therefore reachable after two steps. In a nondeterministic model of computation, acceptance is defined in the same way: an input is accepted if at least one permitted run accepts it. This is a statement about possibility, not a mechanism for choosing among paths. Even if more paths reached one endpoint than another, the rule would not make that endpoint more likely. Counting possibilities and assigning probabilities are different tasks. To predict how often a future occurs, we need more information on the arrows. KEEP-OR-FLIP: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Four paths are possible; both configurations are reachable at time \\(2\\). Problems A rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to0\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(0\\to0\\). List the possible two-step runs from \\(0\\), and the configurations reachable at time \\(2\\). For the same rule, list the possible three-step runs from \\(0\\). Which configurations are reachable at time \\(3\\), and by how many paths? On the configurations \\(0,1,\\dots,k\\), a rule allows \\(i\\to i\\) and \\(i\\to i+1\\) for \\(i\\lt k\\), and only \\(k\\to k\\) at \\(k\\). Show that \\(k\\) is reachable from \\(0\\) at time \\(t\\) exactly when \\(t\\geq k\\), and that the number of paths from \\(0\\) to \\(k\\) at time \\(t\\) is \\(\\binom tk\\)."}, {"title": "Probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-5", "text": "3.1.5 Probabilistic transitions § probabilistic · conditional probability · Markov A probabilistic rule supplies the information that nondeterminism leaves unspecified: the probability of each next configuration, given the present one. The weight of the arrow from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) is now the probability of \\(j\\) given \\(i\\), written \\(P(j\\mid i)\\): \\(\\bra jS\\ket i=P(j\\mid i)\\). Since some next configuration must occur, the weights leaving each configuration add to one, \\(\\sum_j\\bra jS\\ket i=1\\). The current configuration is assumed to contain all the information needed for this prediction. If earlier history matters, that history must be included in the configuration. With this assumption, the process is called Markov . Consider COIN: whatever the bit currently holds, replace it by the result of a fresh fair coin flip. Its permitted arrows are the same as those of KEEP-OR-FLIP, but each now carries probability \\(1/2\\): \\(\\bra j{\\mathrm{COIN}}\\ket i=1/2\\) for every \\(i\\) and \\(j\\). For the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\), the first step has probability \\(1/2\\), and the second has conditional probability \\(1/2\\) given the intermediate configuration. The path probability is therefore \\(1/4\\). The other path to zero, \\(0\\to0\\to0\\), also has probability \\(1/4\\). These are mutually exclusive histories, so their probabilities add: \\[P(c_2=0)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12,\\] where \\(c_2\\) is the configuration after two steps. The probability of ending at one is also \\(1/2\\). Unlike the deterministic rule, COIN does not fix the outcome of a single run. Unlike the nondeterministic rule, it predicts the distribution of outcomes over independently repeated runs. COIN: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Each path has probability \\(1/4\\); two paths end at each configuration. Problems A step keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\). Starting from \\(0\\), compute \\(P(c_2=0)\\) by listing the paths that end at \\(0\\). For the same rule, compute \\(P(c_3=0)\\) from \\(0\\). Check your answer against the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\). Prove the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\) for the rule above, and describe what happens to the run’s memory of its starting configuration as \\(t\\) grows."}, {"title": "The product rule along a path", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 The product rule along a path § product rule A path specifies every intermediate configuration, so following it requires every transition along it. Suppose a step \\(S\\) is followed by a step \\(T\\). The path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\) uses the arrow from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) under \\(S\\), then the arrow from \\(j\\) to \\(k\\) under \\(T\\). Its weight is the product \\[\\bra kT\\ket j\\,\\bra jS\\ket i.\\] Read from right to left, the factors appear in the order the steps happen: the first step stands next to the initial configuration. When the same rule acts twice, \\(T=S\\). For deterministic and nondeterministic rules, let an allowed arrow have weight one and a missing arrow weight zero. The product is one precisely when the whole path is permitted: a single missing transition makes it zero. For a probabilistic rule, use the transition probabilities as weights. Multiplication then gives the probability of that particular history. The factors are conditional probabilities for successive steps; they need not describe independent events. Their dependence on the past is already accounted for by the current configuration. For COIN, each two-step path has weight \\((1/2)(1/2)=1/4\\). A path of \\(t\\) steps has weight \\(2^{-t}\\). This determines the contribution of one history. An endpoint may be reached through several histories, whose contributions must still be combined. The weight of the path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\), with step \\(S\\) and then step \\(T\\), is the product of its two arrow weights. Problems Under the rule that keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\), what is the weight of the path \\(0\\to0\\to1\\to1\\)? A nondeterministic rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to0\\), \\(0\\to1\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(1\\to0\\). Using the product rule with weights \\(1\\) and \\(0\\), decide which of the eight three-step paths from \\(0\\) are possible. The product rule for probabilities assumes the Markov property. Find a two-step random process on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) in which the probability of the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\), given the start \\(0\\), differs from \\(P(c_1=1\\mid c_0=0)\\,P(c_2=0\\mid c_1=1)\\)."}, {"title": "The sum rule across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 The sum rule across paths § sum rule An endpoint specifies where the run finishes without specifying how it got there. To calculate its weight, we therefore sum the contributions of all paths that reach it. For two steps from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\), the paths differ only in the intermediate configuration \\(j\\): \\[\\bra kTS\\ket i=\\sum_j\\bra kT\\ket j\\,\\bra jS\\ket i.\\] Each term accounts for one route; the sum accounts for all routes to the chosen endpoint. The left side treats the two steps together as one combined step \\(TS\\), first \\(S\\) and then \\(T\\), with this total as the weight of its arrow from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\). As before, the step applied first stands on the right. For a deterministic rule, only one path from the start is permitted. The sum is one at its endpoint and zero elsewhere. For a nondeterministic rule, the sum counts permitted paths. A positive count establishes reachability, but is not a probability. For a probabilistic rule, the histories are mutually exclusive, so the sum is the endpoint probability. Writing \\(P_2(k\\mid i)=\\bra kSS\\ket i\\) for the two-step probability, we have \\[P_2(k\\mid i)=\\sum_jP(k\\mid j)P(j\\mid i).\\] For COIN, this is \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) at either endpoint. In all three models, every contribution is nonnegative. Adding a permitted path can therefore increase an endpoint’s total weight or leave it unchanged, but cannot reduce it. This restriction will matter when we encounter quantum transitions. Each route passes through an intermediate configuration \\(j\\). Multiply along each route, then sum over all intermediate configurations; the dots stand for any additional routes. Problems Under KEEP-OR-FLIP, how many possible paths lead from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) in three steps, and what does the count tell us? A rule has \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\). Use the sum rule to compute the four two-step weights \\(\\bra kSS\\ket i\\), and check that the weights from each start add to \\(1\\). Prove that, for any probabilistic rule, the two-step weights \\(\\bra kSS\\ket i\\) given by the sum and product rules add to \\(1\\) over \\(k\\). What does this show about composing two probabilistic steps?"}, {"title": "States and steps", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 States and steps § So far each calculation began from a definite state. The same rules apply to any state \\(\\sum_iv_i\\ket i\\), with weight \\(v_i\\) on each configuration \\(i\\). During a step, the contribution from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) is \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i\\). Summing over all starting configurations gives the new weight at \\(j\\): \\[v^\\prime_j=\\sum_i\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i.\\] Kets record the same calculation. A step sends a definite configuration to the weighted sum of its successors, \\[S\\ket i=\\sum_j\\bra jS\\ket i\\,\\ket j,\\] so that, for example, \\(\\mathrm{COIN}\\ket0=\\tfrac12\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\). A general state goes to \\(\\sum_iv_i\\,S\\ket i\\): each configuration’s successors are weighted by \\(v_i\\), and terms that land on the same configuration add. That addition is the sum rule; collecting the weight of \\(\\ket j\\) gives exactly \\(v'_j\\). The state thus collects the information needed to predict the next step, without retaining a separate list of all earlier paths. For COIN, \\(v^\\prime_0=v^\\prime_1=(v_0+v_1)/2=1/2\\). Every initial probability distribution becomes the same fair distribution. Whether a step can be undone depends on what this update preserves. A reversible deterministic rule permutes the configurations; reversing its arrows restores every initial state. CNOT is an example on two bits: \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{00}=\\ket{00}\\), \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{01}=\\ket{01}\\), \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{10}=\\ket{11}\\), and \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{11}=\\ket{10}\\). RESET and COIN instead send distinct initial states to the same final state. No rule acting only on that final state can recover which preparation was used. To undo such a process, we would need additional information outside this description. Each initial weight \\(v_i\\) contributes \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i\\) to the new weight at \\(j\\). The new state contains one such sum for every output configuration. Problems Apply one step of COIN, and separately one step of NOT, to the state with weights \\(0.9\\) on \\(0\\) and \\(0.1\\) on \\(1\\). For the rule with \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\), find the distribution that one step leaves unchanged. Prove that a probabilistic rule on finitely many configurations can be undone by another probabilistic rule only if it is deterministic and reversible."}, {"title": "Negative weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 Negative weights § signed contribution · product rule So far, weights have counted paths or described probabilities. In either case they were nonnegative. Let us keep the same sum and product rules, but allow a state or an arrow to carry a negative real weight. This is a mathematical extension of our path description; its physical interpretation will come later. The product rule still assigns a path the product of its arrow weights. For a path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\) under a step \\(S\\) applied twice, the weight is \\[\\bra kS\\ket j\\,\\bra jS\\ket i.\\] The sign now matters. A path with an odd number of negative arrow weights has negative weight; a path with an even number has positive weight. For example, \\((1/2)(-1/2)=-1/4\\), while \\((-1/2)(-1/2)=1/4\\). A negative arrow can therefore reverse the sign of a contribution that was already negative. If the starting configuration itself has weight \\(v_i\\), its contribution along this path is \\(\\bra kS\\ket j\\,\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i\\). We include the sign of \\(v_i\\) as well as those of the arrows. A negative path weight is not a negative probability or a negative number of paths. It is a signed contribution to a later sum. The path remains present in the diagram; what changes is how its contribution combines with others. Each row is a path through configurations \\(i\\), \\(j\\), and \\(k\\), with transition weights on the arrows. One negative arrow gives path weight \\(-1/4\\); two negative arrows give path weight \\(+1/4\\). The rows are separate examples. Problems A path has arrow weights \\(-1/2\\), \\(1/3\\), and \\(-3/4\\). What is its weight? A starting configuration has weight \\(-2\\). A path from it has arrow weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/3\\). What contribution reaches the endpoint? If the sign of one nonzero arrow weight on a path is reversed, how does the path weight change? What if two arrow occurrences change sign?"}, {"title": "Cancellation across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 Cancellation across paths § sum rule · cancellation The sum rule still adds the contributions of all paths with the same start and endpoint. With two intermediate configurations, \\[\\bra kSS\\ket i=\\bra kS\\ket{j_1}\\,\\bra{j_1}S\\ket i+\\bra kS\\ket{j_2}\\,\\bra{j_2}S\\ket i.\\] Write the first pair of arrow weights as \\(a,c\\) and the second as \\(b,d\\). The two path contributions are \\(ca\\) and \\(db\\), and the total is \\(ca+db\\). If \\(a=b=c=1/2\\) and \\(d=-1/2\\), then \\[\\bra kSS\\ket i=\\frac14-\\frac14=0.\\] Both paths have nonzero weight, yet their combined contribution vanishes. This is cancellation . With nonnegative weights, a sum can vanish only when every term vanishes. Signed weights allow a zero total even when individual terms are nonzero. Cancellation need not be complete: \\(1/4-1/8=1/8\\). Contributions with the same sign reinforce one another: \\(-1/4-1/4=-1/2\\). Adding a path can now decrease the total weight, and removing a negative contribution can increase it. The sum of the weights is no longer required to be one or even positive. Predict first. With \\(a=b=c=1/2\\), predict \\(W(i\\to k)\\) for \\(d=-1/2\\), \\(d=-1/4\\), and \\(d=1/2\\). For which values of \\(d\\) does removing the second path increase the total? Two paths that can cancel Weight \\(d\\) −1/2 Include the path through \\(j_2\\) Reset Path 1 runs through \\(j_1\\) with weights \\(a\\) and \\(c\\); path 2 runs through \\(j_2\\) with weights \\(b\\) and \\(d\\). Here \\(a=b=c=1/2\\). Line thickness shows the size of each path’s contribution, and dashed orange marks a negative one. On the number line the second contribution starts where the first ends, so the marker shows the total \\(W(i\\to k)\\). After trying it: compare your explanation The totals are \\(0\\), \\(1/8\\), and \\(1/2\\). Removing the second path leaves \\(ca=1/4\\), which exceeds the total exactly when \\(d\\) is negative, because then \\(db<0\\). Only contributions to the same final configuration are added. A weight \\(+1/4\\) at configuration \\(0\\) and a weight \\(-1/4\\) at configuration \\(1\\) remain two separate entries. Their sum is zero, but neither entry has vanished. In ket notation, \\(\\tfrac14\\ket0-\\tfrac14\\ket0=0\\ket0\\), whereas \\(\\tfrac14\\ket0-\\tfrac14\\ket1\\) keeps both terms. For state weights, the update therefore keeps exactly its earlier form: \\[v'_j=\\sum_i\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i.\\] Multiplication carries each signed contribution forward; addition combines contributions at their common endpoint. These two rules now describe cancellation as well as accumulation. No rule for converting weights to observed probabilities has yet been specified. Problems Three paths to the same endpoint contribute \\(1/3\\), \\(-1/2\\), and \\(1/6\\). What is the total? Does it imply that no path reaches the endpoint? In the two-path diagram, set \\(a=b=c=1/2\\). Find the total at \\(k\\) for \\(d=1/2\\), \\(d=-1/4\\), and \\(d=-1/2\\). Two paths to \\(0\\) contribute \\(1/4\\) each, and two paths to \\(1\\) contribute \\(-1/4\\) each. What are the final state weights? Can any contributions cancel?"}, {"title": "The Hadamard step", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 The Hadamard step § Hadamard operation We can use cancellation to construct a step that spreads a definite input over two configurations, yet undoes itself when repeated. Call the step \\(H\\). Give three arrows weight \\(c\\) and the remaining arrow weight \\(-c\\): \\[\\bra0H\\ket0=\\bra1H\\ket0=\\bra0H\\ket1=c,\\qquad \\bra1H\\ket1=-c.\\] Start at \\(0\\) with weight one. After one step the state is \\(H\\ket0=c\\ket0+c\\ket1\\). After two steps, the paths \\(0\\to0\\to0\\) and \\(0\\to1\\to0\\) each contribute \\(c^2\\), giving \\(2c^2\\) at \\(0\\). The paths \\(0\\to0\\to1\\) and \\(0\\to1\\to1\\) contribute \\(c^2\\) and \\(-c^2\\), giving zero at \\(1\\). The signed step with weights \\(c\\) and \\(-c\\): the two paths to \\(0\\) add to \\(2c^2\\), while the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. To restore the starting weight one, require \\(2c^2=1\\). To recover the original weight one, choose \\(2c^2=1\\), or \\(c=1/\\sqrt2\\). This gives the Hadamard operation : \\[\\bra0H\\ket0=\\bra1H\\ket0=\\bra0H\\ket1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\qquad \\bra1H\\ket1=-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}.\\] In ket form, the step sends each configuration to \\[H\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H\\ket1=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Each arrow leaving an input becomes one term of its equation; the dashed arrow carries the negative amplitude. Each term records one arrow: its coefficient is the arrow weight \\(\\bra jH\\ket i\\). The minus sign before \\(\\ket1\\) in \\(H\\ket1\\) is the negative arrow, and it will matter when a later step adds contributions that reach the same configuration. The factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) has been fixed by composition: two applications must return the original weights. Starting at \\(1\\) provides the other check. The paths to \\(0\\) cancel; the paths to \\(1\\) reinforce, including the path with two negative arrows, whose product is positive. Thus \\(\\bra kHH\\ket i\\) is one when \\(k=i\\) and zero otherwise: \\(HH=I\\), where \\(I\\) is the identity step, whose only arrows are self-loops of weight one, \\(\\bra jI\\ket i=1\\) for \\(j=i\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. Notice what this choice does to the weights after one step. From either definite input, their squares add to one: \\[c^2+c^2=c^2+(-c)^2=1.\\] The weights themselves may be negative, and their sum need not be one. Their squares, however, are nonnegative and here give \\(1/2\\) for each configuration. This suggests using squared weights as probabilities, while retaining the signed weights for composition and cancellation. It is a motivation for the quantum probability rule, rather than a derivation of it; we state that rule when we connect these transitions to physical outcomes. For a general input state, the update is \\[H(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1)=\\frac{v_0+v_1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0+\\frac{v_0-v_1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket1.\\] Applying the same update again gives back \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\). The intermediate weights retain enough information to recover the input: \\(H\\ket0\\) and \\(H\\ket1\\) differ only in the sign in front of \\(\\ket1\\), and that sign determines which endpoint survives the second step. If we replace the negative arrow by a positive one, the two contributions to each endpoint reinforce. Both inputs then produce the same intermediate weights, and the second step cannot recover the starting configuration. Cancellation is what makes the signed step reversible. Negative real weights have given us opposite signs and cancellation. Next we extend weights to complex numbers, where a continuously varying phase takes the place of this two-way choice of sign, and connect the resulting rules to quantum transitions. \\(H\\): state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The paths to \\(0\\) add to weight \\(1\\); the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to the state weights \\((1,-1)/\\sqrt2\\). Draw the two-step paths from \\(1\\) under \\(H\\). Compute the contribution of each path and the final weights. Replace the negative arrow of \\(H\\) by \\(+1/\\sqrt2\\). Starting at \\(0\\), find the weights after two steps. Explain why this modified step cannot be reversible."}, {"title": "Complex weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Complex weights § amplitude · conjugate · squared magnitude · phase Complex weights associated with states and transitions are called amplitudes . They obey the same sum and product rules as real weights. The Hadamard operation satisfies \\(H^2=I\\). A square root of NOT is an operation \\(\\sqrt X\\) satisfying \\(\\sqrt X\\sqrt X=X\\), where \\(X\\) denotes NOT. Neither deterministic reversible operation on one bit has this property: both the identity and NOT square to the identity. Let \\(\\sqrt X\\) have stay-weight \\(a=\\bra0\\sqrt X\\ket0=\\bra1\\sqrt X\\ket1\\) and switch-weight \\(b=\\bra1\\sqrt X\\ket0=\\bra0\\sqrt X\\ket1\\), the same from either configuration. In two steps, staying twice or switching twice returns to the initial configuration, with total weight \\(a^2+b^2\\). Staying then switching or switching then staying reaches the opposite configuration, with total weight \\(ab+ba=2ab\\). For the combined operation to be NOT, \\[a^2+b^2=0,\\qquad2ab=1.\\] The step \\(S\\) has stay-weight \\(a\\) and switch-weight \\(b\\). In two steps from \\(0\\),\n                        the paths returning to \\(0\\) contribute \\(a^2+b^2\\); the paths reaching \\(1\\) contribute\n                        \\(ab+ba=2ab\\). To obtain NOT, these totals must be zero and one, respectively. For real \\(a,b\\), the first equation forces both to vanish, contradicting the second. Complex numbers\n                    permit cancellation between the squares themselves. With \\(i^2=-1\\), choose \\[a=\\frac{1+i}{2},\\qquad b=\\frac{1-i}{2}.\\] Then \\(a^2=i/2\\), \\(b^2=-i/2\\), and \\(2ab=1\\). The two-step transformation is exactly NOT. The weights of \\(\\sqrt X\\) now give the desired composition, but we still need to associate them with probabilities. In the real-weight example, squaring gave a nonnegative number. Here the squares \\(a^2=i/2\\) and \\(b^2=-i/2\\) cannot serve that purpose. Multiplying a complex weight by its conjugate offers a natural extension: if \\(z=u+iv\\), its conjugate is \\(z^*=u-iv\\), and \\[zz^*=(u+iv)(u-iv)=u^2+v^2=|z|^2.\\] This is the squared magnitude of \\(z\\). It is real and nonnegative, and becomes the ordinary square when the weight is real. We can therefore propose that a configuration with amplitude \\(v_j\\) has probability \\[p(j)=v_jv_j^*=|v_j|^2.\\] With this assignment, the requirement that probabilities sum to one becomes a condition on the amplitudes: \\[\\sum_jp(j)=1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_j|v_j|^2=1.\\] We call this normalization . For the two outgoing weights of \\(\\sqrt X\\), \\(|a|^2=|b|^2=1/2\\), so their probabilities do sum to one. The same assignment also gives the probabilities \\(1/2,1/2\\) for a Hadamard step. Thus it agrees with the real-weight example and accommodates the complex weights just constructed. A computation involves more than one step, however. The probability assignment must remain consistent as weights multiply along paths and add at their endpoints. Multiplication has a useful property here: \\[|z_1z_2|^2=(z_1z_2)(z_1z_2)^*=|z_1|^2|z_2|^2.\\] The squared magnitude of a path product is the product of the squared magnitudes of its factors. When several paths meet, their amplitudes must still be added before assigning a probability. For two contributions, \\[|A+B|^2=|A|^2+|B|^2+AB^*+A^*B.\\] The last two terms retain the dependence on how the contributions combine. In two steps of \\(\\sqrt X\\), the return contributions are \\(A=i/2\\) and \\(B=-i/2\\); their sum is zero, and so is the return probability. Assigning separate probabilities and adding them would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\), losing the cancellation. The distinction is therefore between adding amplitudes at a common endpoint and adding probabilities over distinct final configurations. This cancellation changes the probability of a particular configuration. The total over all final configurations must nevertheless remain one. That places a restriction on the transition weights. If the input amplitudes are \\(v_i\\), the sum and product rules give, for a step \\(S\\), \\[v'_j=\\sum_i\\bra jS\\ket i\\,v_i.\\] For a definite input \\(i\\), only \\(v_i=1\\) is nonzero, so the outgoing amplitudes are simply \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\). Normalization then requires \\[\\sum_j|\\bra jS\\ket i|^2=1\\] for each input configuration. This checks the transition on definite inputs. With several nonzero input amplitudes, contributions from different starting configurations combine at each endpoint, producing the same kind of cross terms as \\(A+B\\) above. Summing over endpoints gives \\[\\sum_j|v'_j|^2=\\sum_i|v_i|^2\\sum_j|\\bra jS\\ket i|^2+\\sum_{i\\ne k}v_i^*v_k\\sum_j\\bigl(\\bra jS\\ket i\\bigr)^*\\bra jS\\ket k.\\] The outgoing normalization condition makes the first term equal to the original total \\(\\sum_i|v_i|^2\\). The second term must therefore vanish for every choice of input amplitudes. Since their relative magnitudes and complex directions can vary, this requires \\[\\sum_j\\bigl(\\bra jS\\ket i\\bigr)^*\\bra jS\\ket k=0\\qquad(i\\ne k).\\] This cross-sum condition allows contributions to reinforce or cancel at individual endpoints while ensuring that their cross terms cancel in the total. Together with outgoing normalization, it makes the proposed probability assignment compatible with the amplitude update. Each step takes a normalized input to a normalized output, so a sequence of such steps preserves total probability. The step \\(\\sqrt X\\) satisfies both requirements. Its outgoing squared magnitudes sum to \\(|a|^2+|b|^2=1\\), and the cross sum for its two inputs is \\[a^*b+b^*a=-\\frac i2+\\frac i2=0.\\] It therefore preserves normalization for arbitrary input amplitudes, as well as for the definite inputs used in its construction. After two steps, the return amplitude \\(a^2+b^2=0\\) gives probability zero, and the switch amplitude \\(2ab=1\\) gives probability one. Both the one-step probabilities and the composition into NOT fit the proposed rule. The complex directions appearing in these sums can be expressed by writing a nonzero amplitude as \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\). Here \\(r=|z|\\) is its magnitude and \\(\\phi\\) its phase . The probability \\(|z|^2=r^2\\) retains the magnitude but discards the phase. Positive and negative real weights correspond to phases zero and \\(\\pi\\); complex weights allow the intervening angles. Multiplication adds phases, and addition depends on their relative values. This is how two amplitude lists with the same probabilities can give different probabilities after a further transition. Predict first. The text found one square root \\(S\\) of NOT. Is it the only one? Does \\(-S\\) work? Name a step other than doing nothing whose square does nothing. Square roots of any step Target \\(B\\): NOT Hadamard do nothing sign flip Another square root Paths from 0 Paths from 1 Switching probability of \\(B\\) Phase of its stay weight from 0 Phase of its switch weight from 0 Extra phase from 1 With scripting enabled, this figure finds the square roots of a chosen one-bit step \\(B\\) and checks \\(A^2=B\\) path by path. The question \\(\\sqrt X\\sqrt X=X\\) generalized: \\(A\\) has weights \\(a,b\\) from \\(0\\) and \\(c,d\\) from \\(1\\), and two steps of \\(A\\) must give a chosen step \\(B\\). Each two-step weight is a sum over the intermediate configuration, for example \\(a^2+bc\\) from \\(0\\) back to \\(0\\). The sliders vary \\(B\\) over valid steps, and \\(a,b,c,d\\) follow continuously. At the start, \\(B\\) is NOT and \\(a=d=(1+i)/2\\), \\(b=c=(1-i)/2\\), the solution found above. The weights come from \\(A=(B+sI)/t\\), where \\(I\\) does nothing, \\(s^2=B(0\\to0)B(1\\to1)-B(0\\to1)B(1\\to0)\\), and \\(t^2=B(0\\to0)+B(1\\to1)+2s\\). Each sign choice for \\(s\\) and \\(t\\) can give a different root. Chapter 4 explains why this works. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(-S\\) works, because each path product has two factors and the signs cancel. NOT has four square roots: \\(S\\), \\(-S\\), the step with the stay and switch weights of \\(S\\) exchanged, and its negative. Doing nothing has infinitely many: NOT, Hadamard, and the sign flip each square to doing nothing. The construction in the figure finds only two of them. Problems A symmetric step has stay-weight \\(a\\) and switch-weight \\(b\\). Write the two-step weights for returning and switching. Why can real \\(a,b\\) not make two steps equal NOT? For the square root of NOT, \\(\\sqrt X\\), with \\(\\bra0\\sqrt X\\ket0=\\bra1\\sqrt X\\ket1=(1+i)/2\\) and \\(\\bra1\\sqrt X\\ket0=\\bra0\\sqrt X\\ket1=(1-i)/2\\), compute the two-step weights from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\) and to \\(1\\). Find every operation with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=a\\) and \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=b\\) whose two steps give NOT. Show in particular that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) cannot both be real."}, {"title": "Interference and phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Interference and phase § interference · coherent · global phase · relative phase When paths end at the same configuration, we add their amplitudes and then take the squared magnitude. The probability rule therefore gives \\[p(k)=\\Bigl|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\Bigr|^2.\\] The contributions can reinforce or cancel. For two Hadamard steps starting at \\(0\\), the two paths to \\(0\\) each contribute \\(1/2\\). They add to one, giving probability one. The two paths to \\(1\\) contribute \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They cancel, giving probability zero. Taking the squared magnitude of each path separately would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) at either endpoint. Adding the amplitudes first raises one probability and lowers the other. This effect is called interference . It is constructive when the probability is greater than the sum of the separate squared magnitudes, and destructive when it is smaller. Paths whose amplitudes combine in this way are called coherent . For complex amplitudes, phase determines how the contributions combine. Multiplying every amplitude by the same factor \\(e^{i\\theta}\\) changes all their phases by the same amount. Every later sum acquires that same factor, and its squared magnitude stays unchanged because \\(|e^{i\\theta}|=1\\). Such a common phase is called a global phase . It has no effect on the probabilities, even after further transitions. The phase difference between two amplitudes is their relative phase . Changing it can change their sum. For example, take the state \\[\\frac{\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Both squared magnitudes are \\(1/2\\), whatever the value of \\(\\phi\\). But the two contributions arriving at \\(0\\) after a Hadamard step are \\(1/2\\) and \\(e^{i\\phi}/2\\). Their sum depends on \\(\\phi\\), so \\[p(0)=\\left|\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}2\\right|^2=\\frac{1+\\cos\\phi}{2}.\\] At \\(\\phi=0\\), the contributions have the same sign and add to one. At \\(\\phi=\\pi\\), they have opposite signs and cancel. At \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\), the probability is \\(1/2\\). The initial probabilities are the same in all three cases; the relative phase changes the probabilities after \\(H\\). Predict first. Predict \\(p(0)\\) after \\(H\\) for \\(\\phi=0\\), \\(\\pi/2\\), and \\(\\pi\\). Then change only the global phase \\(\\theta\\): what happens to the arrows, and what happens to the probabilities? Relative and global phase Relative phase \\(\\phi\\) = 0 Global phase \\(\\theta\\) = 0 Apply \\(Z\\): add \\(\\pi\\) to \\(\\phi\\) Reset Each plane shows the complex amplitudes arriving at one final configuration after \\(H\\), starting from \\((v_0,v_1)=e^{i\\theta}\\bigl(1/\\sqrt2,\\,e^{i\\phi}/\\sqrt2\\bigr)\\). Teal: the contribution from \\(v_0\\). Orange: the contribution from \\(v_1\\), drawn from the end of the teal arrow. Blue: their sum. The dashed circle has radius one; the bar shows the squared length of the sum. After trying it: compare your explanation \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\) gives \\(1\\), \\(1/2\\), and \\(0\\). A global phase turns both contributions, and so their sum, by the same angle. The lengths of the sums, and therefore the probabilities, do not change. A simple way to change this phase is to multiply the amplitude at \\(1\\) by \\(-1\\), leaving the amplitude at \\(0\\) unchanged. This is the operation \\(Z\\), with stay-weights \\(\\bra0Z\\ket0=1\\) and \\(\\bra1Z\\ket1=-1\\) and no arrows between configurations. It changes the relative phase by \\(\\pi\\). Starting at \\(0\\), two Hadamard steps return to \\(0\\); inserting \\(Z\\) between them gives \\(1\\) instead. An algorithm can use such phase changes to control which contributions reinforce and which cancel, and so change the final probabilities. Check your understanding What goes wrong if we take the squared magnitude of each path amplitude before adding? Compare your explanation Taking squared magnitudes first removes the signs and phases that allow cancellation. For the two paths to \\(1\\) under two Hadamard steps, it gives \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) instead of zero. Problems Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(i/2\\). What is its probability? Is the interference constructive, destructive, or neither? Two paths contribute \\(0.6\\) and \\(-0.2\\) to an outcome. Compare the probability obtained by adding first with the result of squaring first. Start with amplitudes \\((1/\\sqrt2,e^{i\\phi}/\\sqrt2)\\) and apply \\(H\\). The probability of \\(0\\) is \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Which values of \\(\\phi\\) give the same probabilities, and what extra operation could distinguish them?"}, {"title": "Path information", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 Path information § record · uncomputation Two Hadamard steps starting at \\(0\\) return to \\(0\\). The two contributions to final configuration \\(1\\) are \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\), so they cancel. What changes if another part of the machine records which intermediate configuration was visited? Introduce a second bit as a record , initially zero. A complete configuration now has two labels, \\(ij\\): \\(i\\) is the value of the first system and \\(j\\) the record. Thus \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) are different configurations even though their first labels agree. We apply the same path rules to these complete configurations: multiply weights along a path, add amplitudes at a common endpoint, and then take the squared magnitude. Starting at \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first label while leaving the record unchanged. This gives the state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). Next record the first label by a controlled-NOT operation: leave the second label alone when the first is zero, and flip it when the first is one. Its transitions are \\[\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{00}=\\ket{00},\\quad\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{01}=\\ket{01},\\quad\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{10}=\\ket{11},\\quad\\mathrm{CNOT}\\ket{11}=\\ket{10},\\] each with weight one. The state is now \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Apply the final \\(H\\) to the first label only. From \\(00\\), it reaches \\(00\\) and \\(10\\), with transition weights \\(+1/\\sqrt2\\) each. From \\(11\\), it reaches \\(01\\) with weight \\(+1/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(11\\) with weight \\(-1/\\sqrt2\\). Every node names the complete configuration: first system, then record. Each incoming amplitude in the left column is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). Edge labels are transition weights; the values at the right are the resulting amplitudes. The final state is \\[\\tfrac12\\ket{00}+\\tfrac12\\ket{01}+\\tfrac12\\ket{10}-\\tfrac12\\ket{11}.\\] The contributions \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\) associated with first-label value one end at \\(10\\) and \\(11\\). They cannot cancel because these are different complete configurations. Each has probability \\(1/4\\). If we ask only whether the first label is one, we include both distinct outcomes and add their probabilities: \\[p(\\text{first label}=1)=p(10)+p(11)=\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12.\\] Ignoring the record does not make its two values the same configuration. The loss of interference here follows from the different endpoints, whether or not anyone reads the record. We can recover the earlier interference by undoing the recording step before the final \\(H\\). Controlled-NOT is its own inverse: a second application takes \\(00\\) back to \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) back to \\(10\\), both with weight one. The record is again zero on both alternatives, and their amplitudes remain \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). Now the final \\(H\\) has two routes to the same endpoint \\(10\\), with contributions \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They cancel. The contributions to \\(00\\) add to one, restoring a certain return to \\(00\\). Undo the recording before recombining the paths. The record has the same value on both alternatives, so contributions can meet at the same complete configuration. The crossed edges do not introduce an extra node. This is uncomputation applied to path information. It means reversing the operation that created the record while preserving the amplitudes, not merely deleting a label from our description. The bookkeeping lesson is that every part of the machine capable of distinguishing the paths belongs in the configuration. Next we treat these weighted sums of kets as vectors and state the physical rules for evolution, measurement, and composite systems. Problems After retaining the record, what is the probability of each final configuration? What is the probability that the record is one? Follow the routes from the two configurations after recording, \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), through the final \\(H\\). Calculate the four final amplitudes and explain why the contributions to first-label value one do not cancel. Suppose the recording is undone only after the final \\(H\\). Track the four amplitudes through this extra controlled-NOT. Does the first label now return to zero with certainty? Explain why the timing matters."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(e^{i\\pi/3}/2\\). What is its probability? \\(|1+e^{i\\pi/3}|^2/4=(2+2\\cos60^\\circ)/4=3/4\\). Squaring each path first would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\): the phases partly reinforce."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-2", "text": "Which of these amplitude lists \\((v_0,v_1)\\) are normalized? (a) \\((\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)\\); (b) \\((\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2},-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\); (c) \\((0.6,\\,0.8i)\\); (d) \\((\\tfrac{1+i}{2},\\tfrac{1-i}{2})\\). For each normalized list, give the two probabilities. (a) \\(\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12\\): not normalized, so these amplitudes cannot describe a state as they stand. (b) \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\): normalized, probabilities \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\). (c) \\(0.36+0.64=1\\): normalized, probabilities \\(0.36\\) and \\(0.64\\). (d) \\(|1+i|^2/4=2/4=\\frac12\\) for each: normalized, probabilities \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\). Signs and complex phases do not affect normalization; only magnitudes do."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-3", "text": "Three states have amplitudes \\(A=(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\), \\(B=(-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2},-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\), and \\(C=(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2},-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\). All give probabilities \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\). Apply one Hadamard step to each. Which pairs remain indistinguishable, and why? \\(A\\) becomes \\((1,0)\\), so the result is \\(0\\) with certainty. \\(B\\) becomes \\((-1,0)\\), also \\(0\\) with certainty. \\(C\\) becomes \\((0,1)\\), so the result is \\(1\\) with certainty. \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) differ by a global phase \\(-1\\), which multiplies every later amplitude by the same factor and never changes a probability. \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) differ in relative phase, which the Hadamard step converts into a difference in outcome. Equal probabilities do not mean equal states."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-4", "text": "An operation \\(G\\) on a qubit has the real weights \\(\\bra0G\\ket0=\\bra1G\\ket1=\\cos\\alpha\\), \\(\\bra1G\\ket0=\\sin\\alpha\\), and \\(\\bra0G\\ket1=-\\sin\\alpha\\). Check the two conditions on an operation, compute the two-step weights by path sums, and find the \\(\\alpha\\) for which two steps send \\(0\\) to \\(1\\). The squares leaving each configuration add to \\(\\cos^2\\alpha+\\sin^2\\alpha=1\\), and the cross sum is \\(-\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\alpha+\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=0\\). From \\(0\\): weight \\(\\cos^2\\alpha-\\sin^2\\alpha=\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). From \\(1\\): \\(-\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). At \\(\\alpha=\\pi/4\\), two steps send \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) and \\(1\\) to \\(-0\\): NOT, except for a relative sign. Real weights reach NOT only up to this sign; exactly NOT needs complex weights."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-5", "text": "A proposed step \\(S\\) has all four weights \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\) equal to \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). (a) Check the outgoing-normalization condition for each definite input. (b) Check the cross-sum condition \\(\\sum_j(\\bra jS\\ket0)^*\\bra jS\\ket1=0\\). (c) Apply the step to the normalized input \\((\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\) and find the total of the squared magnitudes. What goes wrong? (a) From \\(0\\) or from \\(1\\) the outgoing squared magnitudes are \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\), so definite inputs stay normalized. (b) The cross sum is \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\neq0\\), so the condition fails. (c) Each new amplitude is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big)=1\\), so the output is \\((1,1)\\), with total \\(2\\). The “probabilities” would add to \\(2\\). Because contributions from the two inputs always reinforce, the cross terms add instead of cancelling. The Hadamard step avoids this with its single negative weight, which makes the cross sum \\(\\frac12-\\frac12=0\\). Checking definite inputs alone is not enough."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-6", "text": "Let \\(\\sqrt X\\) be the square root of NOT, with stay-weight \\(a=(1+i)/2\\) and switch-weight \\(b=(1-i)/2\\). Starting at \\(\\ket0\\), find the amplitudes after three steps of \\(\\sqrt X\\), and after four. Use \\(\\sqrt X\\sqrt X=X\\) to shorten the calculation, and give the probabilities. One step from \\(0\\) gives amplitudes \\((a,b)=\\big(\\frac{1+i}{2},\\frac{1-i}{2}\\big)\\). Two steps give NOT, so three steps are one step followed by NOT, which exchanges the amplitudes: \\(\\big(\\frac{1-i}{2},\\frac{1+i}{2}\\big)\\). Each has squared magnitude \\(\\frac12\\), so the probabilities are \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\). Four steps are NOT twice, which does nothing: the amplitudes are \\((1,0)\\), and the run returns to \\(0\\) with certainty. After one or three steps the outcome looks like a fair coin, yet the next steps can still produce a certain result. The amplitudes retain information that the probabilities do not show."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-7", "text": "Repeat the path-information experiment of Section 3.4.3 with a different “record”: between the two Hadamard steps on the first label, apply NOT to the record bit whatever the first label is. Track the four amplitudes from \\(00\\) and find the final probabilities. Does the first label still return to \\(0\\) with certainty? Explain using complete configurations. After the first \\(H\\): \\(00\\) and \\(10\\), each with amplitude \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\). NOT on the record gives \\(01\\) and \\(11\\). The final \\(H\\) on the first label sends \\(01\\) to \\(01\\) and \\(11\\) with weights \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\), and \\(11\\) to \\(01\\) with \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\) and to \\(11\\) with \\(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\). The totals are \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\) at \\(01\\) and \\(\\frac12-\\frac12=0\\) at \\(11\\). So the outcome is \\(01\\) with certainty: the first label returns to \\(0\\), and the record reads \\(1\\). The record changed, but identically on both paths, so the two paths still meet at the same complete configurations and interfere. Only a record that distinguishes the paths prevents interference."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “After one Hadamard step from \\(0\\), the bit is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\) each. That is the same as a fair coin, so a second Hadamard step should act like a second coin flip, giving \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\) again.” Compute what actually happens, and identify the step in the student’s reasoning that fails. Two Hadamard steps from \\(0\\) give amplitude \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(\\frac12-\\frac12=0\\) at \\(1\\): the result is \\(0\\) with certainty. The student treated the intermediate state as a probability distribution and combined the two paths by adding probabilities, \\(\\frac14+\\frac14\\). But the intermediate state is the amplitude list \\((\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\), and the paths to each endpoint must be combined by adding amplitudes, which can cancel. Equal probabilities after one step do not determine what happens after the next. The amplitudes carry the signs that make cancellation possible, and a coin’s probabilities do not."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-9", "text": "Apply \\(H\\), then the phase step \\(R\\) with \\(\\bra0R\\ket0=1\\) and \\(\\bra1R\\ket1=e^{i\\phi}\\), then \\(H\\), to \\(0\\). Using path sums, find the probability of outcome \\(0\\) as a function of \\(\\phi\\), and the values of \\(\\phi\\) that make each outcome certain. To \\(0\\): \\(\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). To \\(1\\): \\(\\tfrac12-\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). The probability of \\(0\\) is \\(|1+e^{i\\phi}|^2/4=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Outcome \\(0\\) is certain at \\(\\phi=0\\) and outcome \\(1\\) at \\(\\phi=\\pi\\). A phase that no direct measurement sees controls the result completely."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-10", "text": "Find a one-bit step \\(A\\) that changes only phases (\\(\\bra1A\\ket0=\\bra0A\\ket1=0\\)) and satisfies \\(A^2=Z\\). How many such steps are there? Then compute the probability of \\(0\\) after \\(H\\), \\(A\\), \\(H\\) starting at \\(0\\), and compare it with \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\). What does “half of \\(Z\\)” do to the interference? Two steps of a diagonal step multiply each weight by itself, so \\(p^2=1\\) and \\(q^2=-1\\): \\(p=\\pm1\\) and \\(q=\\pm i\\). That gives four steps, and each satisfies normalization because \\(|p|=|q|=1\\). Take \\(A\\) with \\(p=1\\), \\(q=i\\). After \\(H\\) the amplitudes are \\((\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2})\\); \\(A\\) changes them to \\((\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2})\\), a relative phase \\(\\pi/2\\). The final \\(H\\) gives \\(p(0)=(1+\\cos\\frac\\pi2)/2=\\frac12\\). With \\(Z\\), the relative phase is \\(\\pi\\) and \\(p(0)=0\\); with no step it is \\(0\\) and \\(p(0)=1\\). Half the phase gives a probability halfway between, not a step halfway between “keep” and “flip.” The step with \\(q=i\\) reappears in later chapters as the phase gate \\(S\\)."}, {"title": "State vectors and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 State vectors and ket notation § qubit · ket · superposition · state vector At any step of a computation, each configuration \\(i\\) carries a complex amplitude \\(v_i\\), the amplitude for the computation to be in configuration \\(i\\); its squared magnitude \\(|v_i|^2\\) is the probability of that configuration. We have written the state as the weighted sum of configuration kets. Naming the whole state \\(\\ket\\psi\\), \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sum_iv_i\\ket i.\\] So far the sum has been bookkeeping: terms for the same configuration add their weights, and terms for different configurations stay apart. These are exactly the rules of vector arithmetic, and from now on we treat \\(\\ket\\psi\\) as a vector. For one bit, \\(\\ket\\psi=v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\). The weighted sum is a superposition , \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is a state vector , and a quantum system with two distinguishable configurations is a qubit . Fix an order for the configurations, \\(0\\) first, and list the amplitudes in that order. The state becomes a column, and each configuration ket becomes a column with a single one: \\[\\ket0=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1=v_0\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}+v_1\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}v_0\\\\v_1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] An ordered list like this is a vector , and its entries are its coordinates . The kets \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) form the computational basis . In columns, the two bookkeeping rules become entrywise operations. Adding two weighted sums adds matching coordinates, and multiplying a sum by a number, as a step does when it weights the successors of configuration \\(i\\) by \\(v_i\\), multiplies every coordinate: \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a_0\\\\a_1\\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix}b_0\\\\b_1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}a_0+b_0\\\\a_1+b_1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad c\\begin{pmatrix}a_0\\\\a_1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ca_0\\\\ca_1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] These are vector addition and multiplication by a number . A column built from only some of the paths holds part of each final amplitude, so normalization applies to the complete state, not to its parts. \\(\\ket\\psi=(3/5)\\ket0+(4/5)\\ket1\\) has coordinates \\((3/5,4/5)^T\\) along the basis directions \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Complex amplitudes use the same coordinates. For the vector drawn above, the probabilities of the two configurations are \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\), which add to one. The amplitudes are coordinates of one description, not two outcomes obtained at once. Each row follows one input through a Hadamard step. Read the amplitude on an arrow, then find the term with its output label in the equation. The dashed arrow in the second row corresponds to the negative term before \\(\\ket1\\). The Hadamard step, \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(H\\ket1=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), becomes a statement about columns: \\[H:\\ \\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}\\mapsto\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}\\mapsto\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Each coordinate of an image is an arrow weight \\(\\bra jH\\ket i\\); the minus sign is the negative arrow from \\(1\\) to \\(1\\). Nothing here is limited to two configurations. With the four configurations \\(00,01,10,11\\), taken in that order, \\[\\frac{\\ket{00}-\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\0\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The column lists the amplitudes in that order: \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) for \\(00\\), zero for \\(01\\) and \\(10\\), and \\(-1/\\sqrt2\\) for \\(11\\). The ket form names each configuration, so we need not remember its position in the column. A state gives the amplitude for each configuration. What is the amplitude to go from one state to another? Problems Write \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\ket0+i\\sqrt{\\tfrac23}\\ket1\\) as a column vector, and check that its coordinates satisfy the condition for a state vector. With the two-qubit configurations ordered \\(00,01,10,11\\), write \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) as a column vector, and give the probability of each configuration. Find numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with \\(\\ket0=a\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}+b\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\). Relative to which basis is \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) a superposition, and relative to which is it not?"}, {"title": "Inner products and transition amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Inner products and transition amplitudes § inner product · transition amplitude · bra · conjugate symmetry A state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_i\\psi_i\\ket i\\) gives the amplitude \\(\\psi_j\\) for configuration \\(j\\). From here on we name the amplitudes after their state, so that a second state \\(\\ket\\phi\\) has amplitudes \\(\\phi_j\\). As with arrow weights, we write the amplitude to go from the initial state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to the final configuration \\(j\\) with the final one on the left: \\[\\braket j\\psi=\\psi_j.\\] When the initial state is itself a configuration \\(\\ket i\\), its only nonzero amplitude is a one at \\(i\\), so \\[\\braket ji=\\begin{cases}1,&j=i,\\\\0,&j\\ne i.\\end{cases}\\] These are the weights \\(\\bra jI\\ket i\\) of the identity step: with no step acting between the two configurations, we leave out the \\(I\\). Now let the end be a state too, \\(\\ket\\phi=\\sum_j\\phi_j\\ket j\\). What is the amplitude to go from \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket\\phi\\)? A path passes through one configuration \\(j\\): it reaches \\(j\\) with amplitude \\(\\braket j\\psi\\), then continues into \\(\\ket\\phi\\) with amplitude \\(\\braket\\phi j\\). The product rule multiplies along each path, and the sum rule adds the paths: \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\braket\\phi j\\,\\braket j\\psi.\\] This has the shape of the sum rule \\[\\bra kTS\\ket i=\\sum_j\\bra kT\\ket j\\,\\bra jS\\ket i,\\] with the step-free amplitudes \\(\\braket j\\psi\\) and \\(\\braket\\phi j\\) in place of arrow weights. Each path multiplies the amplitude \\(\\braket j\\psi\\) into configuration \\(j\\) by the amplitude \\(\\braket\\phi j\\) from \\(j\\) into the final state, and the paths add to \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). On the right the final state is \\(\\ket0\\): \\(\\braket00=1\\) and \\(\\braket01=0\\), so only the path through \\(0\\) contributes, leaving \\(\\braket0\\psi\\). What is \\(\\braket\\phi j\\), the amplitude to go from configuration \\(j\\) into \\(\\ket\\phi\\)? Nothing so far fixes it, so we must choose it, and the probability rule suggests the choice. That rule writes the probability of configuration \\(j\\) as an amplitude times its conjugate, \\(|\\psi_j|^2=\\braket j\\psi^*\\braket j\\psi\\), so normalization reads \\[\\sum_j\\braket j\\psi^*\\,\\braket j\\psi=1.\\] The path formula for a state compared with itself, \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j\\braket\\psi j\\,\\braket j\\psi\\), has the same shape term by term if the amplitude into a state is the conjugate of the amplitude out of it: \\[\\braket\\phi j=\\braket j\\phi^*=\\phi_j^*.\\] We take this as the definition. With it, \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\) is the total probability, so a normalized state arrives at itself with amplitude one, \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=1\\), and for a final configuration the definition gives back the values \\(1\\) and \\(0\\) above. Without the conjugate, the normalized state \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) would give \\(\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{i^2}2=0\\), as if its total probability were zero. Therefore \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j.\\] This sum is the inner product of the two vectors, and it gives the transition amplitude from \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket\\phi\\), written \\(\\mathcal A(\\text{final}\\leftarrow\\text{initial})=\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). When both states are configurations, it is \\(\\braket ji\\) again. The definition is chosen for consistency with the probability rule; what \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\) means physically for a general final state is settled later, with the postulates. The conjugated row belonging to \\(\\ket\\phi\\) is called its bra : \\[\\ket\\phi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_0\\\\\\phi_1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\bra\\phi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_0^*&\\phi_1^*\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Its entries are the amplitudes \\(\\braket\\phi j\\) into the final state, and the column \\(\\ket\\psi\\) holds the amplitudes \\(\\braket j\\psi\\) out of the initial one. Multiplying the row by the column performs the path sum and gives \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). What happens if we exchange the initial and final states? The conjugate moves to the other factor in each product, so the amplitude is conjugated: \\[\\braket\\psi\\phi=\\sum_j\\psi_j^*\\phi_j=\\left(\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\right)^*=\\bigl(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\bigr)^*.\\] This is conjugate symmetry . The magnitude is unchanged; only the phase reverses. For \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), \\(\\braket1\\psi=i/\\sqrt2\\), while \\(\\braket\\psi1=-i/\\sqrt2\\). For real vectors the order does not matter. An inner product turns two state vectors into one complex number. What does that number say about the vectors themselves? Problems Compute \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\) for \\(\\ket\\phi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Are the two states orthogonal? For \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(i/2)\\ket1\\), find the amplitudes \\(\\braket0\\psi\\) and \\(\\braket1\\psi\\), their probabilities, and the norm of \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Use conjugate symmetry with \\(\\ket\\phi=\\ket\\psi\\) to show that \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi\\) is real. Then use the coordinate formula to show that it is never negative, and zero only for the zero vector."}, {"title": "Geometric view", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Geometric view § norm · unit vector · orthogonal · orthonormal · component · angle With real amplitudes, a qubit state is an arrow in a plane, and the inner product can be read off a picture of two arrows. What we find there carries over to complex amplitudes. The first thing the inner product measures is length. The norm , or length, of a vector is \\(\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\braket\\psi\\psi}\\), so normalized states are unit vectors. Two vectors are orthogonal when their inner product is zero, and a set of mutually orthogonal unit vectors is orthonormal . The computational basis is orthonormal: \\(\\braket jk\\) is one when \\(j=k\\) and zero otherwise. How large can a transition amplitude between unit vectors be? Split \\(\\ket\\psi\\) into a part along \\(\\ket\\phi\\) and a part orthogonal to it: \\[\\ket\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi\\,\\ket\\phi+\\ket{\\psi_\\perp},\\qquad \\braket\\phi{\\psi_\\perp}=0.\\] The component \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\,\\ket\\phi\\) lies along \\(\\ket\\phi\\), and the remainder \\(\\ket{\\psi_\\perp}\\) meets that line at a right angle. Because the two parts are perpendicular, their squared lengths add to the squared length of \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Taking the inner product of both sides with \\(\\ket\\phi\\) confirms that the coefficient of the first part is \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). Because the two parts are orthogonal, their squared lengths add up to the squared length of \\(\\ket\\psi\\): \\[1=|\\braket\\phi\\psi|^2+\\|\\psi_\\perp\\|^2.\\] So \\(|\\braket\\phi\\psi|^2\\) lies between zero and one. It is one when the two vectors differ only by a global phase , and zero when they are orthogonal. For real unit vectors in a plane, \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\cos\\theta\\), where \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between them; complex vectors extend this geometry while keeping phase in the inner product. The same split, done along every basis direction at once, gives the coordinates. Each \\(\\braket j\\psi\\) is the component of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) along \\(\\ket j\\). Because the basis directions are orthonormal, these components rebuild the vector, and their squared lengths add up to its squared length: \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sum_j\\ket j\\,\\braket j\\psi,\\qquad \\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\braket j\\psi|^2.\\] The probabilities of the configurations are the squared lengths of these perpendicular components. For example, with \\(\\ket\\phi=\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the transition amplitude is \\(\\braket0\\psi=\\sqrt3/2\\), and its squared magnitude \\(3/4\\) is the probability of configuration \\(0\\). The remaining part, \\((1/2)\\ket1\\), is orthogonal to \\(\\ket0\\). Predict first. Keep \\(\\ket\\phi\\) fixed and turn \\(\\ket\\psi\\). At what angle between them is the probability \\(3/4\\)? \\(1/2\\)? \\(0\\)? What happens at \\(180^\\circ\\)? An amplitude as a component Direction of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) Direction of \\(\\ket\\phi\\) Reset In this real-coordinate example, the initial and final states are unit vectors in a plane of state space, not physical space. The orange component of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) along \\(\\ket\\phi\\) has signed length \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\cos\\theta\\); the dashed segment is \\(\\ket{\\psi_\\perp}\\). At the start, \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\) gives \\(\\sqrt3/2\\), with square \\(3/4\\). After trying it: compare your explanation The probability is \\(\\cos^2\\theta\\): \\(3/4\\) at \\(30^\\circ\\), \\(1/2\\) at \\(45^\\circ\\), and \\(0\\) at \\(90^\\circ\\), where the states are orthogonal and the component vanishes. At \\(180^\\circ\\) the amplitude is \\(-1\\), but the probability is \\(1\\). Problems Find the norm of \\(\\ket v=3\\ket0+4i\\ket1\\), and the unit vector pointing in the same direction. For the real unit vectors \\(\\ket\\phi=\\cos\\alpha\\ket0+\\sin\\alpha\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=\\cos\\beta\\ket0+\\sin\\beta\\ket1\\), show that \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)\\). For which angles are the two states orthogonal? Let \\(\\ket\\phi=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\). Find the component \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\,\\ket\\phi\\) and the orthogonal remainder. Check that their squared norms add to one."}, {"title": "Transition matrices", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-4", "text": "4.1.4 Transition matrices § matrix · matrix product · identity matrix · linearity We already know how a step acts on a state: each configuration goes to the weighted sum of its successors, \\(U\\ket i=\\sum_j\\bra jU\\ket i\\,\\ket j\\), and a state goes to the same combination of these images. Geometrically, the operation moves each basis direction and combines the moved components to give the new vector. For a qubit state \\(\\ket\\psi=v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\), this means \\[U\\ket\\psi=v_0U\\ket0+v_1U\\ket1.\\] The coefficients weight the images of the original basis vectors. Their sum is the output state, whose coordinates in the fixed computational basis will generally differ from the input coordinates. This is linearity : an operation carries a sum of input contributions to the same sum of their outputs. More generally, \\[U(a\\ket\\phi+b\\ket\\psi)=aU\\ket\\phi+bU\\ket\\psi.\\] A transition matrix records the amplitudes for this motion between basis configurations. Its entry \\(U_{ji}\\) is the amplitude to arrive at \\(j\\) from \\(i\\): \\[U_{ji}=\\bra jU\\ket i.\\] This is the arrow weight we have used since our first transition diagrams, now placed in a table. We use column \\(i\\) for the initial configuration and row \\(j\\) for the final one, so column \\(i\\) lists the coefficients of \\(U\\ket i\\). The matrix is thus a coordinate description of the same transition graph. The arrows leaving \\(0\\) carry amplitudes \\(a,b\\), which form column \\(0\\); those leaving \\(1\\) carry \\(c,d\\), which form column \\(1\\). Arrows arriving at \\(0\\) fill row \\(0\\), and arrows arriving at \\(1\\) fill row \\(1\\). Thus \\(U_{ji}=\\bra jU\\ket i\\). For a general input, the amplitude arriving at \\(j\\) is found by carrying each \\(v_i\\) along the arrow \\(i\\to j\\) and adding: \\[v'_j=\\sum_i\\bra jU\\ket i\\,v_i=\\sum_iU_{ji}v_i,\\qquad \\ket{\\psi'}=U\\ket\\psi.\\] Matrix–vector multiplication performs these path sums. The resulting coordinates describe where the state vector has moved. We want to allow only operations that preserve the probability interpretation of the amplitudes. For a closed system evolving within the same state space, every normalized input must therefore give a normalized output: the probabilities of all possible configurations must still add to one. \\[\\sum_i|v_i|^2=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\sum_j\\left|\\sum_iU_{ji}v_i\\right|^2=1\\qquad\\text{for every input state}.\\] We have met this requirement before, as two conditions on the arrow weights of a step. Outgoing normalization, \\(\\sum_j|\\bra jU\\ket i|^2=1\\), says that each column has unit length. The cross-sum condition, \\(\\sum_j\\bigl(\\bra jU\\ket i\\bigr)^*\\bra jU\\ket k=0\\) for \\(i\\ne k\\), says that distinct columns are orthogonal. Together, \\[\\sum_j U_{ji}^*U_{jk}=\\delta_{ik},\\] where \\(\\delta_{ik}\\) is one when \\(i=k\\) and zero otherwise. A square matrix with these orthonormal columns is called unitary . Linear operations on a closed system must be unitary to preserve the probability interpretation for every state. For example, consider the real transition \\[U=\\frac15\\begin{pmatrix}4&-3\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}.\\] It rotates \\(\\ket0\\) to \\((4/5)\\ket0+(3/5)\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(-(3/5)\\ket0+(4/5)\\ket1\\). The two directions remain perpendicular and retain their lengths. For \\(\\ket\\psi=(3/5)\\ket0+(4/5)\\ket1\\), the weighted images have horizontal components that cancel and vertical components that add to one. The vector therefore moves to \\(\\ket1\\). This real transition matrix rotates the basis vectors and the state vector. The coefficients \\(3/5\\) and \\(4/5\\) still weight the transformed basis vectors: \\(U\\ket\\psi=(3/5)U\\ket0+(4/5)U\\ket1=\\ket1\\). The dashed parallelogram shows their addition. The coordinate axes stay fixed; the drawing shows a real plane in state space. The familiar gates also have simple geometric actions. \\(X\\), or NOT, exchanges the two basis directions. \\(Z\\) changes the sign of the component along \\(\\ket1\\). \\(H\\) sends the basis vectors to the two perpendicular diagonal directions \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Their transition matrices record these actions: \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Here \\(Z\\) can also be written \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\). The drawings use real vectors; the same linear description applies when amplitudes and transition weights are complex. After the operation, comparison with a final state \\(\\ket\\phi\\) uses the inner product: \\[\\mathcal A(\\phi\\leftarrow\\psi;U)=\\bra\\phi U\\ket\\psi=\\sum_{i,j}\\braket\\phi j\\,\\bra jU\\ket i\\,\\braket i\\psi.\\] The operation moves the initial vector, and the final bra selects its component along the final direction. In path terms, every route enters some configuration \\(i\\), crosses the arrow to some \\(j\\), and continues into \\(\\ket\\phi\\). For definite initial and final configurations, this is the arrow weight \\(U_{ji}\\). Two successive operations move the vector twice. If \\(U\\) acts first and \\(V\\) second, the final state is \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\). The amplitude between two configurations is our two-step sum rule, read in matrix entries: \\[\\bra kVU\\ket i=\\sum_j\\bra kV\\ket j\\,\\bra jU\\ket i,\\qquad (VU)_{ki}=\\sum_jV_{kj}U_{ji}.\\] This defines the matrix product . The rightmost matrix acts first. For example, two Hadamard steps restore every input vector, so \\(HH=I\\), where \\(I\\) is the identity matrix . Problems An operation \\(U\\) has weights \\(\\bra0U\\ket0=0\\), \\(\\bra1U\\ket0=i\\), \\(\\bra0U\\ket1=-i\\), and \\(\\bra1U\\ket1=0\\). Write its matrix, and apply it to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Compute the matrices \\(XH\\) and \\(HX\\), and apply each to \\(\\ket0\\). Can the outputs differ by only a global phase? A proposed rule sends \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) to \\(\\alpha\\ket0+2\\beta\\ket1\\), rescaled so that the squared magnitudes add to \\(1\\). Show that the rule is not linear by comparing its action on \\(\\ket0\\), \\(\\ket1\\), and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Reversibility of transitions", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-5", "text": "4.1.5 Reversibility of transitions § adjoint · unitary Every step we have built so far can be undone: NOT, CNOT, and the Hadamard step each undo themselves. How do we undo a step that does not undo itself, and is there a recipe that works for every step? For a deterministic reversible step the recipe is plain: run every arrow backward. CNOT sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to \\(\\ket{11}\\), so undoing it sends \\(\\ket{11}\\) back to \\(\\ket{10}\\). Try the same recipe on the square root of NOT, whose arrows carry the stay-weight \\(a=(1+i)/2\\) and the switch-weight \\(b=(1-i)/2\\). Its arrows look the same in both directions, so reversing them gives \\(\\sqrt X\\) again, and \\(\\sqrt X\\sqrt X=X\\). Instead of returning, the bit flips. What went wrong? Follow the paths that return to the start. Staying twice contributes \\(a\\cdot a\\), switching twice contributes \\(b\\cdot b\\), and \\[a^2+b^2=\\frac i2-\\frac i2=0.\\] The two contributions cancel when we need them to add to one. Now conjugate the reversed weights as well. The return paths contribute \\(a^*a+b^*b=|a|^2+|b|^2=1\\), and the paths that end at the other configuration contribute \\[b^*a+a^*b=\\frac i2-\\frac i2=0.\\] Reversing the arrows and conjugating their weights undoes \\(\\sqrt X\\). This recipe is the adjoint . The adjoint \\(U^\\dagger\\) of a step \\(U\\) reverses every arrow and conjugates its weight: \\[\\bra iU^\\dagger\\ket j=\\bigl(\\bra jU\\ket i\\bigr)^*.\\] It is the rule we chose for amplitudes between states, \\(\\braket\\phi j=\\braket j\\phi^*\\), now applied to the arrows of a step: going backward conjugates. In the matrix, row \\(i\\) of \\(U^\\dagger\\) is column \\(i\\) of \\(U\\) with its entries conjugated, so \\(U^\\dagger\\) is the conjugate transpose of \\(U\\). For \\(U^\\dagger\\), reverse each arrow of \\(U\\) and conjugate its amplitude. The arrow \\(i\\to j\\) becomes \\(j\\to i\\), so the original column \\(i\\) becomes conjugated row \\(i\\). Colors match the columns in the preceding diagram. When \\(U\\) is unitary, this reversed transition is its inverse. Does the adjoint undo every step, or did the square root of NOT simply happen to work? Apply \\(U\\) and then \\(U^\\dagger\\), and use the sum rule: \\[\\bra iU^\\dagger U\\ket k=\\sum_j\\bra iU^\\dagger\\ket j\\,\\bra jU\\ket k=\\sum_j\\bigl(\\bra jU\\ket i\\bigr)^*\\bra jU\\ket k.\\] This is exactly the expression in our two conditions on a step. For \\(i=k\\), outgoing normalization makes it one; for \\(i\\ne k\\), the cross-sum condition makes it zero. So every step that preserves total probability is undone by its adjoint: \\[U^\\dagger U=I.\\] Apply \\(U\\) first and \\(U^\\dagger\\) second. The paths from \\(0\\) back to \\(0\\) contribute \\(a^*a+b^*b=1\\); those from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) contribute \\(c^*a+d^*b=0\\). The other input gives the second column in the same way. Normalization and the cross-sum condition make the total transition the identity. We called such a step unitary. Now we see what that buys: its adjoint is its inverse , \\(U^{-1}=U^\\dagger\\), the operation that restores every state, superpositions included. For example, \\(H^\\dagger=H\\), which is why the Hadamard step undoes itself. What does a unitary step do to the geometry of states? Comparing a moved state with \\(\\ket\\phi\\) gives the same amplitude as moving \\(\\ket\\phi\\) back with the adjoint and comparing it with the original state: \\[\\bra\\phi U\\ket\\psi=\\braket{U^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi.\\] Writing both sides as path sums gives the same terms. Now take \\(\\ket\\phi=U\\ket\\chi\\). Since \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\), we get \\(\\braket{U\\chi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\chi\\psi\\): a unitary step keeps every length and every angle between states. It moves the whole state space rigidly, like a rotation, and a rigid motion can always be run backward. The adjoint exists for every matrix, not only for unitary ones, but then it need not undo anything. Take the shear \\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad A^\\dagger=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] \\(A\\) slides each point sideways by an amount set by its height. Its entries are real, so \\(A^\\dagger\\) is just the transpose, which slides each point upward by an amount set by its horizontal position. The two move \\(\\ket\\psi=\\tfrac35\\ket0+\\tfrac45\\ket1\\) in different directions. The same axes show the initial \\(\\ket\\psi\\) in blue, \\(A\\ket\\psi\\) in orange, and \\(A^\\dagger\\ket\\psi\\) in green. The arcs compare each image with the initial direction: \\(\\alpha\\approx23.4^\\circ\\) for \\(A\\) and \\(\\beta\\approx13.7^\\circ\\) for \\(A^\\dagger\\). Both direction and length can change; this nonunitary example has \\(A^\\dagger A\\ne I\\). Applying one after the other does not bring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) back either, in either order. Orange shows \\(AA^\\dagger\\ket\\psi\\): apply \\(A^\\dagger\\) first, then \\(A\\). Green shows \\(A^\\dagger A\\ket\\psi\\): apply \\(A\\) first, then \\(A^\\dagger\\). Faint dashed vectors mark the intermediate states, and the arcs compare the final directions with the blue initial vector. The order changes the result, and neither composition restores this input. Undoing \\(A\\) needs a slide the other way, \\(A^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\), not \\(A^\\dagger\\). The identity \\(\\bra\\phi A\\ket\\psi=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\) still holds, so the adjoint always moves an operation from one side of an inner product to the other. It undoes the operation only when the operation is unitary. A shear stretches lengths, so it cannot be a step of a closed quantum system. Predict first. For the shear, which order turns \\(\\ket\\psi\\) further from its starting direction? Switch to the rotation: what do \\(AA^\\dagger\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(A^\\dagger A\\ket\\psi\\) become? Both orders of \\(A\\) and \\(A^\\dagger\\) for any real \\(A\\) shear (the example) rotation stretch squeeze random \\(A\\) Play both orders \\(A_{00}\\) \\(A_{01}\\) \\(A_{10}\\) \\(A_{11}\\) Direction of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) With scripting enabled, this figure draws \\(AA^\\dagger\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(A^\\dagger A\\ket\\psi\\) for an adjustable real matrix \\(A\\). As in the preceding figure, blue is \\(\\ket\\psi\\), orange follows \\(A^\\dagger\\) then \\(A\\), and green follows \\(A\\) then \\(A^\\dagger\\). Faint dashed vectors are the intermediate states, and the arcs \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) measure each final direction from \\(\\ket\\psi\\). The entries of \\(A\\) are real, so \\(A^\\dagger\\) is its transpose and every vector stays in this plane. The dashed circle has radius one; the drawing rescales when vectors grow. Play both orders shows each matrix acting in turn. With a rotation, both orders return \\(\\ket\\psi\\), because a rotation is unitary. After trying it: compare your explanation For the shear, \\(AA^\\dagger\\) turns \\(\\ket\\psi\\) by about \\(18.1^\\circ\\) and \\(A^\\dagger A\\) by about \\(4.4^\\circ\\), and both change its length. For a rotation, \\(A^\\dagger A=AA^\\dagger=I\\), so both orders return \\(\\ket\\psi\\) exactly: a rotation is unitary, and its adjoint is its inverse. Problems Find the adjoints of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). Which of the two matrices is unitary? For \\(U=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\i&1\\end{pmatrix}\\), find \\(U^\\dagger\\) and compute \\(U^\\dagger U\\). Interpret the four entries as transition amplitudes for the two-step process. Using \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\), prove that \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Conclude that the product of two unitaries is unitary."}, {"title": "State and evolution postulates", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 State and evolution postulates § postulate Do different unit vectors always describe different preparations? Not quite. Multiplying every amplitude by the same phase \\(e^{i\\theta}\\) changes no probability, now or after any later step, because every path sum picks up the same factor. Two vectors that differ only in this way can never be told apart, so they must describe the same situation. State postulate. A pure state of a finite quantum system is represented by a unit vector in a complex inner-product space. Vectors differing only by a global phase represent the same physical state. How much freedom does that leave for a qubit, \\(\\ket\\psi=v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\)? Normalization, \\(|v_0|^2+|v_1|^2=1\\), ties the two magnitudes together, and the global phase can make the first nonzero amplitude real and nonnegative. What remains is one angle for the split of magnitudes and one relative phase : \\[\\ket\\psi=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\ket1,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi.\\] At \\(\\theta=0\\) or \\(\\pi\\) one amplitude vanishes, and no relative phase remains. Which changes can a system undergo when nothing outside it interferes? The mathematics has already narrowed the choice. A step that keeps the probability rule consistent for every state must be unitary, and a unitary step is undone by its adjoint and moves state space rigidly. The postulate claims that this is how nature behaves. Evolution postulate. The evolution of a closed system is unitary: \\[\\ket\\psi\\longmapsto U\\ket\\psi,\\qquad U^\\dagger U=I.\\] With real amplitudes the picture is a rotation. Unit vectors end on a circle, and a rotation moves every arrow around that circle by the same angle, keeping each length and the angle between any two states: \\[U(\\delta)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\delta&-\\sin\\delta\\\\\\sin\\delta&\\cos\\delta\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Every arrow ends on the unit circle: both states have length one before and after the operation. The same rotation carries \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(U\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(\\ket\\chi\\) to \\(U\\ket\\chi\\), preserving the angle between them. This real-amplitude example shows a rotation in state space; general unitaries preserve inner products in complex state space. In complex state space the preserved quantity is the inner product itself, \\(\\braket{U\\chi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\chi\\psi\\). These arrows are amplitude lists, not directions in physical space. Problems Normalize \\(\\ket0+2i\\ket1\\), and give the probabilities of a computational-basis measurement. Write the state \\((i\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) in the form \\(\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket1\\), after removing a global phase. Show that \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(e^{i\\gamma}\\ket\\psi\\) give the same probabilities for every sequence of operations followed by a computational-basis measurement. Then show that \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) do not differ by a global phase."}, {"title": "The measurement postulate", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 The measurement postulate § measurement postulate Unitary evolution carries every amplitude forward. An observation does something else: it reports one outcome. Which outcome, how often, and what state is left for the next calculation? Measurement postulate. For a normalized state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_jv_j\\ket j\\), an ideal measurement in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket j\\psi|^2=|v_j|^2\\). Conditional on that outcome, the state becomes \\(\\ket j\\). The probability is the rule we have used since complex amplitudes first appeared; the postulate adds what happens to the state. Both are physical assumptions, not consequences of path arithmetic. Measuring \\(\\ket\\psi=\\frac{\\sqrt3}2\\ket0+\\frac12\\ket1\\) gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). If the result is \\(1\\), the state is now \\(\\ket1\\), so an immediate second measurement gives \\(1\\) again. For \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the first measurement gives the indicated probabilities. Each branch leaves a definite state, so an immediate repeat gives the same result. Geometrically, the postulate uses the split we already know. A state is the sum of its perpendicular components along the basis directions, \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_j\\ket j\\,\\braket j\\psi\\). Measurement picks one component, with probability equal to its squared length, keeps it, and rescales it to unit length. The operator that keeps the component along \\(\\ket j\\) is the projector \\(P_j=\\ketbra jj\\), since \\(P_j\\ket\\psi=\\ket j\\,\\braket j\\psi\\). In this language, \\[p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2,\\qquad \\ket{\\psi_j}=\\frac{P_j\\ket\\psi}{\\sqrt{p(j)}}\\quad(p(j)>0).\\] Predict before measuring Prepare \\(\\ket+=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Measuring in the computational basis gives two equally likely outcomes. Must every measurement of this state be uncertain? Compare your prediction No. A measurement in a basis containing \\(\\ket+\\) gives its corresponding outcome with certainty. The preparation is unchanged; the measurement asks a different question. Why should the computational basis be special? It is not. Suppose a unitary \\(U\\) carries an orthonormal basis \\(\\ket{b_j}\\) to the computational basis, \\(U\\ket{b_j}=\\ket j\\). Measuring in the \\(b\\) basis means applying \\(U\\) and then measuring as above. The probability of outcome \\(j\\) is then \\[|\\bra jU\\ket\\psi|^2=|\\braket{U^\\dagger j}\\psi|^2=|\\braket{b_j}\\psi|^2,\\] using the adjoint to move \\(U\\) across the inner product. This is the physical meaning of the transition amplitude promised earlier: a measurement in an orthonormal basis that contains \\(\\ket\\phi\\) reports \\(\\phi\\) with probability \\(|\\braket\\phi\\psi|^2\\), and conditional on that outcome the state becomes \\(\\ket\\phi\\). In a real state-vector plane, take \\(\\ket\\psi=\\cos\\alpha\\ket0+\\sin\\alpha\\ket1\\) and the basis \\[\\ket{b_0}=\\cos\\beta\\ket0+\\sin\\beta\\ket1,\\qquad \\ket{b_1}=-\\sin\\beta\\ket0+\\cos\\beta\\ket1.\\] The amplitudes are \\(\\braket{b_0}\\psi=\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)\\) and \\(\\braket{b_1}\\psi=\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\), and their squares are the probabilities. At \\(\\beta=0\\) this is the computational, or Z, measurement. At \\(\\beta=45^\\circ\\) it is the X measurement, with \\(\\ket{b_0}=\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket{b_1}=-\\ket-\\), where \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\); the overall sign of \\(\\ket{b_1}\\) does not change its projector. Preparing \\(\\ket+\\) and measuring X gives \\(+\\) with certainty, while measuring Z on the same preparation gives each outcome half the time. Changing the measurement changes the question asked, not the prepared state. Predict first. For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), about how many of 100 fresh copies give 1? If one measurement gives 1, what does measuring the same system again give? One state, different measurements Prepared state angle α 30° Prepare |+⟩ Prepare |−⟩ Measurement basis angle β 0° Measure in Z Measure in X Measure this system Prepare a fresh copy Measure 100 fresh copies Reset For \\(\\ket+\\), the Z-basis probabilities are \\((1/2,1/2)\\); the X-basis probabilities are \\((1,0)\\). For \\(\\ket-\\), the Z-basis probabilities are also \\((1/2,1/2)\\), while the X-basis probabilities are \\((0,1)\\). Blue shows the current system; teal and orange show its projected components in the selected measurement basis. After an outcome, the animation keeps that component and normalizes it; the intermediate lengths illustrate the calculation, not a physical trajectory. The bars count fresh copies of the prepared state, with dashed lines at the predicted probabilities. Changing the basis preserves the system but clears counts from the old basis. Repeating a measurement on one system is distinct from measuring independently prepared copies. After trying it: compare your explanation About 25, varying from run to run by roughly 4. Measuring the same system again gives 1 every time, because its state is now \\(\\ket1\\). Only fresh copies reproduce the frequencies \\(3/4\\) and \\(1/4\\). A full history alternates the two kinds of change. Between measurements, unitary evolution carries the state smoothly. At each measurement the observed outcome selects a component, which is rescaled to give the next state, and evolution resumes from there. A schematic history conditional on a sequence of observed outcomes. Blue curves show continuous unitary evolution; orange drops show the idealized measurement updates. Here \\(\\ket{\\psi(t_k^-)}\\) is the state just before measurement and \\(\\ket{\\psi_k}\\) is the normalized state just after it. The vertical coordinate is illustrative: a drop does not mean that the norm, total probability, or energy decreases. Different outcomes can produce different subsequent histories, and a measurement can leave the state unchanged when its outcome is already certain. This explains what happens when we look between two Hadamard steps. From \\(\\ket0\\), the first \\(H\\) gives \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). A measurement leaves \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\), and the second \\(H\\) then gives equal probabilities, whereas two uninterrupted Hadamard steps return to \\(\\ket0\\) with certainty. Unlike every step we have studied, the measurement update cannot be undone. After outcome \\(0\\), an initial \\(\\ket0\\) and an initial \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) both leave \\(\\ket0\\), so the final state cannot tell which one we started from. This does not stop a larger system, including the measuring device and its record, from evolving unitarily; the conditional description keeps only the reported branch. Problems Measure \\((\\ket0+2i\\ket1)/\\sqrt5\\) in the computational basis. Give the probability of each outcome and the state after it. A qubit in \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) is measured twice in a row. Find the probabilities of the four sequences of outcomes \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Start from \\(\\ket0\\). Apply \\(H\\), measure, apply \\(H\\), and measure again. Find the probabilities of the four pairs of outcomes, and compare with applying \\(H\\) twice and measuring once."}, {"title": "The composite-state postulate", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 The composite-state postulate § tensor product · product state · entanglement One qubit needs two amplitudes. What does a pair of qubits need? It is tempting to keep two lists of two, one for each qubit. But we met the answer when a second bit recorded a path: a complete configuration of the pair names a configuration of each part. Two qubits have four complete configurations, \\(00,01,10,11\\), the first label for qubit \\(A\\) and the second for qubit \\(B\\). A state of the pair assigns an amplitude to each of them: \\[\\ket\\Psi=c_{00}\\ket{00}+c_{01}\\ket{01}+c_{10}\\ket{10}+c_{11}\\ket{11},\\qquad\\sum_{i,j}|c_{ij}|^2=1.\\] The complete bipartite graph has one edge for every joint configuration. Edge \\((i,j)\\) carries amplitude \\(c_{ij}\\), and \\(|c_{ij}|^2\\) is the probability of that joint computational-basis outcome. These edges describe a joint state, not transitions in time; crossing edges do not form an extra node. For general states the four weights need not factor into local amplitudes. The count grows quickly. Two separate lists for \\(n\\) qubits hold \\(2n\\) amplitudes, while the pair-by-pair description of \\(n\\) qubits needs one amplitude for each of the \\(2^n\\) bit strings. When do the separate lists suffice? Suppose the two qubits are prepared independently, \\(A\\) in \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and \\(B\\) in \\(c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\). Reaching the pair \\(01\\) means reaching \\(0\\) in \\(A\\) and \\(1\\) in \\(B\\), so by the product rule its amplitude is \\(ad\\). Doing this for every pair builds the joint column from the two separate columns: \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{pmatrix}\\otimes\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\d\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\ket i\\otimes\\ket j=\\ket{ij}.\\] This combination is the tensor product . Each entry multiplies one amplitude of \\(A\\) by one of \\(B\\), so the probability of \\(01\\) is \\(|a|^2|d|^2\\), the product of the separate probabilities, as independent preparations require. A joint state of this form is a product state ; if both parts are normalized, so is the pair. The postulate says which joint states are allowed. Composite-state postulate. The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the state spaces of its parts. Its basis consists of the products \\(\\ket{ij}=\\ket i\\otimes\\ket j\\) of basis states, and every unit vector in it is an allowed state. If the parts have \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) basis states, the pair has \\(mn\\): dimensions multiply. The postulate allows every normalized combination of the joint basis, not only the products that independent preparations produce. Are there allowed states that are not products? Take \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). A product would need \\(ac\\) and \\(bd\\) to be nonzero, which makes all four of \\(a,b,c,d\\) nonzero, and then \\(ad\\) and \\(bc\\) could not vanish. But this state has no \\(01\\) or \\(10\\) term. A pure joint state that is not a product is entangled ; the failure to factor is called entanglement . What does entanglement mean? Measuring both qubits of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(00\\) or \\(11\\), each half the time, so the two results always agree. A classical coin that prepares either \\(00\\) or \\(11\\) produces the same counts, so agreement alone does not reveal entanglement. The difference lies in the amplitudes of the whole state: neither qubit has a state vector of its own from which the pair could be rebuilt. The next chapter uses the postulates to define quantum computation; operations on joint states, and measurements that tell entanglement from classical correlation, follow after that. Problems If system A has two basis states and system B has three, how many basis states does their composite have? Expand \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\otimes\\ket1\\) in the joint basis and give its computational-basis outcome probabilities. Why can \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) not be written as a product of two pure qubit states?"}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\tfrac35\\ket0+\\tfrac45\\ket1\\), compute the amplitudes \\(\\braket{+}{\\psi}\\) and \\(\\braket{-}{\\psi}\\), where \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Find the two probabilities and check that they add to one. Why must they? \\(\\braket{+}{\\psi}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(\\frac35+\\frac45\\big)=\\frac{7}{5\\sqrt2}\\), with probability \\(\\frac{49}{50}\\). \\(\\braket{-}{\\psi}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(\\frac35-\\frac45\\big)=-\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt2}\\), with probability \\(\\frac{1}{50}\\). They add to \\(1\\). The vectors \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) are orthonormal, so \\(\\ket\\psi=\\braket{+}{\\psi}\\ket++\\braket{-}{\\psi}\\ket-\\) and its squared length, \\(1\\), is the sum of the two squared components. In the computational basis the same state gives \\(\\frac{9}{25}\\) and \\(\\frac{16}{25}\\): different tests, different probabilities, one state."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Is \\(U=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\i&-i\\end{pmatrix}\\) unitary? Check that its columns have unit length and are orthogonal, then compute \\(U\\ket0\\) and \\(U\\ket1\\). Each column has squared length \\(\\frac12(1+1)=1\\). Their overlap is \\(\\frac12\\big(1^*\\cdot1+i^*\\cdot(-i)\\big)=\\frac12\\big(1+(-i)(-i)\\big)=\\frac12(1-1)=0\\). So the columns are orthonormal and \\(U\\) is unitary. The columns are the images: \\(U\\ket0=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(U\\ket1=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). These are two orthogonal states with equal computational-basis probabilities; they differ only in relative phase."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "Which pairs describe the same physical state? (a) \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((i\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\); (b) \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\); (c) \\(\\ket1\\) and \\(-\\ket1\\). For the pair that differs, compute the inner product. (a) Same: \\(i(\\ket0+i\\ket1)=i\\ket0-\\ket1\\), a global phase \\(i\\). (c) Same: a global phase \\(-1\\). (b) Different: no common factor works, because the first coordinates would require the factor \\(1\\) and the second would then require \\(i=-i\\). Their inner product is \\(\\frac12\\big(1\\cdot1+(-i)(-i)\\big)=\\frac12(1-1)=0\\), so they are orthogonal: a test along one direction never reports the other. A relative phase can make two states with identical computational-basis probabilities perfectly distinguishable."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-4", "text": "Let \\(U=\\frac15\\begin{pmatrix}4&-3\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Starting from \\(\\ket0\\): (a) apply \\(U\\) twice and then measure, giving both probabilities; (b) apply \\(U\\), measure, apply \\(U\\), and measure, giving the probability that the final outcome is \\(0\\). Explain the difference. (a) \\(U^2\\ket0=\\frac{1}{25}(7\\ket0+24\\ket1)\\), so outcome \\(0\\) has probability \\(\\frac{49}{625}\\approx0.078\\) and outcome \\(1\\) has \\(\\frac{576}{625}\\approx0.922\\). (b) After the first \\(U\\), the measurement gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(\\frac{16}{25}\\) and \\(1\\) with \\(\\frac{9}{25}\\), leaving \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\). From \\(\\ket0\\), \\(U\\) gives outcome \\(0\\) with probability \\(\\frac{16}{25}\\); from \\(\\ket1\\), with probability \\(\\frac{9}{25}\\). So \\(p(0)=\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot\\frac{9}{25}=\\frac{337}{625}\\approx0.539\\). Without the measurement, the two routes to \\(0\\) have amplitudes \\(\\frac{16}{25}\\) and \\(-\\frac{9}{25}\\), which partly cancel. The intermediate measurement keeps one route per run, so probabilities add instead of amplitudes."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-5", "text": "For \\(U=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\i&-i\\end{pmatrix}\\), compute \\(U^\\dagger\\) and verify \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). Then use the adjoint to find the state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) with \\(U\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\), and check your answer by applying \\(U\\). Transposing and conjugating gives \\(U^\\dagger=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&-i\\\\1&i\\end{pmatrix}\\). Then \\(U^\\dagger U=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1+(-i)(i)&1+(-i)(-i)\\\\1+i\\cdot i&1+i(-i)\\end{pmatrix}=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{pmatrix}=I\\). Since \\(U\\) is unitary, \\(U^\\dagger\\) undoes it: \\(\\ket\\psi=U^\\dagger\\ket0=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket1)\\), the first column of \\(U^\\dagger\\). Check: \\(U\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket1)=\\frac12\\big((1+1)\\ket0+(i-i)\\ket1\\big)=\\ket0\\). The adjoint gives the inverse without solving equations, but only because \\(U\\) is unitary."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-6", "text": "Write \\(\\ket\\psi=\\tfrac12\\ket0+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}e^{i\\pi/3}\\ket1\\) in the form \\(\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\ket1\\). Then find the state of the same form that is orthogonal to it, and check the orthogonality. What happens to \\(\\theta\\) and \\(\\phi\\) when \\(Z\\) is applied to \\(\\ket\\psi\\)? \\(\\cos\\frac\\theta2=\\frac12\\) gives \\(\\theta=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\), and then \\(\\sin\\frac\\theta2=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\) and \\(\\phi=\\frac\\pi3\\). Take \\(\\ket{\\psi^\\perp}=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\ket0-e^{i\\pi/3}\\frac12\\ket1\\). Then \\(\\braket{\\psi^\\perp}{\\psi}=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\cdot\\frac12+\\big(-e^{-i\\pi/3}\\tfrac12\\big)\\big(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}e^{i\\pi/3}\\big)=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}=0\\). In the standard form, \\(\\ket{\\psi^\\perp}\\) has \\(\\theta'=\\pi-\\theta=\\frac\\pi3\\) and \\(\\phi'=\\phi+\\pi\\). Applying \\(Z\\) multiplies the \\(\\ket1\\) amplitude by \\(-1=e^{i\\pi}\\): \\(\\theta\\) stays \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(\\phi\\) becomes \\(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\). \\(Z\\) changes only the relative phase, so computational-basis probabilities are unchanged."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-7", "text": "Show that a two-qubit state \\(\\sum_{ij}c_{ij}\\ket{ij}\\) is a product state only if \\(c_{00}c_{11}=c_{01}c_{10}\\). Apply the test to (a) \\(\\frac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})\\); (b) \\(\\frac12(\\ket{00}+i\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+i\\ket{11})\\). For the product state, find the factors. If \\(c_{ij}=a_ib_j\\), then \\(c_{00}c_{11}=a_0b_0a_1b_1=c_{01}c_{10}\\). (a) \\(c_{00}c_{11}=-\\frac14\\) but \\(c_{01}c_{10}=\\frac14\\): the test fails, so the state is entangled, even though every computational-basis outcome has probability \\(\\frac14\\). (b) \\(c_{00}c_{11}=\\frac i4=c_{01}c_{10}\\), and indeed the state is \\(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\frac{\\ket0+i\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\). Expanding the product reproduces all four amplitudes. Uniform probabilities do not reveal whether a state is entangled; the relations among the amplitudes do. (The converse also holds: when the condition is met, the state factors.)"}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “The state \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) means the qubit is really \\(0\\) or really \\(1\\), each with probability \\(\\frac12\\); we just don’t know which.” Propose an experiment that distinguishes the superposition from a qubit prepared as \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\) by a fair coin, and compute its predictions for both. Apply \\(H\\) and then measure in the computational basis. The superposition gives \\(H\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\ket0\\): outcome \\(0\\) with certainty. For the coin preparation, \\(H\\ket0\\) and \\(H\\ket1\\) each give outcome \\(0\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\), so the average is \\(\\frac12\\). A computational-basis measurement alone cannot tell the two preparations apart, but the Hadamard step reveals the difference, because the superposition has a definite relative phase between its amplitudes and the coin preparation has none. A superposition is a definite state, not a description of our ignorance."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-9", "text": "Prove that a unitary preserves inner products: \\(\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). Deduce that no unitary can map \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) to two orthogonal states. Explain why this means that no measurement, even after any closed-system evolution, distinguishes \\(\\ket0\\) from \\(\\ket+\\) with certainty. By the defining property of the adjoint, \\(\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\bra\\phi U^\\dagger U\\ket\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi\\), since \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). For \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\), the overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), and so is the overlap of \\(U\\ket0\\) and \\(U\\ket+\\) for every unitary \\(U\\): the images are never orthogonal. A computational-basis measurement that always gave one outcome for the first state and the other outcome for the second would require the two evolved states to lie along different basis directions, which are orthogonal. So no sequence of unitary steps followed by such a measurement separates \\(\\ket0\\) from \\(\\ket+\\) perfectly. Nonorthogonal states can be told apart only with some probability of error, a limit that Quantum Information develops in detail."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-10", "text": "Count the real parameters needed to specify (a) a general pure state of \\(n\\) qubits and (b) a product of \\(n\\) one-qubit pure states, after removing normalization and global phase. Evaluate both for \\(n=2\\) and \\(n=10\\). What does the comparison suggest about how common entangled states are? (a) \\(2^n\\) complex amplitudes are \\(2\\cdot2^n\\) real numbers. Normalization removes one and global phase another, leaving \\(2^{n+1}-2\\). (b) Each qubit needs \\(\\theta\\) and \\(\\phi\\), so a product needs \\(2n\\). For \\(n=2\\): \\(6\\) against \\(4\\). For \\(n=10\\): \\(2046\\) against \\(20\\). Product states form a thin subset of all pure states, and the gap grows exponentially. A randomly chosen pure state of many qubits is therefore almost never a product. This is also why a classical description of a general \\(n\\)-qubit state needs exponentially many numbers, while a product state needs only \\(2n\\)."}, {"title": "Input, evolution, and output", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Input, evolution, and output § A computation begins with a task: which input is supplied, and what output would count as a successful answer? In the quantum model, we encode the input in a state \\(\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{in}}}\\), apply a prescribed unitary operation \\(U_f\\), and obtain \\[\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{out}}}=U_f\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{in}}}.\\] Measurement then produces a classical result \\(y\\). The output state determines the probabilities of the possible results; it is not itself a classical answer that can be read out in full. In particular, one measurement does not reveal the entire list of amplitudes. Here \\(U_f\\) denotes the complete unitary evolution chosen for the task. The label does not promise that an arbitrary classical function can be applied irreversibly to a quantum state, nor that a whole computation costs one step. The construction and cost of this operation are part of the algorithm . The diagram describes a computation with a final measurement. A more general procedure may include intermediate measurements and later operations selected by their recorded results. We begin with the simpler arrangement so that the relation between the input state, evolution, and readout remains visible. We will draw this model as a circuit. Read it from left to right: a solid horizontal wire carries the quantum system, and a gate box specifies the operation applied there. The input ket is written at the left. A box marked M measures the system; the double line after it carries the classical result. A box for a complete unitary can hide many elementary gates, so its size on the page says nothing about its cost. The wire carries the quantum input through the complete unitary Uf to measurement. M produces one classical result; Uf may contain many elementary gates. Problems What are the three stages in the overview of a quantum computation? Does one measurement reveal every amplitude in the output state? Why does drawing the whole evolution as one block not make it a one-step computation?"}, {"title": "Composition and interference", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Composition and interference § A gate is an elementary unitary operation available to the computation. A program specifies an ordered sequence of such operations. If \\(U_1\\) acts first and \\(U_T\\) acts last, their combined action is \\[U_f=U_T\\cdots U_2U_1,\\qquad\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{out}}}=U_T\\cdots U_1\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{in}}}.\\] The rightmost factor acts first because it is applied directly to the input. Each gate preserves normalization for every state, so the complete unitary sequence preserves the probability interpretation as well. The number \\(T\\) counts the gates in this sequence; naming their product \\(U_f\\) does not remove their implementation cost. Linearity applies each operation to every component of a superposition . Amplitudes multiply along paths and add at common endpoints. A computation uses these sums to make useful outcomes more likely and unwanted outcomes less likely. An intermediate superposition is not an answer: its relative phases matter because later operations can turn them into different output probabilities. For example, \\(HH=I\\), while \\(HZH=X\\). Here \\(I\\) leaves a qubit unchanged, \\(X\\) exchanges its two basis states, and \\(Z\\) changes the sign of the amplitude at \\(1\\). The same two Hadamard steps therefore have different results when a phase change is inserted between them. Section 5.2 follows those amplitudes explicitly. The same wire passes through U1 and then U2. The output is U2 U1 applied to the input. Problems In the product U₃U₂U₁, which operation acts first? Compare \\(HH\\) and \\(HZH\\) on input \\(\\ket0\\). Why can a phase change matter even when it leaves the immediate basis probabilities unchanged?"}, {"title": "Readout and computational cost", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 Readout and computational cost § For an ideal final measurement in the computational basis, the probability of result \\(y\\) is \\[p(y)=|\\braket y{\\psi_{\\mathrm{out}}}|^2=|\\bra yU_f\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{in}}}|^2.\\] The input specification, available operations, measurement, and interpretation of its result together define a computational procedure. For a task depending on an input \\(x\\), the input state or the prescribed operations may depend on information supplied about \\(x\\). The procedure must state what information is actually available; it cannot assume that the answer has already been encoded for free. Each run returns one outcome. If success is probabilistic, the algorithm must give its success probability for the promised inputs. Repeated runs require fresh initialization and cost additional work. Measurement during a run changes the state conditional on its result, so it cannot generally be inserted without changing the later interference . A recorded result can also determine a later operation; this is classical control. Resources include the number of qubits, elementary gates, sequential operation stages, repetitions, and the work needed to initialize the input and interpret the output. On a single qubit, successive gates must be applied in order. The next chapter extends the model to several qubits, where operations on different parts and joint operations must be distinguished. A one-qubit example illustrates the model, but does not by itself establish a computational speedup. Such a claim needs a family of growing tasks and a comparison of costs under stated access rules. Each fresh run prepares its input and repeats the circuit. The double line carries the sampled classical result, not the state’s amplitude list. Problems What does a measurement return on a single run? An output state has amplitudes \\(3/5\\) and \\(4/5\\). Find the two basis probabilities. Why must repeated executions and initialization be included in the cost?"}, {"title": "A single-qubit circuit", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 A single-qubit circuit § The simplest register contains one qubit. Its input state is \\(\\ket{\\psi_{\\mathrm{in}}}=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\), where \\(|a|^2+|b|^2=1\\). For a definite classical input bit \\(x\\), choose \\(\\ket x\\). One way to start is to initialize the qubit to \\(\\ket0\\) and apply \\(X\\) if the specified input is one. A circuit represents the ordered operations from left to right. A horizontal line, called a wire, tracks this qubit. Its initial state is written at the left. A box labelled H, X, or Z applies that operation; an uninterrupted segment leaves the state unchanged. The wire describes the ordering of operations, not the physical path of a particle. A box labelled M here means a computational-basis measurement. After it, a double line carries the classical result. These are different descriptions: before measurement the wire carries a quantum state with amplitudes; the recorded output is a bit, zero or one. In the example below, H sends \\(\\ket0\\) to \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). The measurement returns either bit with probability \\(1/2\\). Writing and reading this simple circuit requires only one wire; controlled gates and multiple wires enter with the multi-qubit model in Chapter 6. Read left to right: initialize the qubit to zero, apply H, then measure. The double line carries the classical outcome y, not a state vector. Problems What do a single wire, a gate box, and a double output line mean? Find the output probabilities after H on \\(\\ket1\\). How can a specified classical input bit 1 be initialized from \\(\\ket0\\)?"}, {"title": "A complete one-qubit computation", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 A complete one-qubit computation § Consider the task of reversing an input bit: output one for input zero, and zero for input one. X does this directly. We can also implement the same task using interference, with H, then Z, then H. For input zero, \\[\\ket0\\xrightarrow{H}\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{Z}\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{H}\\ket1.\\] After the first H, both computational-basis outcomes would have probability \\(1/2\\). Z changes their relative sign without changing those immediate probabilities. The final H combines the two paths to each endpoint. At zero the contributions are \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\), which cancel; at one they are both \\(1/2\\), which add to one. The final measurement returns one with certainty. We must also check the other allowed input. Starting from one, \\[\\ket1\\xrightarrow{H}\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{Z}\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{H}\\ket0.\\] The circuit therefore implements NOT on both classical inputs. By linearity, its action on an arbitrary input is \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\mapsto b\\ket0+a\\ket1\\), agreeing with \\(HZH=X\\). This construction uses three gates instead of the single X gate; its purpose is to expose how interference computes the result. If Z is omitted, the two H operations cancel and the input is returned unchanged. If a computational-basis measurement is inserted after the first H, either selected basis state gives equal final probabilities after Z and the last H. Thus both the relative phase and the absence of an intervening measurement are essential to this particular implementation. On input zero, the sequence H, Z, H gives output one with certainty. On input one, the same sequence gives zero. Measurement occurs only after the full unitary evolution. Problems What is the result of H, Z, H on input zero? Follow input \\(\\ket1\\) through H, Z, H. What changes if a measurement is inserted after the first H?"}, {"title": "Phase and output probabilities", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Phase and output probabilities § Replace Z by a phase operation \\(R_\\phi\\) that leaves \\(\\ket0\\) unchanged and multiplies the amplitude of \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(e^{i\\phi}\\): \\[R_\\phi\\ket0=\\ket0,\\qquad R_\\phi\\ket1=e^{i\\phi}\\ket1.\\] This factor has magnitude one, so the operation preserves normalization. The angle \\(\\phi\\) specifies the phase shift. For input zero, the complete evolution is \\[\\ket0\\xrightarrow{H}\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{R_\\phi}\\frac{\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\xrightarrow{H}\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}2\\ket0+\\frac{1-e^{i\\phi}}2\\ket1.\\] We can now read the probabilities from the final amplitudes: \\[p(0)=\\left|\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}2\\right|^2=\\frac{1+\\cos\\phi}{2},\\qquad p(1)=\\frac{1-\\cos\\phi}{2}.\\] At \\(\\phi=0\\), the middle operation is I and the output is certainly zero. At \\(\\phi=\\pi\\), it is Z and the output is certainly one. At \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\), the two outcomes are equally likely. In every case the state just before the final H has the same basis probabilities, \\(1/2,1/2\\); the final gate converts its relative phase into a measurable difference. This illustrates the model on one qubit: specify an input, compose unitary operations, calculate the output state, and obtain a classical result with the stated probability. Before enlarging the register in Chapter 6, we examine how the Pauli operations describe general single-qubit transformations. The final Hadamard converts the relative phase into measurement probabilities. Problems What does \\(R_\\phi\\) do to each basis state? Calculate the output probabilities at \\(\\phi=0,\\pi/2,\\pi\\). Start the phase circuit at \\(\\ket1\\) instead of \\(\\ket0\\). Find its final amplitudes and probabilities."}, {"title": "The Pauli operations", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 The Pauli operations § The single-qubit examples have used X to exchange the two configurations and Z to change their relative sign. A third operation combines exchange with opposite phase factors: \\[Y\\ket0=i\\ket1,\\qquad Y\\ket1=-i\\ket0.\\] The three operations X, Y, and Z are the Pauli gates . Including the identity I, their matrices in the ordered basis \\(\\ket0,\\ket1\\) are \\[I=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Each column lists the amplitudes produced from the corresponding input configuration. On an arbitrary state, their actions are \\[X(a\\ket0+b\\ket1)=b\\ket0+a\\ket1,\\] \\[Y(a\\ket0+b\\ket1)=-ib\\ket0+ia\\ket1,\\qquad Z(a\\ket0+b\\ket1)=a\\ket0-b\\ket1.\\] Each operation preserves normalization, and each undoes itself: \\(X^2=Y^2=Z^2=I\\). For Y, applying it twice to zero gives \\(i(-i)\\ket0=\\ket0\\); the same check works for one. The order of different Pauli operations matters. Applying Z and then X gives \\(XZ\\); applying X and then Z gives \\(ZX=-XZ\\). Moreover, \\(Y=iXZ\\). Thus products of Pauli gates reduce to I, X, Y, or Z multiplied by a common phase. For a standalone single-qubit computation, that common phase does not affect outcome probabilities. These are four separate circuits on the same arbitrary input. The displayed output vectors include their phase factors. Problems Find \\(Y\\ket0\\) and \\(Y\\ket1\\). Check \\(Y^2=I\\) on both basis states. Calculate \\(XZ\\ket0\\) and \\(ZX\\ket0\\). How does \\(Y=iXZ\\) account for the phase?"}, {"title": "Completeness and its meaning", "chapter": "Quantum Model of Computation", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 Completeness and its meaning § There is a precise sense in which these four matrices describe every single-qubit linear operation. Consider an arbitrary matrix \\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The diagonal entries can be supplied by I and Z: their sum gives equal diagonal entries, while their difference distinguishes the two positions. The off-diagonal entries can be supplied by X and Y: X contributes equally to both positions, while Y supplies opposite imaginary factors. Matching the four entries gives \\[A=\\frac{a+d}{2}I+\\frac{b+c}{2}X+\\frac{c-b}{2i}Y+\\frac{a-d}{2}Z.\\] For example, the upper-right entry of this expression is \\((b+c)/2-(c-b)/2=b\\), and the lower-left entry is \\((b+c)/2+(c-b)/2=c\\). The diagonal entries are a and d. These coefficients are uniquely determined, so I, X, Y, and Z form a complete basis for single-qubit operators . An operator here is a linear transformation of the state vector; it need not be a physically allowed gate. A unitary matrix is one important case. The Hadamard and the phase gate from the preceding section have particularly simple expansions: \\[H=\\frac{X+Z}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad R_\\phi=\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}2I+\\frac{1-e^{i\\phi}}2Z.\\] Completeness here concerns linear combinations of matrices, not successive application of gates. A circuit containing X followed by Z implements the product ZX; it does not implement the sum \\((X+Z)/\\sqrt2\\). Nor does randomly choosing X or Z produce that coherent sum. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), any sequence of Pauli gates leaves a basis state, up to a common phase. It cannot produce \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Thus the Pauli gates alone are not a universal gate set , even for one qubit. An arbitrary linear combination of them is not necessarily unitary either: \\(I+Z\\), for example, sends \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(2\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to zero. Continuous rotations generated by the Paulis provide additional operations. For example, define \\[R_x(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I-i\\sin(\\theta/2)X.\\] For real \\(\\theta\\), this is unitary: multiplying by its adjoint cancels the cross terms and gives \\((\\cos^2(\\theta/2)+\\sin^2(\\theta/2))I=I\\). It sends \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket0-i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket1\\), allowing continuously varying amplitudes. Choosing an arbitrary angle is an extra capability beyond applying the fixed X gate. We therefore have a compact language for describing single-qubit operations, while the separate problem of constructing them from available gates remains. Chapter 6 now extends the state and computation model to multiple qubits; the later chapter on universality returns to gate construction. Further reading: IBM Quantum’s guide to the Pauli basis . H equals the normalized matrix sum of X and Z. Applying X then Z instead gives their product ZX, a different circuit. Problems What is the distinction between a linear combination of Pauli matrices and a sequence of Pauli gates? Expand \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) in the Pauli basis. Can a sequence of I, X, Y, and Z map \\(\\ket0\\) to \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)? Explain without counting circuit lengths."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-1", "text": "Why does drawing the whole evolution as one block not make it a one-step computation? The block abbreviates a sequence of elementary operations; its implementation cost must still be counted."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-2", "text": "Apply \\(H\\), then \\(X\\), then \\(H\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and to \\(\\ket1\\), and identify the resulting operation. Do the same for \\(H\\), \\(Y\\), \\(H\\). For \\(HXH\\): \\(\\ket0\\to\\ket+\\to\\ket+\\to\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\to\\ket-\\to-\\ket-\\to-\\ket1\\). So \\(HXH=Z\\). For \\(HYH\\): \\(\\ket0\\to\\ket+\\to\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(i\\ket1-i\\ket0)=-i\\ket-\\to-i\\ket1\\), and \\(\\ket1\\to\\ket-\\to\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(i\\ket1+i\\ket0)=i\\ket+\\to i\\ket0\\). The operation sends \\(\\ket0\\mapsto-i\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket1\\mapsto i\\ket0\\), which is \\(-Y\\). Conjugating by \\(H\\) exchanges \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) and flips the sign of \\(Y\\); the earlier identity \\(HZH=X\\) is one case of this pattern."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-3", "text": "In the circuit \\(H\\), \\(R_\\phi\\), \\(H\\) on input \\(\\ket0\\), which values of \\(\\phi\\) give \\(p(1)=\\frac34\\)? Can a measurement of the output distinguish \\(\\phi=2\\pi/3\\) from \\(\\phi=4\\pi/3\\)? \\(p(1)=\\frac34\\) requires \\(\\cos\\phi=-\\frac12\\), so \\(\\phi=2\\pi/3\\) or \\(\\phi=4\\pi/3\\) in \\([0,2\\pi)\\). The probabilities depend on \\(\\phi\\) only through \\(\\cos\\phi\\), so these two phases give identical statistics for this circuit: no number of repetitions will separate them. The final amplitudes do differ, \\(\\frac{1\\pm e^{i\\phi}}{2}\\), but only in a way that the computational-basis measurement does not see. Telling them apart needs a different circuit, for example one that adds a known phase of \\(\\pi/2\\) before the final \\(H\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-4", "text": "Follow input \\(\\ket1\\) through H, Z, H. \\(\\ket1\\) becomes \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), then \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), then \\(\\ket0\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-5", "text": "Expand \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}\\) in the Pauli basis using \\(A=\\frac{a+d}{2}I+\\frac{b+c}{2}X+\\frac{c-b}{2i}Y+\\frac{a-d}{2}Z\\). Verify the expansion by adding the matrices. Then find \\(A\\ket0\\) and \\(A\\ket1\\), and explain why \\(A\\) is not a gate. The coefficients are \\(0\\) for \\(I\\) and \\(Z\\), \\(\\frac12\\) for \\(X\\), and \\(\\frac{-1}{2i}=\\frac i2\\) for \\(Y\\), so \\(A=\\frac12(X+iY)\\). Check: \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac i2\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}0&1+1\\\\1-1&0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Then \\(A\\ket1=\\ket0\\) and \\(A\\ket0=0\\), the zero vector. A gate must send every normalized state to a normalized state; \\(A\\) destroys \\(\\ket0\\) entirely, so it is not unitary. Completeness lets the Paulis describe every \\(2\\times2\\) matrix, including ones that no closed-system evolution can produce."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compute the products \\(XY\\), \\(YZ\\), and \\(ZX\\) by applying them to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), and express each as a phase times a single Pauli. A circuit applies \\(X\\) and then \\(Y\\). Which single gate has the same effect on every input, up to global phase? \\(XY\\ket0=X(i\\ket1)=i\\ket0\\) and \\(XY\\ket1=X(-i\\ket0)=-i\\ket1\\), so \\(XY=iZ\\). \\(YZ\\ket0=Y\\ket0=i\\ket1\\) and \\(YZ\\ket1=-Y\\ket1=i\\ket0\\), so \\(YZ=iX\\). \\(ZX\\ket0=Z\\ket1=-\\ket1\\) and \\(ZX\\ket1=Z\\ket0=\\ket0\\), which is \\(iY\\) since \\(iY\\ket0=-\\ket1\\) and \\(iY\\ket1=\\ket0\\). So \\(ZX=iY\\). The circuit “\\(X\\), then \\(Y\\)” is the product \\(YX=-XY=-iZ\\), which is \\(Z\\) up to the global phase \\(-i\\). Any product of Paulis collapses to one Pauli times a phase, which is why Pauli gates alone cannot create superpositions from \\(\\ket0\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-7", "text": "Using \\(R_x(\\theta)=\\cos\\frac\\theta2I-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2X\\) and \\(X^2=I\\), show that \\(R_x(\\alpha)R_x(\\beta)=R_x(\\alpha+\\beta)\\). Deduce that \\(R_x(\\pi/2)\\) is a square root of NOT up to global phase, and compare it with the square root \\(\\sqrt X\\) of Chapter 3, whose stay-weight is \\(\\frac{1+i}{2}\\) and switch-weight is \\(\\frac{1-i}{2}\\). Multiplying, \\(R_x(\\alpha)R_x(\\beta)=\\cos\\frac\\alpha2\\cos\\frac\\beta2I-i\\big(\\sin\\frac\\alpha2\\cos\\frac\\beta2+\\cos\\frac\\alpha2\\sin\\frac\\beta2\\big)X-\\sin\\frac\\alpha2\\sin\\frac\\beta2X^2\\). With \\(X^2=I\\), the \\(I\\) terms combine to \\(\\cos\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2}\\) and the \\(X\\) terms to \\(-i\\sin\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2}\\), giving \\(R_x(\\alpha+\\beta)\\). Hence \\(R_x(\\pi/2)^2=R_x(\\pi)=-iX\\): NOT up to the global phase \\(-i\\). Multiplying \\(R_x(\\pi/2)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(I-iX)\\) by \\(e^{i\\pi/4}=\\frac{1+i}{\\sqrt2}\\) gives \\(\\frac{1+i}{2}I+\\frac{1-i}{2}X\\), which is exactly \\(S\\). The square root of NOT found by path sums is a rotation by \\(\\pi/2\\) about the \\(X\\) direction, up to global phase."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student runs the phase circuit \\(H\\), \\(R_\\phi\\), \\(H\\) once, obtains outcome \\(0\\), and reports “\\(\\phi=0\\), because \\(p(0)=1\\) at \\(\\phi=0\\).” What does one outcome actually establish? How many runs would estimate \\(p(0)\\) to within about \\(\\pm0.05\\), and what would still remain unknown? One outcome \\(0\\) rules out only \\(\\phi=\\pi\\), where \\(p(0)=0\\). For every other \\(\\phi\\) the outcome had probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2>0\\); at \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\), for instance, it was a fair coin. Estimating \\(p(0)\\) requires repeated runs with fresh initialization. With \\(R\\) runs the observed frequency has standard deviation at most \\(1/(2\\sqrt R)\\), so about \\(100\\) runs give roughly \\(\\pm0.05\\). Even then, the estimate determines \\(\\cos\\phi\\) but not the sign of \\(\\phi\\). The cost of a measured answer includes the repetitions, as Section 5.1.3 warns."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-9", "text": "Start the phase circuit at \\(\\ket1\\) instead of \\(\\ket0\\). Find its final amplitudes and probabilities. The final state is \\((1-e^{i\\phi})\\ket0/2+(1+e^{i\\phi})\\ket1/2\\). Its probabilities are \\(p(0)=(1-\\cos\\phi)/2\\) and \\(p(1)=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Model of Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-quantum-model.html#exercise-10", "text": "Use \\(HXH=Z\\), \\(HZH=X\\), and \\(HIH=I\\) to show that \\(HR_x(\\theta)H=R_z(\\theta)\\), where \\(R_z(\\theta)=\\cos\\frac\\theta2I-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2Z\\). Write \\(R_z(\\theta)\\) as a diagonal matrix and show that it equals the phase operation \\(R_\\theta\\) up to a global phase. What does this say about the circuit \\(H\\), \\(R_\\phi\\), \\(H\\)? Since \\(H(aI+bX)H=aHIH+bHXH=aI+bZ\\), we get \\(HR_x(\\theta)H=\\cos\\frac\\theta2I-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2Z=R_z(\\theta)\\). On the diagonal, \\(R_z(\\theta)=\\mathrm{diag}(e^{-i\\theta/2},e^{i\\theta/2})=e^{-i\\theta/2}\\,\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})=e^{-i\\theta/2}R_\\theta\\). Hence \\(HR_\\phi H=e^{i\\phi/2}HR_z(\\phi)H=e^{i\\phi/2}R_x(\\phi)\\), because \\(H\\) undoes itself. The interference circuit is, up to global phase, a single rotation about the \\(X\\) direction by \\(\\phi\\): from \\(\\ket0\\) it gives \\(p(1)=\\sin^2\\frac\\phi2=\\frac{1-\\cos\\phi}{2}\\), as found in Section 5.2.3. Phase changes and rotations are the same operations seen in different bases."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § tensor product · product basis The memory of a multi-qubit computation is a register. For \\(n\\) qubits, its basis labels are the \\(n\\)-bit strings; a pure state assigns an amplitude to each label. The amplitudes have total squared magnitude one. A specified input can occupy some qubits while extra working qubits, called ancillas , start in \\(\\ket0\\). Their initialization and later operations are part of the computation. A register of two qubits has four configurations, \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), so its state has four amplitudes. Suppose we prepare the first qubit in \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and, independently, the second in \\(c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\). What are the four joint amplitudes? Reaching the configuration \\(01\\) means that the first qubit is \\(0\\) and the second is \\(1\\). The two preparations are independent, so by the product rule the amplitude is the product of the two local amplitudes, \\(ad\\). Ordering the configurations with the first qubit as the leftmost bit, \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{pmatrix}\\otimes\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\d\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix}.\\] This combination is the tensor product . For basis states it gives \\(\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b=\\ket{ab}\\); for example, \\(\\ket0\\otimes\\ket1=\\ket{01}\\). The four vectors \\(\\ket{ab}\\) form the product basis . Two qubits need four amplitudes and three qubits need eight: dimensions multiply rather than add. In a circuit we now draw one wire for each qubit, with the first qubit above the second. Separate gate boxes act independently on their own wires; a box spanning both wires specifies a joint operation. Later diagrams may combine several wires into one register wire, marked by a diagonal slash. That shorthand still represents all of the qubits, not one larger-amplitude qubit. A and B are single-qubit preparation unitaries. Their independent actions give the four joint amplitudes ac, ad, bc, bd; each box can require several elementary gates. Problems Compute \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigr)^T\\otimes(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac35,\\tfrac45\\bigr)^T\\otimes(0,1)^T\\). Compute \\((H\\otimes I)\\ket{01}\\) and \\((I\\otimes H)\\ket{01}\\). Show that \\(\\braket{a\\otimes b}{c\\otimes d}=\\braket ac\\braket bd\\) for vectors of two coordinates, and deduce that the tensor product of two unit vectors is a unit vector."}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Product states § product state Which joint states arise from independent preparations? A joint state of the form \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\) is a product state . Its amplitudes are products of local amplitudes, so its probabilities are products of local probabilities: the two qubits behave independently. Does a superposition of several joint configurations signal something more? Not necessarily. The state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) has two nonzero amplitudes, yet \\[\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0:\\] the first qubit is in an equal superposition, and the second is certainly \\(0\\). The real question is whether one local vector for each qubit reproduces every joint amplitude. When it does, the state is a product state. H acts only on the first wire; the second remains zero. The output is a product despite having two nonzero joint amplitudes. Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\) a product state? If so, factor it. Factor \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\), and find the probability that the second qubit gives \\(0\\). Show that a two-qubit state with amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{00},\\alpha_{01},\\alpha_{10},\\alpha_{11}\\) is a product state exactly when \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}\\)."}, {"title": "Joint amplitudes", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Joint amplitudes § composition postulate Chapter 4 introduced the composition postulate. Here it gives the allowed memory states of a computation. The product rule tells us how independently prepared systems combine. Must every joint state be a product? Quantum mechanics does not require it. It takes the whole tensor-product space as the state space of the pair. Composition. The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the state spaces of its parts. Its basis vectors are the products of basis vectors of the parts. For two qubits we write \\(\\ket\\Psi=\\alpha_{00}\\ket{00}+\\alpha_{01}\\ket{01}+\\alpha_{10}\\ket{10}+\\alpha_{11}\\ket{11}\\), where \\(\\sum_{a,b}|\\alpha_{ab}|^2=1\\). These four amplitudes need not have the product form \\(ac,ad,bc,bd\\). Gates update the joint list, and measurement samples a label from it. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis vectors, and a general state needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. Does a measurement reveal \\(2^n\\) numbers? No: measuring all \\(n\\) qubits gives a single \\(n\\)-bit label. Storing a general \\(n\\)-qubit state on a classical computer takes up to \\(2^n\\) amplitudes, which quickly becomes impractical. Does that by itself make quantum computers powerful? No. An advantage needs a task, and a procedure that makes its answer observable efficiently. A joint unitary updates four amplitudes on two qubits, but measurement returns a single pair of bits. U denotes the complete operation, not a free elementary gate. Problems How many amplitudes describe a general state of \\(10\\) qubits? How many bits does a measurement of all \\(10\\) qubits return? A classical simulation stores each amplitude as two \\(8\\)-byte numbers. How much memory does a general \\(40\\)-qubit state need? A product state of \\(n\\) qubits is described by \\(n\\) pairs of amplitudes. Show that applying one one-qubit operation to each qubit of a product state can be simulated with work proportional to \\(n\\). What does this show about the size of the state space as a source of difficulty?"}, {"title": "Local and controlled gates", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-1-4", "text": "6.1.4 Local and controlled gates § local gate · controlled gate · CNOT · circuit depth How does an operation on the first qubit alone act on the pair? It changes the first label and leaves the second unchanged: each arrow of \\(U\\) from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) becomes, for each value \\(b\\) of the second label, an arrow from \\(ib\\) to \\(jb\\) with the same weight, \\(\\bra{jb}U\\otimes I\\ket{ib}=\\bra jU\\ket i\\). We write this operation \\(U\\otimes I\\). On a product state, \\((U\\otimes I)(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi)=(U\\ket\\psi)\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). A multi-qubit circuit has one wire for each qubit. A gate on one wire acts on that qubit and as the identity on the others; gates on separate qubits can act in parallel when the hardware permits. A joint gate connects the wires on which it acts. Controlled-NOT, or CNOT, leaves the control label unchanged and flips the target exactly when the control label is one. With the first qubit as control, \\[\\operatorname{CNOT}\\ket{a,b}=\\ket{a,b\\oplus a},\\qquad a,b\\in\\{0,1\\}.\\] Here \\(\\oplus\\) is exclusive OR, or addition modulo two. The circuit symbol is a filled dot on the control wire joined vertically to a circled plus on the target wire. The basis-state rule extends linearly to a superposition; the dot is not a measurement. In the order \\(00,01,10,11\\), CNOT exchanges the last two configurations: \\[\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha_{00}\\\\\\alpha_{01}\\\\\\alpha_{10}\\\\\\alpha_{11}\\end{pmatrix}\\longmapsto\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha_{00}\\\\\\alpha_{01}\\\\\\alpha_{11}\\\\\\alpha_{10}\\end{pmatrix}.\\] No amplitude is lost, and applying the exchange twice restores the input. CNOT is unitary and its own inverse. More generally, a controlled \\(U\\) leaves the target unchanged on the control-zero alternatives and applies \\(U\\) on the control-one alternatives. The operations on individual wires are called local gates . For a first calculation, start with \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) on the first wire and \\(X\\) on the second. The result is \\[(H\\otimes X)\\ket{00}=\\frac{\\ket{01}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Measuring both wires now returns 01 or 11, each with probability one half. Apply CNOT before measuring instead: 01 stays 01 while 11 becomes 10, so the answers are now 01 and 10. The circuit has changed a definite second bit into a bit that is always opposite the first. The complete unitary is \\(U=\\operatorname{CNOT}(H\\otimes X)\\): the rightmost operation acts first. Its implementation uses two qubits and three gates. The independent \\(H\\) and \\(X\\) gates can share a time step, followed by CNOT, giving two gate layers before measurement. This number of successive layers is the circuit depth , assuming these gates are available directly. An arbitrary operation on the whole register is not counted as one elementary gate. Read left to right. The aligned single-qubit gates act on separate wires; the vertical CNOT connects control and target. Each M measures one qubit, and double lines carry classical output bits. Problems Apply CNOT to \\((\\ket{00}+i\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). What are the measurement probabilities? In the circuit above, move CNOT before both local gates. Starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), calculate the output and compare the measured bit pairs. Can local gates alone turn a product state into an entangled state? Use \\((U\\otimes V)(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi)\\) to justify your answer."}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Bell state Start from \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first qubit as control. What happens? \\(H\\) gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), still a product state. CNOT acts on each configuration: it leaves \\(00\\) alone and sends \\(10\\) to \\(11\\): \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The result is a Bell state , written \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running from left to right. Does the vertical line of CNOT mean that the first qubit is measured and the result used to decide whether to flip the second? No. CNOT acts on both configurations of the superposition at once, without any measurement. That is why the result is a superposition of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), rather than one of them. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Predict first. Reset to \\(\\ket{00}\\). After \\(H\\) on the first qubit, which two outcomes are possible? Which outcomes remain after CNOT? Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: \\(\\ket{00}\\) Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture. After trying it: compare your explanation After \\(H\\), \\(00\\) and \\(10\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). CNOT changes \\(10\\) to \\(11\\), so the final outcomes are \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). The bars alone do not establish entanglement. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit of \\(\\ket{10}\\), then CNOT with the first qubit as control. Which state results? Apply the same circuit to \\(\\ket{01}\\) and to \\(\\ket{11}\\). Show that the four states produced from \\(\\ket{00},\\ket{01},\\ket{10},\\ket{11}\\) form an orthonormal basis, and give a circuit that turns each of them back into its label."}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § entangled Try to factor the Bell state as \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\). Matching coefficients requires \\[ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2,\\qquad ad=bc=0.\\] The first pair makes all four factors nonzero; the second pair then cannot hold. The state is entangled : this pure joint state is not a product of pure local states. We can still predict every measurement on either qubit by using the joint amplitudes. What fails is assigning each qubit its own pure-state vector and multiplying the two vectors to recover the pair. We do not need a new mathematical representation to carry out the computations here. Do correlated zero–one results establish entanglement ? No. Preparing \\(00\\) or \\(11\\) according to a fair classical coin gives the same computational-basis counts. We need a measurement that distinguishes that mixture from the coherent Bell superposition. Before CNOT the two wires have a product state. After it, the coefficients of 00 and 11 are nonzero while those of 01 and 10 vanish; no product of two local vectors has these coefficients. Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\) entangled? For which real numbers \\(\\varepsilon\\) is \\((\\ket{00}+\\varepsilon\\ket{11})/\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon^2}\\) a product state? For a two-qubit state in which both outcomes of the first qubit are possible, measure the first qubit in the computational basis. Show that the state is entangled exactly when the conditional state of the second qubit depends on the outcome."}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § joint probability · marginal · conditional probability Although neither qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) has an individual pure-state vector, each still has well-defined local measurement probabilities. How do we read probabilities from a joint state? With amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), the joint probability is \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The probability that the first qubit gives \\(a\\), whatever the second gives, is the marginal \\(p(a)=\\sum_bp(a,b)\\), and the conditional probability of \\(b\\) given \\(a\\) is \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\) when \\(p(a)\\gt 0\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\); each qubit alone gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); and once the first result is known, the second is certain. If only the first qubit is measured and its outcome is \\(a\\), keep the terms with that first label and divide by \\(\\sqrt{p(a)}\\). For example, measuring the first qubit of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt3\\) gives 0 with probability \\(2/3\\); conditional on that outcome the state becomes \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\). Measuring part of a register therefore uses the same projection and normalization rule as Chapter 4. To test coherence, apply \\(H\\) to each qubit before measuring. Recall \\(\\ket+=H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket-=H\\ket1=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). This procedure is called measurement in the \\(\\pm\\) basis: an input \\(\\ket+\\) gives 0 certainly, and \\(\\ket-\\) gives 1 certainly. For the Bell state, the two contributions are \\[(H\\otimes H)\\ket{00}=\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2,\\qquad (H\\otimes H)\\ket{11}=\\frac{\\ket{00}-\\ket{01}-\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2.\\] Adding them with the initial factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) cancels 01 and 10, leaving \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) again. The results still agree with certainty. For the classical mixture, each run starts in either 00 or 11, and either preparation gives all four results with probability \\(1/4\\) after the two Hadamards. We average those probabilities; there is no addition of amplitudes between different runs. The output depends on both the joint state and the gates before readout. A single basis measurement misses the coherence that this second circuit exposes. The upper circuit measures a Bell pair directly. The lower circuit first applies H on both wires. Both give matching bits; the corresponding classical mixture gives all four pairs in the lower circuit. Problems For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), compute \\(p(\\text{first}=1)\\) and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=1)\\). For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt3\\), compute the joint probabilities, both marginal distributions, and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=0)\\). Compare the Bell state and an equally weighted classical mixture of 00 and 11 after applying H to both qubits and measuring. Why must you add amplitudes in one calculation and probabilities in the other?"}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Basis-state copying § Can a quantum state be copied? CNOT seems to copy. With a blank target, \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\to\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\to\\ket1\\ket1\\). What does it do to a superposition? By linearity, \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\to\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}.\\] Two copies would instead be \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha^2\\ket{00}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{01}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{10}+\\beta^2\\ket{11}.\\] These differ whenever \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are both nonzero. CNOT copies the labels \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), not superpositions of them. CNOT copies a basis label into a blank target. For a superposition the joint output is entangled, rather than two independent copies. Problems Apply CNOT to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\) and to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket1\\). For which states \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) does CNOT turn \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\) into two copies, \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\)? A unitary \\(U\\) copies both basis states: \\(U\\ket0\\ket0=\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(U\\ket1\\ket0=\\ket1\\ket1\\). Show that, whatever \\(U\\) does on other inputs, it sends \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0\\) to an entangled state."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § no-cloning theorem Could some cleverer unitary copy every state? Suppose \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\) for every state \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Take two states \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(\\ket\\phi\\). A unitary preserves inner products, and the inner product of two product states is the product of the inner products of their parts, so \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi\\braket00=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2,\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2.\\] The overlap must therefore be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). For two different states that are not orthogonal, such as \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) with overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), this fails. No unitary copies every state. This is the no-cloning theorem . What does the theorem not forbid? States known to belong to an orthogonal set, such as basis labels, can be copied, and a state that we know how to prepare can simply be prepared again. What is impossible is a single fixed operation that perfectly copies an arbitrary unknown state. U? is a hypothetical universal copying unitary, not an available gate. The required output overlap differs from the input overlap, so the two requested actions cannot belong to one unitary. Problems Compute \\(\\braket0+\\) and \\(\\braket{00}{++}\\). Why do these values rule out a unitary that copies both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\)? Show that a unitary can copy each of the two states \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) into a register prepared in \\(\\ket0\\). Give one. A cloning machine may change its own state: \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0\\ket e=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\ket{e_\\psi}\\), where the final machine state \\(\\ket{e_\\psi}\\) may depend on \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Show that it can still clone two states only if they are orthogonal or equal up to phase."}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § no-signalling · local unitary · conditional Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). If Alice measures her qubit in the computational basis, her outcomes occur equally often. Given her outcome, Bob has the matching basis state. Without her message, his probability of 0 is still \\(\\tfrac12(1)+\\tfrac12(0)=1/2\\). Alice cannot choose which result she obtains. Checking only this one measurement would leave a loophole: perhaps Bob could apply a gate before readout and detect her choice. We can close it using the same amplitude rules. Write any joint state as \\(\\sum_{a,b}\\alpha_{ab}\\ket{a,b}\\). For each fixed Bob label \\(b\\), his probability is the squared length of the amplitude list \\((\\alpha_{0b},\\alpha_{1b})\\). An Alice-only unitary multiplies this list by her unitary, preserving its squared length. Thus it leaves every Bob probability unchanged. If Bob first applies any local gate \\(V\\), perform that step first in the calculation. Alice’s and Bob’s gates act on different labels, so their order does not matter. The same norm argument then proves that Alice cannot change his probabilities for this readout either. An unreported computational-basis measurement by Alice also leaves those probabilities unchanged: it separates the two values of \\(a\\) into alternatives, and averaging their probabilities gives the same sum \\(\\sum_a|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). A measurement in another basis is a local unitary followed by this measurement, so it cannot help her signal. The conclusion concerns Bob’s unconditioned statistics; a message reporting Alice’s outcome lets him select a conditional branch. This is no-signalling for the local gates and projective measurements used here. Correlations become useful for communication only when supplemented by an ordinary message. Appendix D extends the argument to more general local operations. H and CNOT prepare the shared Bell pair. Alice’s local gate UA cannot affect Bob’s outcome probabilities, even when Bob chooses a different readout gate V. Problems Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Alice measures her qubit and does not report the result. What is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) in a computational-basis measurement? Alice applies \\(X\\) to her qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), without measuring. Find the new joint state and Bob’s computational-basis probabilities. Alice measures her half of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) in either the computational basis or the \\(\\pm\\) basis, without reporting her result. Bob tests for any normalized state \\(\\ket v=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\). Show that his probability of success is \\(1/2\\) for either choice. Why is this stronger than checking only Bob’s computational-basis outcomes?"}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds a qubit in an unknown state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and wants Bob to have it. She cannot copy it, and measuring it would reveal only one bit. What can she do if they share a Bell pair? The three qubits start in \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\); Alice holds the first two and Bob the third. Alice applies CNOT from her input qubit to her half of the Bell pair, then \\(H\\) to her input qubit. The Bell pair must already have been distributed; no qubit is sent during the subsequent protocol. In the order input, Alice’s half, Bob’s half, the initial expansion is \\((\\alpha\\ket{000}+\\alpha\\ket{011}+\\beta\\ket{100}+\\beta\\ket{111})/\\sqrt2\\). CNOT changes it to \\((\\alpha\\ket{000}+\\alpha\\ket{011}+\\beta\\ket{110}+\\beta\\ket{101})/\\sqrt2\\). Applying \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(H\\ket1=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) on the first label now gives the four branches below. The three wires are Alice’s input, Alice’s half of the shared pair, and Bob’s half. Alice operates on her first two wires; the dashed classical records later select Bob’s corrections. Problems In teleportation, how many qubits does each party hold at the start? Write the initial three-qubit state for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\). For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\), apply Alice’s CNOT and \\(H\\), and group the result by the configuration of Alice’s two qubits. Suppose Alice sends Bob only the bit \\(m\\), and Bob applies \\(Z^m\\). Show that for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\) his qubit is wrong with probability \\(1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § teleportation Write Alice’s unknown input as \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\). After her CNOT and Hadamard, grouping the three-qubit state by her two basis labels gives \\[\\frac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}\\ket\\psi+\\ket{01}X\\ket\\psi+\\ket{10}Z\\ket\\psi+\\ket{11}XZ\\ket\\psi\\bigr).\\] For example, the 01 branch contains \\(\\alpha\\ket1+\\beta\\ket0=X\\ket\\psi\\); the 11 branch contains \\(\\alpha\\ket1-\\beta\\ket0=XZ\\ket\\psi\\). The first label records Alice’s input-wire measurement, and the second records her Bell-half measurement. This identity is the protocol. Every branch has squared norm \\(1/4\\), regardless of \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\). Alice measures her two qubits, sends their values \\((m,n)\\), and Bob receives the conditional state \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\). Alice’s bits Bob’s conditional state Bob applies, in time order 00 \\(\\ket\\psi\\) Nothing 01 \\(X\\ket\\psi\\) \\(X\\) 10 \\(Z\\ket\\psi\\) \\(Z\\) 11 \\(XZ\\ket\\psi\\) \\(X\\), then \\(Z\\) The correction works because \\(Z^mX^nX^nZ^m=I\\). Nothing requires Bob to know the amplitudes. He only needs to know which reversible disturbance to undo. All four outcomes work; teleportation does not wait for a lucky one. To check the identity, expand the initial state \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), apply Alice’s two gates to each term, and collect equal first-two-bit labels. The next problems let you test the same mechanism on specific inputs. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left. Problems Alice obtains \\((m,n)=(1,0)\\). Which state does Bob hold, and which correction does he apply? For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket+\\) and Alice’s result \\((1,1)\\), write Bob’s state before correction, and check that the correction restores \\(\\ket+\\). Bob applies his corrections in the wrong order: \\(Z^m\\) first, then \\(X^n\\). Show that he still obtains \\(\\ket\\psi\\), up to a global phase."}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Before the classical bits arrive, Bob averages probabilities over the four equally likely states \\(\\ket\\psi,X\\ket\\psi,Z\\ket\\psi,XZ\\ket\\psi\\). His probability of 0 is \\((|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2+|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2)/4=1/2\\). In fact every normalized test state \\(\\ket v=c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\) has probability \\(1/2\\): pairing the two opposite signs in these four branches cancels cross terms, leaving \\((|c|^2+|d|^2)(|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2)/2\\). No choice of local readout reveals the input. His qubit is already present, but he cannot use it as the recovered input or learn the input’s amplitudes from it. The useful transfer waits for the message. Nor is there a second copy left with Alice. Her measured qubits are basis states, and the shared Bell pair has been consumed. Conditioned on the communicated outcome and Bob’s correction, the unknown input is now at Bob. Keep three descriptions separate: the full joint state, Bob’s state given Alice’s result, and Bob’s state when that result is unknown. Confusing them makes teleportation look like instantaneous communication or cloning. Tracking them turns it into an explicit calculation with the rules we already have. We now have the multi-qubit model in working form: initialize a register, update its joint amplitudes with local and controlled gates, measure selected wires, and use classical records for later decisions. So far the register has carried states from one place to another. Can it also compute? Alice’s measurements produce classical records that select Bob’s corrections. The quantum input is transferred after the message arrives; no second copy remains with Alice. Problems Before the classical bits arrive, what is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) when measuring his qubit, for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\)? Show that, before the bits arrive, Bob obtains \\(0\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) for every input \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\). After Bob’s correction, write the three-qubit state for a particular measurement result \\((m,n)\\). Do Alice’s qubits retain a second copy? Explain the scope of your conclusion."}, {"title": "Evaluation on every input", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-5-1", "text": "6.5.1 Evaluation on every input § quantum parallelism Teleportation used a register to move a state. Can a register also compute? Reversible computation already gave us a way to evaluate any Boolean function \\(f\\) without discarding information: keep the input \\(x\\) and add \\(f(x)\\) to a target bit. On the basis states of a quantum register this is the gate \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket y=\\ket x\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] For each input it either leaves the target alone or flips it, so it only rearranges configurations. It is unitary, and it can be built from Toffoli and CNOT gates at about the cost of evaluating \\(f\\) once, provided any workspace is cleared by uncomputation . What does \\(U_f\\) do when the input register is not a single string? Take two input qubits, both \\(\\ket0\\), and apply \\(H\\) to each: \\[(H\\otimes H)\\ket{00}=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}\\bigr).\\] Two gates spread equal amplitude over all four inputs. With \\(n\\) input qubits, \\(n\\) Hadamard gates give all \\(2^n\\) strings, each with amplitude \\(2^{-n/2}\\). The number of gates grows by one per qubit; the number of configurations doubles. Now add a target qubit in \\(\\ket0\\) and apply \\(U_f\\). The gate is defined by one arrow from each basis configuration, and each configuration carries its amplitude along its own arrow. So \\[U_f\\Bigl(\\tfrac12\\sum_x\\ket x\\Bigr)\\ket0=\\tfrac12\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}.\\] For a concrete case, let \\(f\\) be AND, so \\(f(x)=1\\) only for \\(x=11\\): \\[\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}\\ket0+\\ket{01}\\ket0+\\ket{10}\\ket0+\\ket{11}\\ket1\\bigr).\\] One application of \\(U_f\\) has produced a state in which every input appears beside its own value. This is quantum parallelism . Has the computer evaluated \\(f\\) four times for the price of once? The state holds four input–value pairs, but holding them is not the same as reading them. Two H gates spread the inputs over all four strings. One application of \\(U_f\\) then places each input beside its own value: \\(\\tfrac12\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\). Problems How many Hadamard gates prepare the uniform superposition of all inputs on five qubits? How many configurations does it contain, and what is the amplitude of each? Write the state that \\(U_f\\) produces from \\(\\tfrac12\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket0\\) when \\(f(x)=x_1\\oplus x_2\\). Can it be written as an input state times a target state? Start the target in \\(\\ket1\\) instead of \\(\\ket0\\). What state does \\(U_f\\) produce from the uniform input superposition, and does it carry more or less information about \\(f\\) than the state with target \\(\\ket0\\)?"}, {"title": "What a measurement reveals", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-5-2", "text": "6.5.2 What a measurement reveals § Suppose we measure every qubit of the AND state. Each configuration \\(\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\) has amplitude \\(1/2\\), so each of the four pairs \\((00,0)\\), \\((01,0)\\), \\((10,0)\\), and \\((11,1)\\) appears with probability \\(1/4\\). A run returns one input, chosen at random, together with its value. Afterwards the register is in the observed configuration, and the other three pairs are gone. To learn the whole table, we prepare and measure again. The inputs arrive at random, so four runs are the minimum and repeats make more runs likely. Evaluating \\(f\\) on the four inputs one at a time takes exactly four evaluations. With \\(n\\) inputs, the table has \\(2^n\\) entries, while a measurement of the \\(n+1\\) qubits yields \\(n+1\\) bits. Could we copy the state before measuring it and measure each copy? A device that copied every such state would copy states that are not orthogonal, which the no-cloning theorem forbids. Two different functions can give overlapping parallel states: for AND and for the constant zero function, the states agree in three of their four terms. What if we measure only the target? For AND it gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\), leaving the inputs in \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\), and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\), leaving \\(\\ket{11}\\). The target alone tells us one value that \\(f\\) takes, and the inputs that remain are exactly the strings with that value. That conditional state shows something else. Unless \\(f\\) is constant, the parallel state is entangled: it cannot be written as an input state times a target state, because the target is \\(\\ket0\\) beside some inputs and \\(\\ket1\\) beside others. The target keeps a record of which input occurred. Parallel evaluation alone produces a random sample of the table, not the table. Predict first. After \\(U_f\\) has acted on all four inputs of AND, measure every qubit. How many of the four values does one run reveal? About how many runs do you expect before the whole table is known? Reading a parallel evaluation Function \\(f\\) AND XOR first bit constant 0 random table Apply H to the inputs Measure every qubit Measure the target only Prepare again Run until every value is known Each row is an input string \\(x\\); the two columns are the target values. Bar length shows the size of the amplitude of \\(\\ket x\\ket y\\), and orange marks the configuration a measurement just returned. The table on the right records the values that runs have revealed. After \\(U_f\\), every input has its value beside it, yet each run reveals at most one of them. After trying it: compare your explanation One run reveals one input with its value, each pair with probability \\(1/4\\). Inputs arrive at random and repeat, so learning all four values takes \\(25/3\\approx8.3\\) runs on average, more than the four evaluations of a one-at-a-time classical procedure. Measuring only the target gives a value of \\(f\\) and leaves the inputs that share it. Problems After the parallel evaluation of AND, every qubit is measured. List the possible results with their probabilities. What is the state of the register after the result \\((10,0)\\)? Measure only the target after the parallel evaluation of XOR. Give both outcome probabilities and the conditional state of the inputs in each case. What have you learned about \\(f\\)? Each run of the parallel evaluation of a two-input function, followed by measuring every qubit, reveals one random pair \\((x,f(x))\\). What is the expected number of runs before all four values are known? Compare with evaluating \\(f\\) on each input in turn."}, {"title": "Global properties through interference", "chapter": "Multi-Qubit Computation", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#topic-6-5-3", "text": "6.5.3 Global properties through interference § global property If a run cannot deliver the table, what could it deliver? Many questions need much less than the table. Is \\(f\\) constant? Does it take the value \\(1\\) anywhere? Each answer is a single bit, but it depends on all the values together. Such an answer is a global property of \\(f\\). Interference combines contributions from all the inputs, so it is the natural tool. After \\(U_f\\), apply \\(H\\) to each input qubit again and measure the inputs. Each input string \\(x\\) sends amplitude \\(1/2\\) to the readout \\(00\\), so the readout \\(00\\) collects contributions from all four branches. For a constant function \\(f(x)=c\\), the parallel state is \\(\\tfrac12\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket c\\), a product of the uniform input state and \\(\\ket c\\). Applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) again returns the inputs to \\(\\ket{00}\\). The readout is \\(00\\) with certainty. For AND, the contributions to \\(00\\) do not all end at the same configuration. Three branches carry target \\(0\\) and contribute \\(\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12\\) each to \\(\\ket{00}\\ket0\\); the branch \\(x=11\\) carries target \\(1\\) and contributes \\(\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12\\) to \\(\\ket{00}\\ket1\\). Amplitudes add only at a common configuration, so \\[p(00)=\\Bigl(\\tfrac34\\Bigr)^2+\\Bigl(\\tfrac14\\Bigr)^2=\\tfrac58.\\] The readout \\(00\\) no longer separates the two cases reliably. The cause is the record the target keeps: branches with different target values sit at different configurations, and their contributions cannot cancel or reinforce one another. This is the path-information effect of interference, now produced by the computation itself. Quantum parallelism is therefore a resource only when a later interference step turns many values into one useful bit, and that step needs a target that keeps no record of the branch. Which global properties can be read out this way, and how must the target be prepared? Functions that can be consulted only through queries make these questions precise. After \\(U_f\\), H on each input combines contributions from all four inputs. A constant \\(f\\) returns the readout 00 with certainty; for AND the target’s record keeps the branches apart, and 00 has probability \\(5/8\\). Problems For a constant function \\(f(x)=c\\) on two inputs, show that applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) to the inputs after the parallel evaluation gives the readout \\(00\\) with certainty. Repeat the calculation for \\(f(x)=x_1\\), the first input bit. Find the probability of the readout \\(00\\). Why can contributions that end with different target values not cancel? Use this to explain why the readout \\(00\\) does not reliably separate a constant \\(f\\) from AND, and say what the target would need to do instead."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\) entangled? \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=0\\) but \\(\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}=1/3\\), so the state is not a product: it is entangled."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-2", "text": "Two qubits are prepared independently: the first in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), the second in \\(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\ket0+\\frac12\\ket1\\). Write the four joint amplitudes, the probability that the two measured bits agree, and the marginal probability that the second bit is \\(0\\). The amplitudes are \\(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt2}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt2}\\), \\(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt2}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt2}\\) for \\(00,01,10,11\\), with probabilities \\(\\frac38,\\frac18,\\frac38,\\frac18\\). The bits agree with probability \\(\\frac38+\\frac18=\\frac12\\). The second bit is \\(0\\) with probability \\(\\frac38+\\frac38=\\frac34\\), exactly its local probability: for a product state the marginals are the single-qubit probabilities, and the joint probabilities factor."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-3", "text": "Alice holds the first qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Find the joint state after she applies (a) \\(Z\\), (b) \\(X\\), (c) \\(Z\\) and then \\(X\\). Does any of these change Bob’s computational-basis probabilities? (a) \\(Z\\) gives \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). (b) \\(X\\) gives \\((\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\). (c) \\(Z\\) then \\(X\\) gives \\((\\ket{10}-\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\). Together with \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) these are four orthogonal states, so a local gate by Alice changes the joint state in a way a joint measurement could detect. Bob’s computational-basis probabilities stay \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\) in every case, as no-signalling requires: the change is visible only by comparing both qubits."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-4", "text": "Prepare \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) from \\(\\ket{00}\\) using X, H, and CNOT. Calculate the outcomes if H is then applied to both qubits before measurement. X on both wires gives 11. H on the first gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and CNOT gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). Applying H on both sends this to \\((\\ket{10}-\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\), differing only by a global minus sign. Outcomes 01 and 10 each occur with probability one half."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-5", "text": "Measure the first qubit of \\(\\ket\\Psi=\\frac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+i\\ket{11})\\). Give each outcome probability and the conditional state of the second qubit. Is \\(\\ket\\Psi\\) entangled? Then, for each first outcome, find the probability that a \\(\\pm\\)-basis measurement of the second qubit (apply \\(H\\), then measure) gives \\(0\\). Each first outcome has probability \\(\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12\\). After outcome \\(0\\) the second qubit is \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\); after outcome \\(1\\) it is \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). The conditional state depends on the outcome, so \\(\\ket\\Psi\\) is entangled; consistently, \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=\\frac i4\\neq\\frac14=\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}\\). In the \\(\\pm\\) test, the first conditional state gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(1\\); the second gives \\(|(1+i)/2|^2=\\frac12\\). The computational-basis statistics of the second qubit are the same for both outcomes, \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\). The entanglement shows up only in a measurement sensitive to relative phase."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-6", "text": "Prepare three qubits from \\(\\ket{000}\\) with \\(H\\) on the first qubit, CNOT from the first to the second, and CNOT from the second to the third. (a) Track the state. (b) Apply \\(H\\) to all three qubits and find which outcomes occur and with what probabilities. (c) Compare with a classical mixture of \\(000\\) and \\(111\\) prepared by a fair coin. (a) \\(\\ket{000}\\to\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{000}+\\ket{100})\\to\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{000}+\\ket{110})\\to\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{000}+\\ket{111})\\). (b) The amplitude of outcome \\(z\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt2}\\big(1+(-1)^{z_1+z_2+z_3}\\big)\\). It is \\(\\frac12\\) when \\(z\\) has an even number of ones and \\(0\\) otherwise. So the four outcomes \\(000,011,101,110\\) each occur with probability \\(\\frac14\\), and the outcomes with odd parity never occur. (c) For the mixture, each preparation gives all eight outcomes with probability \\(\\frac18\\), and so does their average. In the computational basis the two preparations look identical, but after the Hadamards the coherent state shows a strict parity rule that the mixture lacks."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-7", "text": "CNOT copies \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) into a blank target. Use it on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\) and test the result as if it were two copies of \\(\\ket+\\): apply \\(H\\) to both qubits and measure. What fraction of runs pass the test (outcome \\(00\\)), and what fraction would pass for genuine copies \\(\\ket+\\ket+\\)? CNOT gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2=\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) returns \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) (Section 6.2.3), so the measurement gives \\(00\\) or \\(11\\), each with probability \\(\\frac12\\). The test passes in half the runs. Genuine copies \\(\\ket+\\ket+\\) become \\(\\ket{00}\\) and would pass every time. CNOT is a perfect copier for the basis states and fails for \\(\\ket+\\): it produces an entangled pair, not two independent copies. No-cloning says this failure is unavoidable for any single device that copies both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\)."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reasons: “Each qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\), so each qubit by itself is in the state \\(\\ket+\\).” Test this by computing the probability that the first qubit alone gives \\(0\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis (apply \\(H\\) to it, then measure). What would \\(\\ket+\\) predict? Can any single-qubit pure state reproduce the first qubit’s statistics? \\((H\\otimes I)\\ket{\\Phi^+}=\\frac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})\\), so the first qubit gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12\\). The state \\(\\ket+\\) would give \\(0\\) with certainty. The first qubit gives \\(\\frac12,\\frac12\\) in both the computational and the \\(\\pm\\) basis, and the same holds for every other test. A pure state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) always has a test with a certain result, namely the one that checks for \\(\\ket\\psi\\) itself. So no pure state describes half of a Bell pair: its statistics match a fair coin in every basis. The precise description of such a part needs the mixed-state tools of Appendix D."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-9", "text": "Teleport \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). For each of Alice’s four outcomes \\((m,n)\\), give Bob’s state before correction and the correction he applies. \\((0,0)\\): \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), no correction. \\((0,1)\\): \\((i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\). \\((1,0)\\): \\((\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(Z\\). \\((1,1)\\): \\((-i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\) and then \\(Z\\). In every case the result is \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Multi-Qubit Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-composite-systems.html#exercise-10", "text": "Repeat the parallel evaluation of Section 6.5 with the target prepared in \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) instead of \\(\\ket0\\). (a) Show that \\(U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-\\). (b) Apply \\(H\\otimes H\\) to the inputs and compute \\(p(00)\\) for a constant \\(f\\), for \\(f(x)=x_1\\), and for AND. (c) Why does the target \\(\\ket-\\) succeed where \\(\\ket0\\) failed? (a) If \\(f(x)=0\\), the target is untouched. If \\(f(x)=1\\), it is flipped, and \\(\\frac{\\ket1-\\ket0}{\\sqrt2}=-\\ket-\\). So \\(U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-\\). (b) Starting from the uniform input superposition, the state after \\(U_f\\) is \\(\\frac12\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\otimes\\ket-\\), and the readout \\(00\\) has amplitude \\(\\frac14\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\). Constant \\(f\\): \\(\\pm1\\), so \\(p(00)=1\\). \\(f(x)=x_1\\): two \\(+1\\) and two \\(-1\\), so \\(p(00)=0\\). AND: \\(\\frac14(3-1)=\\frac12\\), so \\(p(00)=\\frac14\\). (c) The target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) on every branch, so it keeps no record of the input. The function values move into signs on the input branches, and those signs can reinforce or cancel at the common readout. Chapter 7 develops this effect, phase kickback."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § constant · balanced · promise Here is a small question about a function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\): is \\(f(0)\\) equal to \\(f(1)\\)? If they are equal, \\(f\\) is constant ; if they differ, \\(f\\) is balanced . For a one-bit input, every function is one or the other. What do we actually need to learn? One bit: equal or different. We do not need the values \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) themselves. Can an algorithm learn that one bit without learning both values? The gap between the answer we want and the full data is what the quantum algorithm will exploit. For inputs of more bits, a function need not be constant or balanced. Restricting attention to those two cases is then a promise about the input. The following circuits represent the four functions while retaining the input bit. The first wire holds \\(x\\); the second holds \\(y\\), which is replaced by \\(y\\oplus f(x)\\). With \\(y=0\\), the second wire ends at \\(f(x)\\). This reversible form lets us compare ordinary basis-input queries with coherent quantum queries; Section 7.1.3 defines the oracle access precisely. The four reversible circuits encode the four Boolean functions. They retain x and update y by XOR with f(x). This anticipates the reversible query definition in 7.1.3. Problems List the four functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) as pairs \\((f(0),f(1))\\), and classify each as constant or balanced. Among the functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), how many are constant, how many are balanced (output \\(1\\) on exactly half the inputs), and how many are neither? For \\(f\\) on \\(n\\)-bit inputs, promised to be constant or balanced, show that a deterministic classical algorithm can decide which with \\(2^{n-1}+1\\) queries, and that \\(2^{n-1}\\) queries do not always suffice."}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 Classical query access § query Suppose the only access to \\(f\\) is a query : we choose \\(x\\) and receive \\(f(x)\\). How many queries does a classical algorithm need to answer with certainty? After one query, say with the reply \\(f(0)=0\\), both possibilities remain: \\(f(1)=0\\) makes \\(f\\) constant, and \\(f(1)=1\\) makes it balanced. One query cannot settle the question; two clearly suffice. This count belongs to the stated access model. If \\(f\\) were given in another form, for instance as a formula we could inspect, the comparison could change. Two separate basis-input runs of the reversible oracle reproduce classical value-query access. Each target is measured to read a function value; no superposition is used. Problems A classical algorithm queries \\(f(1)\\) and receives \\(1\\). Which of the four functions remain possible? For \\(f\\) on two-bit inputs, promised constant or balanced, an algorithm queries \\(f(00)\\) and \\(f(01)\\). If they differ it answers “balanced”; otherwise it makes one more query. Complete the algorithm, and give its number of queries in the worst case. Show that no one-query classical algorithm for \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), even one that chooses its query at random, is correct with probability greater than \\(1/2\\) on every function."}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § oracle · black-box How can a quantum computation query \\(f\\)? A query must be a valid operation, hence unitary , while \\(f\\) itself may merge its inputs. The reversible embedding of a function solves this: keep the input and add \\(f(x)\\) to a target bit. \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,\\,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On the four configurations, \\(U_f\\) is a rearrangement: it exchanges \\((x,0)\\) and \\((x,1)\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\) and leaves them alone otherwise. Its matrix is a permutation matrix, so it is unitary, and applying it twice restores every configuration. What counts as one query? One use of \\(U_f\\), whatever state it is applied to, including a superposition . This is the black-box , or oracle , model: it counts the uses of \\(U_f\\) and ignores how \\(U_f\\) is built. The construction costs return when circuits are analysed in detail. The box acts on both wires and retains the input label. One use counts as one query in the specified oracle model. Problems Write the action of \\(U_f\\) on the four basis states for \\(f=(1,0)\\), that is, \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f(1)=0\\). Write the matrix of \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(0,1)\\) in the order \\(00,01,10,11\\), and check that it is unitary. Show that the map \\(\\ket{x,y}\\mapsto\\ket{x,f(x)}\\), which overwrites the target, is not unitary for any \\(f\\), and that \\(\\ket x\\mapsto\\ket{f(x)}\\) is not unitary when \\(f\\) is constant."}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § A query is a reversible evaluation, so quantum parallelism applies to it. What happens if we query \\(f\\) on both inputs at once? Prepare the input in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and the target in \\(\\ket0\\). By linearity, \\(U_f\\) acts on each configuration separately: \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Have we computed both values? Both appear in the state, but, as with any parallel evaluation, nothing has read them. Whether this helps depends on what we do next. H prepares both input alternatives coherently. The oracle acts on their joint superposition; it has not supplied two classical answers. Problems Apply \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(1,1)\\) to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\). For each of the four functions, write \\(U_f\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\bigr)\\), and say which results are entangled. With \\(n\\) input qubits in the uniform superposition and a clean target, \\(U_f\\) produces \\(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\). Measuring both registers gives a uniformly random \\(x\\) with \\(f(x)\\). About how many runs are needed, on average, before every value \\(f(x)\\) has been seen?"}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Register measurement § Measure the joint state immediately after the query and you obtain one pair \\((x,f(x))\\), chosen at random. The other function value does not appear as a second answer in the detector. Repeating the preparation obtains more samples, but a classical method can query randomly chosen inputs too. Merely placing both function values in a state has not produced an advantage. The useful target is narrower: make “equal” and “different” lead to distinguishable final states. The remaining gates must act before measurement, while there are still coherent amplitudes to combine. Both wires are measured after one query. A run returns either (0,f(0)) or (1,f(1)), each with probability one half. Problems Measure both registers of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). What are the possible results and their probabilities? How many function values does one run reveal? To decide constant or balanced, we repeatedly prepare \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) and measure both registers until both inputs have been seen. How many runs does this take on average? Show that measuring only the input register of \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each for all four functions."}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Workspace entanglement § The blank target creates a problem. If \\(f(0)\\ne f(1)\\), it holds an orthogonal record of which input branch occurred. Applying \\(H\\) to the input alone gives a random computational-basis outcome; the two contributions now end with different target labels and cannot cancel each other. This is the path-record example from Chapter 3 in a new setting. The question is no longer whether we can evaluate the function. It is whether we can use the evaluation without keeping a record that spoils the readout. We need a target preparation that remains the same physical state under either possible update, while still allowing the updates to contribute different phases. Eigenstates of the target flip provide exactly that possibility. The target starts at zero and can retain the input branch. For a balanced function, the final H on the input cannot make its outcome definite. Problems For \\(f=(0,1)\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and find the probability of each input outcome. For the constant function \\(f=(0,0)\\), repeat the calculation: start from \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\), apply \\(U_f\\), then \\(H\\) to the input. Combining the two previous problems, design a one-query procedure with a clean target that never wrongly answers “balanced.” With what probability does it detect a balanced function?"}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The oracle either flips the target or leaves it alone. Which target states does a flip leave unchanged, apart from a factor? The eigenvectors of \\(X\\): \\[X\\ket+=\\ket+,\\qquad X\\ket-=-\\ket-,\\] since \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\). A flip therefore multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\), and no flip leaves it with the factor \\(+1\\): the target state stays the same while the factor records whether a flip occurred. The state \\(\\ket+\\) is unchanged in both cases and cannot tell them apart. The target we want is \\(\\ket-\\). X leaves the plus state unchanged and multiplies the minus state by a common minus sign. In a controlled operation that factor becomes a relative phase between control alternatives. Problems Compute \\(X(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Is \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) an eigenvector of \\(X\\)? Find every state that \\(X\\) leaves unchanged apart from a factor, by solving \\(X(a,b)^T=\\lambda(a,b)^T\\). The target is prepared in \\(\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\), with real \\(\\theta\\), and the oracle flips it. Show that its overlap with the original target is \\(\\sin2\\theta\\), and that the target keeps no record of the flip only when it is \\(\\ket+\\) or \\(\\ket-\\)."}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § phase kickback Apply \\(U_f\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket-\\). If \\(f(x)=0\\), nothing happens; if \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips, which multiplies the state by \\(-1\\): \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With the input in \\(\\ket+\\), \\[U_f\\ket+\\ket-=\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket-.\\] The target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) whatever \\(x\\) is, so it holds no record of the input. The function value has moved into the sign of the input amplitude . This is phase kickback . What do the signs mean? If \\(f\\) is constant, the two signs are equal and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket+\\); the common sign is a global phase . If \\(f\\) is balanced, the signs differ and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket-\\). The answer has become a relative phase . The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase. Problems For \\(f=(1,0)\\), compute \\(U_f\\ket+\\ket-\\). Prepare the input in \\(\\ket+\\), but mistakenly prepare the target in \\(\\ket+\\) too. Find the output of \\(U_f\\) for each of the four functions. Does leaving the target unentangled suffice to obtain useful phase kickback? With \\(n\\) input qubits and the target in \\(\\ket-\\), show that \\(U_f\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-=\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-\\), and that the target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § compute, phase, uncompute What if \\(f\\) is not given as an oracle but computed by a circuit \\(A\\) that uses workspace, \\(A\\ket{x,0,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\)? Apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, which multiplies the state by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\), then run \\(A\\) backward: \\[A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\ket{x,0,0}=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0,0}.\\] The workspace returns to \\(0\\), and the sign remains. This is compute, phase, uncompute . It is not free: it uses \\(A\\) and its inverse, and an honest count of the cost must include both. A computes the answer and workspace. Z changes the answer-one sign. The inverse A restores the answer and workspace to zero, leaving the input phase. Problems For \\(f(x)=x\\), computed into a clean answer bit by CNOT, compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on \\(\\ket{0,0}\\) and \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on all four basis states, and identify the operation. Why does compute, phase, uncompute need the workspace to start at \\(0\\)? Omit the uncomputation: compute \\(f(x)=x\\) into the answer bit with CNOT and apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, but do not run CNOT again. Starting from \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input at the end. Show that the outcome is random, whereas with uncomputation it is certain."}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Interference and readout § After the query, the input is \\((s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), with \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\). How do we read the relative sign? Apply \\(H\\), which sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\): \\[H\\,\\frac{s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{s_0+s_1}2\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}2\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give \\(0\\) with certainty; opposite signs give \\(1\\) with certainty. In terms of paths, the two contributions to the wrong answer cancel. The outcome depends only on whether \\(s_0\\) and \\(s_1\\) agree, never on \\(f(0)\\) or \\(f(1)\\) separately. We learn the one bit we wanted, and nothing more. The final H makes equal and opposite oracle signs distinguishable. M reads the parity of the two function values, not their individual values. Problems Compute \\(H\\bigl((-\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\bigr)\\). For each of the four functions, compute the final input amplitudes \\((s_0+s_1)/2\\) and \\((s_0-s_1)/2\\). Show that if the final \\(H\\) is omitted, or replaced by \\(X\\), the measurement gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each, for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Deutsch’s algorithm · query separation Prepare \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\), apply \\(H\\) to each qubit , query \\(U_f\\) once, apply \\(H\\) to the input, and measure that input. Outcome zero means constant; outcome one means balanced. This is Deutsch’s algorithm, with exact success in the ideal model. An exact classical value-query method needs two queries in the worst case. The comparison uses the same underlying function but different allowed access: classical queries supply a basis input and read a value; the quantum algorithm applies its specified reversible oracle coherently to a superposition. That access assumption is part of the model. The result is a one-versus-two query separation, not a claim that this tiny circuit runs faster on actual hardware. It matters because the entire mechanism is visible: choose a property, encode it in phase, and turn that phase into an observable answer. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Predict first. Compare a constant function with a balanced function. Before running the circuit, predict whether the final input bit is 0 or 1. Would complementing every function value change that bit? Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard. After trying it: compare your explanation A constant function gives 0; a balanced function gives 1. Complementing the function multiplies the state after the phase oracle by a common minus sign, so it does not change the final measurement. Problems Count the queries and the other operations in Deutsch’s algorithm, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Run Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(f=(1,0)\\), giving the state after each step. With \\(n\\) input qubits, after the query and \\(H\\) applied to each input qubit, show that the amplitude of the input configuration \\(0^n\\) is \\(2^{-n}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\). Deduce that one query distinguishes constant from balanced functions with certainty."}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § The circuit has four jobs: prepare coherent alternatives, encode the desired property, recombine the amplitudes, and read the result. A missing job gives a specific failure. A target starting in \\(\\ket0\\) can keep a branch record. A target starting in \\(\\ket+\\) keeps no such record, but contributes no distinguishing phase either. The choice \\(\\ket-\\) avoids both problems: it remains factored from the input while supplying the required signs. Without the final \\(H\\), those signs still give the same zero–one probabilities. Correctness therefore belongs to the whole preparation–query–readout chain, not to the oracle alone. Search will use the same phase-marking idea, but its readout needs repeated reflections to make a marked label likely. With target input 1, the first H prepares the minus state and the full circuit returns the desired parity. Starting the target at 0 instead makes a plus state and always yields zero. Omitting the final H gives random input-wire outcomes. Problems Name the four stages of Deutsch’s algorithm and the operations used in each. Replace the target \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(\\ket+\\), and run the algorithm for \\(f=(0,1)\\). What does the measurement give? Leave out the first \\(H\\) on the input, so that the input stays \\(\\ket0\\). Show that the algorithm then gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\) for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(f=(0,1)\\), what does \\(U_f\\) do to \\(\\ket1\\ket-\\)? The target flips, which multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\): \\(U_f\\ket1\\ket-=-\\ket1\\ket-\\). The function value appears as a sign, and the target is unchanged."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-2", "text": "A programmer prepares the target of Deutsch’s algorithm as \\(\\ket1\\) but forgets the Hadamard on it, so the query acts on \\(\\ket+\\ket1\\). For the balanced function \\(f=(0,1)\\), find the state after the query and the probability that the final input measurement gives the correct answer \\(1\\). The query gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0\\ket1+\\ket1\\ket0)\\): the target now records the input branch, exactly as with a blank target. After \\(H\\) on the input, the state is \\(\\frac12(\\ket0\\ket1+\\ket1\\ket1+\\ket0\\ket0-\\ket1\\ket0)\\), and the input reads \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12\\). The target \\(\\ket1\\) is a basis state, not an eigenstate of the flip, so the function value is written into the target instead of being kicked back as a sign. Only \\(\\ket-\\) works."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-3", "text": "For \\(n=3\\) input bits under the constant-or-balanced promise, how many queries does an exact deterministic classical algorithm need in the worst case? A randomized algorithm queries \\(k\\) inputs chosen uniformly at random (with replacement) and answers “constant” if all values agree. With what probability can it be wrong on a balanced function, and how large must \\(k\\) be for an error below \\(1\\%\\)? Deterministically, \\(2^{n-1}+1=5\\) queries are needed: after \\(4\\) equal answers the function could still be balanced. The randomized algorithm is never wrong on a constant function. On a balanced one, each query returns \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\), and all \\(k\\) agree with probability \\(2^{1-k}\\). That is below \\(0.01\\) for \\(k\\ge8\\). Deutsch–Jozsa uses one query and never errs, but against algorithms allowed a small error the classical cost stays constant as \\(n\\) grows. The size of a quantum advantage depends on which classical success criterion is used for comparison."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-4", "text": "Run the \\(n\\)-qubit version of Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(n=2\\) and the balanced function \\(f(x_1x_2)=x_1\\). Show that the outcome \\(00\\) never occurs. After the query, the input is \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})=\\ket-\\ket+\\). Applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\), so the outcome is \\(10\\), never \\(00\\). The function is identified as balanced with one query."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-5", "text": "With the target in \\(\\ket-\\), the query acts on the input as the phase oracle \\(O_f=\\mathrm{diag}\\big((-1)^{f(0)},(-1)^{f(1)}\\big)\\). Write \\(O_f\\) for each of the four functions, and compute \\(HO_fH\\) in each case. Use the result to describe Deutsch’s algorithm as a single one-qubit operation. For \\(f=(0,0)\\), \\(O_f=I\\); for \\((1,1)\\), \\(-I\\); for \\((0,1)\\), \\(Z\\); for \\((1,0)\\), \\(-Z\\). Conjugating by \\(H\\): \\(HIH=I\\), \\(H(-I)H=-I\\), \\(HZH=X\\), and \\(H(-Z)H=-X\\). So on the input qubit, Deutsch’s algorithm applies \\(\\pm I\\) for a constant function and \\(\\pm X\\) for a balanced one, to the input \\(\\ket0\\). The result is \\(\\pm\\ket0\\) or \\(\\pm\\ket1\\), and the measurement reads the class with certainty. The signs are global phases, which is why the algorithm cannot reveal which constant or which balanced function was used."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-6", "text": "Let \\(f(x_1,x_2)=x_1\\land x_2\\) be computed by a Toffoli gate into an answer bit starting at \\(0\\). Apply compute, phase, uncompute: Toffoli, then \\(Z\\) on the answer bit, then Toffoli again. Show that the net operation on \\(\\ket{x_1x_2}\\ket0\\) is the controlled-\\(Z\\) gate on the two inputs, and that the answer bit returns to \\(0\\). On \\(\\ket{x_1x_2}\\ket0\\), the first Toffoli gate gives \\(\\ket{x_1x_2}\\ket{x_1x_2}\\). Then \\(Z\\) multiplies by \\((-1)^{x_1x_2}\\). The second Toffoli gate adds \\(x_1x_2\\) to the answer bit again, returning it to \\(0\\). The result is \\((-1)^{x_1x_2}\\ket{x_1x_2}\\ket0\\): a sign \\(-1\\) only on \\(\\ket{11}\\), which is controlled-\\(Z\\), with the answer bit cleanly factored out. By linearity this holds for every superposition of inputs. The phase oracle of AND costs two Toffoli gates and one \\(Z\\), with one temporary bit, and the clean workspace is what allows the inputs’ signs to interfere later."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-7", "text": "A circuit for \\(f(x_1,x_2)=x_1\\oplus x_2\\) first copies \\(x_1\\) into a workspace bit \\(w\\), then forms the answer \\(w\\oplus x_2\\). A programmer applies \\(Z\\) to the answer and uncomputes the answer step only, leaving \\(w=x_1\\). Starting from the uniform input superposition, apply \\(H\\) to both inputs and find the outcome distribution. Compare with the clean version. Group by \\(w\\). For \\(w=0\\) the input part is \\(\\frac12(\\ket{00}-\\ket{01})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0\\ket-\\); for \\(w=1\\) it is \\(\\frac12(-\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket1\\ket-\\). After \\(H\\otimes H\\) these become \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket+\\ket1\\ket0\\) and \\(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket-\\ket1\\ket1\\). The second input reads \\(1\\) with certainty, but the first input is \\(\\ket+\\) on one record and \\(\\ket-\\) on the other, so it reads \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\). The outcomes are \\(01\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(\\frac12\\). The clean version gives \\(11\\) with certainty. The garbage records \\(x_1\\), so it destroys exactly the interference that would reveal the \\(x_1\\) part of the answer, while the \\(x_2\\) part survives."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student claims: “Deutsch’s algorithm evaluates \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) in one query, so with a better readout it could report both values.” Write the full two-qubit output state in terms of \\(f(0)\\) and \\(d=f(0)\\oplus f(1)\\), and explain why no measurement of it can reveal \\(f(0)\\). After the final Hadamard, the input is \\(\\frac{s_0+s_1}{2}\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}{2}\\ket1\\) with \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\). Factoring out \\(s_0\\), this is \\((-1)^{f(0)}\\ket d\\), so the output is \\((-1)^{f(0)}\\ket d\\ket-\\). The value \\(f(0)\\) appears only in a global phase, which no measurement detects, and the target \\(\\ket-\\) is the same for every function. So the output carries exactly one bit, \\(d\\). The query did act on both inputs, but the interference that made \\(d\\) certain also discarded every other distinction. One query of this kind reveals at most one bit about \\(f\\), not both values."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-9", "text": "Show that Deutsch’s algorithm gives the same outcome for \\(f\\) and for its complement \\(1-f\\), and that the outcome depends only on \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)\\). For \\(1-f\\), each sign \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\) is multiplied by \\(-1\\), a global phase that changes no probability. The outcome is \\(1\\) exactly when \\(s_0\\neq s_1\\), that is, when \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)=1\\). The algorithm extracts this one bit and nothing more about \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-10", "text": "A two-bit function is promised to have the form \\(f(x)=s_1x_1\\oplus s_2x_2\\) for an unknown string \\(s=s_1s_2\\). Run the two-qubit version of the algorithm (Hadamards on both inputs, target \\(\\ket-\\), one query, Hadamards on both inputs) for \\(s=10\\) and \\(s=11\\), and show that the measurement returns \\(s\\). How many classical queries are needed to learn \\(s\\)? After the query the input state is \\(\\frac12\\sum_x(-1)^{s_1x_1}(-1)^{s_2x_2}\\ket{x_1x_2}=\\frac{\\ket0+(-1)^{s_1}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\frac{\\ket0+(-1)^{s_2}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\). Each factor is \\(\\ket+\\) if \\(s_i=0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) if \\(s_i=1\\), and \\(H\\) maps them to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). So the output is \\(\\ket{s_1s_2}\\) with certainty: \\(10\\) gives \\(\\ket{10}\\) and \\(11\\) gives \\(\\ket{11}\\). Classically each query returns one bit, \\(s\\cdot x\\), and there are four possible strings, so two queries are necessary (and \\(x=10,01\\) suffice). With \\(n\\) bits the gap is one query against \\(n\\). This is the Bernstein–Vazirani problem, developed in Quantum Algorithms."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § circuit · wire A circuit draws one wire for each qubit and a box for each operation, with time running left to right. A wire identifies a subsystem; it need not carry a definite zero or one. In an entangled computation, follow the joint state of all wires. If \\(U\\) acts first and \\(V\\) second, the state becomes \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\). The drawing’s time order and the matrix product’s right-to-left action describe the same sequence. Gates on disjoint wires commute: \\((U\\otimes I)(I\\otimes V)=U\\otimes V\\). Gates sharing a wire generally do not. This lets us recognize possible parallel work in the diagram, while leaving physical scheduling to the hardware model. The two wires identify subsystems. U followed by V on the first wire gives VU; W on the separate wire may run independently. Problems A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then CNOT with qubit 1 as control and qubit 2 as target, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Write the state after each step. Apply, to \\(\\ket{00}\\), first \\(H\\) on qubit 1 and then CNOT, and in the other order. Do the two operations commute? Prove that \\((U\\otimes I)(I\\otimes V)=(I\\otimes V)(U\\otimes I)=U\\otimes V\\): gates on different wires commute."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § In Deutsch’s circuit, the first pair of \\(H\\) gates prepares \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), the oracle puts the function into signs, and the last \\(H\\) exposes their relation. A box labelled \\(U_f\\) specifies the oracle’s action, but does not show its elementary gates or their cost. Search will use another repeated pattern: a phase oracle marks candidates, then a reflection about the prepared state combines their amplitudes. We will construct those operations here and study their repeated action in the search algorithm . A box in either circuit can stand for many elementary gates, so the diagram alone does not establish implementation cost. Deutsch’s algorithm uses one oracle gate between its preparation and readout gates. The Uf box specifies a joint operation, not its internal gate count. Problems In the four-item search circuit, how many oracle uses and how many Hadamard gates does one Grover iteration contain, and how many Hadamard gates prepare the start? List the gates of Deutsch’s algorithm in time order, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), with the qubits each acts on. An oracle box \\(O_f\\) on \\(n\\) qubits marks one fixed string. It is built from \\(X\\) gates and a phase flip controlled on all \\(n\\) qubits, which in turn uses Toffoli gates and \\(n-2\\) workspace qubits. Count the gates in such a construction."}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § classically controlled · coherent controlled gate A measurement symbol produces a classical outcome. The qubit itself may remain available in its conditional post-measurement state; the symbol does not mean that the physical system ceases to exist. In these drawings, solid single lines carry quantum systems; double lines after measurement and dashed control connections carry classical records. A classically controlled correction chooses a gate using a recorded bit. A coherent controlled gate instead acts on an unmeasured control and can preserve a superposition of branches. Teleportation uses both kinds of control. Alice’s CNOT is coherent. Bob’s correction is selected after receiving her classical outcomes. Replacing the first by a measurement-controlled flip would change the protocol; replacing the second requires retaining and handling the outcomes coherently, rather than simply deleting the message. Alice’s measurements produce classical records that select Bob’s corrections. The quantum input is transferred after the message arrives; no second copy remains with Alice. Problems Bob receives a bit \\(b\\) and applies \\(X\\) only if \\(b=1\\). Write his operation for each value of \\(b\\). For the input \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\), compare (a) CNOT followed by measuring the control with (b) measuring the control first and then applying \\(X\\) to the target if the result is \\(1\\). Show that the outcomes and final states agree. Now the control is not measured at the end. Compare CNOT with “measure the control, then apply \\(X\\) if \\(1\\)” on the input \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), by applying \\(H\\) to the control afterward and measuring it."}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § Pauli matrices · rotation gate · phase gates Which one-qubit gates do we need? We have met three: \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] A fourth, \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), completes the Pauli matrices \\(I\\), \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\). Fixed gates turn a qubit by fixed amounts. A general operation, such as the half of a NOT met earlier, needs turns of any size. For a Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define the rotation gate \\[R_P(\\theta)=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I-i\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P.\\] Is it unitary ? Since \\(P^\\dagger=P\\) and \\(P^2=I\\), we get \\(R_P(\\theta)^\\dagger R_P(\\theta)=\\cos^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I+\\sin^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P^2=I\\). It is. Two fixed rotations will matter when gates must come from a finite set: the phase gates \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\). Here \\(S\\) names the conventional phase gate; the square root of NOT from Chapter 3 is \\(\\sqrt X\\). These gates change the relative phase of \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(\\pi/2\\) and by \\(\\pi/4\\). Predict first. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), compare applying \\(H\\) twice with applying \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Predict both final measurement distributions before trying the gates. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to \\(\\ket0\\) State: \\(1\\ket0+0\\ket1\\) Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(H\\) followed by \\(H\\) returns \\(\\ket0\\), so 0 is certain. \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) produces \\(\\ket1\\), so 1 is certain. \\(Z\\) changes a relative sign that the final \\(H\\) converts into a different outcome. These are separate single-qubit circuits. Rotation parameters are continuous; S and T are fixed phase operations. Problems Compute \\(R_X(\\pi)\\) and \\(R_Z(\\pi)\\), and compare them with \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\). Verify by multiplying matrices that \\(HZH=X\\), \\(HXH=Z\\), \\(S^2=Z\\), and \\(T^2=S\\). Show that \\(R_Z(\\theta)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\\theta/2},e^{i\\theta/2})\\), and that up to a global phase it is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\). Which values of \\(\\theta\\) give \\(S\\) and \\(T\\)?"}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § controlled-\\(Z\\) How do we make a gate act on a target only when a control qubit is \\(1\\)? Use the projectors onto the two values of the control: \\[\\text{controlled-}U=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U.\\] It keeps the control and applies \\(U\\) to the target in the branch where the control is \\(1\\). CNOT is controlled-\\(X\\). Controlled-\\(Z\\) multiplies only \\(\\ket{11}\\) by \\(-1\\). Can one be made from the other? Since \\(HXH=Z\\), applying \\(H\\) to the target before and after a CNOT gives controlled-\\(Z\\). Do controlled gates always entangle? Not on every input: CNOT leaves \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) a product state . On \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), however, it produces a Bell state . Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z. Problems Apply controlled-\\(Z\\) to the four basis states. Show that exchanging the roles of control and target gives the same operation. Verify that \\((I\\otimes H)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes H)\\) acts as controlled-\\(Z\\) on \\(\\ket{10}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). Let \\(\\ket v\\) be an eigenvector of \\(U\\) with \\(U\\ket v=e^{i\\phi}\\ket v\\). Show that controlled-\\(U\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\ket v\\) to \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)\\otimes\\ket v\\). Is the result entangled?"}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § How is a phase oracle built? To mark one bit string, flip with \\(X\\) each of its positions that is \\(0\\), apply a phase flip controlled on all qubits being \\(1\\), and undo the flips. For a predicate computed by a circuit, use compute, phase, uncompute. For a uniform state \\(\\ket s=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}\\ket x\\), with \\(N=2^n\\), the reflection is \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\): it preserves the component along \\(\\ket s\\) and negates the orthogonal component. Since \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) maps \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) to \\(\\ket s\\), we can reflect about \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) between two layers of Hadamards: \\[D=H^{\\otimes n}\\bigl(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I\\bigr)H^{\\otimes n}.\\] The two-qubit drawing implements the reflection about \\(\\ket{00}\\) with \\(X\\) gates and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and so produces \\(-D\\). The sign is a global phase when the circuit is applied on its own; it must be retained if the circuit is used inside a controlled operation. These oracle and reflection circuits will be used in search. A general predicate needs its own evaluation circuit. Problems Which gates mark the string \\(01\\) on two qubits, that is, multiply \\(\\ket{01}\\) by \\(-1\\) and leave the other basis states alone? For two qubits, verify that \\((H\\otimes H)(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)(H\\otimes H)\\) and \\(2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) agree on \\(\\ket{00}\\). Show that \\((X\\otimes X)\\,CZ\\,(X\\otimes X)=-(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)\\). Why does this sign not matter in an ordinary Grover iteration, and when would it matter?"}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § Can one-qubit gates alone produce every operation? A circuit of one-qubit gates acts as \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product state it gives another product state: \\[(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n)(\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\ket{\\psi_n})=U_1\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\ket{\\psi_n}.\\] It can never prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), however many gates it uses. The argument settles the question without counting anything: a universal set needs a gate that lets qubits affect one another. No wire connection appears in this circuit. Each output depends only on the input on its own wire, so local gates cannot create entanglement from a product input. Problems Apply \\(H\\otimes H\\) to \\(\\ket{00}\\), and check that the result is a product state. A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then \\(H\\) to qubit 1 again. Find the result on \\(\\ket{00}\\), and explain why it had to be a product state. Show that one-qubit operations cannot turn an entangled two-qubit state into a product state."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § universal CNOT together with arbitrary one-qubit unitaries can construct every finite-register unitary. This is a universality theorem about the whole set ; possessing one entangling gate with no adequate local controls is not enough. One proof first decomposes a unitary into transformations acting on pairs of basis states, then builds those transformations from controlled operations and one-qubit gates. We state the theorem here; its full synthesis proof is beyond the algorithms we need. Universality settles possibility, not cost. A generic \\(n\\)-qubit unitary has exponentially many parameters and typically needs an exponentially long circuit. Useful algorithms work because their operations have additional structure. Our oracle and reflection constructions are examples of that structure made explicit. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation . A representative circuit uses arbitrary single-qubit gates and CNOT. This finite example shows the gate repertoire; it does not claim that two CNOTs synthesize every two-qubit unitary. Problems Which of these gate sets can prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\): \\(\\{H,X,Z\\}\\); \\(\\{H,\\text{CNOT}\\}\\); \\(\\{\\text{CNOT},X\\}\\)? Show that circuits of CNOT and \\(X\\) gates only permute computational basis states. A reversible circuit on \\(m\\) wires uses \\(g\\) gates, each an \\(X\\), CNOT, or Toffoli on chosen wires. Show that there are at most \\((3m^3)^g\\) such circuits, and deduce that most Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits need at least about \\(2^n/\\log_2(3m^3)\\) gates."}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § Clifford+T · operator-norm error A finite gate alphabet produces only countably many finite circuits, while unitaries vary continuously. Exact synthesis of every unitary is therefore impossible. Approximation is the useful goal: a suitable set, such as Clifford+T, approximates arbitrary quantum operations up to a global phase. Choose consistent phase representatives and ask for \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\). This operator-norm bound guarantees that the two output vectors differ by at most \\(\\epsilon\\) on every unit input. If the operation will itself be controlled, its relative phase must be specified; it cannot simply be discarded as a global phase of the larger circuit. A circuit with \\(L\\) approximated gates has total operator-norm error at most \\(\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\). To meet a budget \\(\\epsilon\\), one simple allocation gives each gate tolerance \\(\\epsilon/L\\). The triangle-inequality proof appears in the problems. An approximation claim therefore has two parts: can we reach the requested tolerance, and how many gates does doing so require? Universality answers only the first. Each Vj approximates the corresponding Uj with a specified operator-norm error. The diagram compares two circuits; it does not assert an exact finite-alphabet synthesis. Problems Each of \\(1000\\) gates is approximated within \\(10^{-6}\\). What error bound holds for the whole circuit? A circuit has \\(10^6\\) gates and its total error must be at most \\(10^{-2}\\). What accuracy must each gate have if the error is split equally? Prove that \\(\\|U_2U_1-V_2V_1\\|\\leq\\|U_2-V_2\\|+\\|U_1-V_1\\|\\) for unitaries \\(U_1,U_2,V_1,V_2\\)."}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § gate count · depth · width · ancilla · uniform What does a circuit cost? Three measures matter: the gate count , the total number of elementary gates; the depth , the number of layers of gates that must run one after another; and the width , the number of qubits, including extra workspace qubits called ancillas . These trade off. More workspace can reduce depth, while recomputing an intermediate value instead of storing it can save qubits. Ancillas must start in a known state and are usually returned to it by uncomputation , so that they can be reused. There is a subtler cost. An algorithm needs a circuit for every input length \\(n\\). A circuit family is uniform if a classical procedure produces the circuit for each \\(n\\), and the family counts as efficient only if that procedure is efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit could hide hard work in its design. Three initial Hadamards run in parallel, followed by two successive CNOTs. This example has five gates, depth three, and width three when these gates are available directly. Problems A circuit on three qubits applies \\(H\\) to each qubit, then CNOT from qubit 1 to qubit 2, then CNOT from qubit 2 to qubit 3. Give its gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of \\(n=8\\) bits with a chain of Toffoli gates: \\(w_1=x_1x_2\\), \\(w_2=w_1x_3\\), and so on, then the output, then uncompute the workspace. Give the gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of the same \\(8\\) bits with a balanced tree instead: combine bits in pairs, then pairs of results, and so on. Compare gate count, depth, and workspace with the chain."}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § connectivity · SWAP gate Can any two qubits interact? In a circuit diagram, yes; in a device, often only neighbouring qubits can. Gates between distant qubits then need SWAP gates to bring them together, which adds to the count and the depth. Is loading data free? Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes can take work proportional to the length of the list, even though the register holding it is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate can cost far more than the number of oracle calls suggests. A resource estimate must therefore state the gate set, the connectivity, how inputs are prepared, and how outputs are read, before an asymptotic advantage can become a claim about real machines. For a linear three-qubit layout, SWAP brings the third logical qubit next to the first. CNOT acts there, and the second SWAP restores the original ordering. Each SWAP itself requires implementation. Problems Three qubits sit on a line, \\(1\\)–\\(2\\)–\\(3\\), and only neighbours can interact. How many SWAP gates are needed to apply a CNOT between qubits 1 and 3 and restore the original placement? A SWAP gate costs three CNOT gates. Count the CNOT gates in the construction above, and for a CNOT between the two ends of a line of \\(n\\) qubits. A data set of \\(N=2^n\\) numbers is loaded into amplitudes using one gate per number, and then searched with about \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries. When does the quadratic query speedup survive in the total cost?"}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § quantum error correction · syndrome A gate can be synthesized accurately in mathematics and still be executed imperfectly. Approximation error concerns the chosen ideal circuit; physical noise concerns how the device actually realizes it. Both matter, but their models and remedies differ. Quantum error correction encodes logical information across several physical qubits. Syndrome measurements reveal information about errors without revealing the encoded logical amplitudes. A syndrome need not identify a unique microscopic error; it must support a correction that preserves the logical state for the errors the code handles. This does not clone the unknown input. The encoding creates a joint state, not independent copies available on separate wires. The no-cloning theorem remains intact. We stop short of constructing a fault-tolerant computer. The foundation for that subject is now visible: joint states, subspaces, measurements, classical corrections, and a resource budget. Proving an ideal algorithm correct is necessary, but reliability is a further requirement. Two CNOTs encode one unknown qubit into a joint three-qubit state. This is the repetition-code encoding used for bit-flip protection; it is not a complete error-correction or fault-tolerance circuit. Problems Each gate fails independently with probability \\(10^{-3}\\). What is the probability that a circuit of \\(1000\\) gates runs without any failure? How small must the failure probability per gate be for a circuit of \\(10^9\\) gates to run without failure with probability at least \\(0.9\\)? A repetition code stores a classical bit as three identical bits and decodes by majority. With independent flip probability \\(0<q<1\\), derive the failure probability and find when encoding helps. Now consider \\(\\alpha\\ket{000}+\\beta\\ket{111}\\): what does a phase flip \\(Z\\) on its first qubit do, and why does majority voting miss it?"}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-1", "text": "Do \\(H\\) followed by \\(Z\\), and \\(Z\\) followed by \\(H\\), have the same action on \\(\\ket0\\)? No. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\); \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket+\\). The order of the matrices must follow the order in time."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Compute \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)=\\cos\\frac\\pi4I-i\\sin\\frac\\pi4Y\\) as a matrix. Compare \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)\\ket0\\) with \\(H\\ket0\\), and \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)\\ket1\\) with \\(H\\ket1\\). Are the two gates the same? Express \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)\\) using \\(H\\) and one Pauli gate. \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). On \\(\\ket0\\) both gates give \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). On \\(\\ket1\\), \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)\\) gives \\((-\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2=-\\ket-\\), while \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\). The sign is relative to the \\(\\ket0\\) column, so the gates differ: on \\(\\ket+\\), for example, \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\) and \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket0\\). In fact \\(R_Y(\\pi/2)=HZ\\): apply \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Agreement on one input does not identify a gate; a unitary is fixed by its action on every basis state, including phases."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-3", "text": "A gate \\(V\\) equals \\(U\\) up to the global phase \\(i\\): \\(V=iU\\). Apply controlled-\\(U\\) and controlled-\\(V\\) to \\(\\ket+\\ket\\psi\\) and compare the results. What one-qubit gate on the control accounts for the difference? Controlled-\\(U\\) gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0\\ket\\psi+\\ket1U\\ket\\psi)\\). Controlled-\\(V\\) gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0\\ket\\psi+i\\ket1U\\ket\\psi)\\). The factor \\(i\\) appears only on the control-\\(1\\) branch, so it is now a relative phase between branches: controlled-\\(V\\) equals controlled-\\(U\\) followed by \\(S\\) on the control. A phase that is invisible when \\(U\\) is applied alone becomes observable once \\(U\\) is controlled, which is why approximations used inside controlled operations must fix the phase, as Section 8.3.3 warns."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-4", "text": "Build the complete two-qubit Grover circuit for the marked item \\(10\\): the oracle from an \\(X\\) gate and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and the reflection from \\(H\\), \\(X\\), and controlled-\\(Z\\) gates. Verify that one iteration from \\(\\ket s\\) gives \\(10\\). Oracle: \\(X\\) on qubit 2, controlled-\\(Z\\), \\(X\\) on qubit 2. It sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to \\(\\ket{11}\\), applies the sign, and restores \\(\\ket{10}\\). Starting from \\(\\ket s=\\tfrac12(1,1,1,1)\\), the oracle gives \\(\\tfrac12(1,1,-1,1)\\). The reflection circuit implements \\(-D\\), and \\(D\\) maps this state to \\(\\ket{10}\\), so the result is \\(-\\ket{10}\\): the outcome is \\(10\\) with certainty."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-5", "text": "Show that the reflection \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) about the uniform state acts on amplitudes as “inversion about the mean”: if \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_xa_x\\ket x\\) and \\(\\bar a\\) is the average of the \\(a_x\\), then \\(D\\ket\\psi=\\sum_x(2\\bar a-a_x)\\ket x\\). Use it to redo one Grover iteration on two qubits with marked item \\(10\\). Since \\(\\braket s\\psi=\\sqrt N\\,\\bar a\\), we have \\(2\\ket s\\braket s\\psi=2\\sqrt N\\,\\bar a\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}\\sum_x\\ket x=\\sum_x2\\bar a\\ket x\\). Subtracting \\(\\ket\\psi\\) gives amplitudes \\(2\\bar a-a_x\\): each amplitude is reflected about the mean. For two qubits, \\(\\ket s\\) has all amplitudes \\(\\frac12\\). The oracle for \\(10\\) makes them \\(\\frac12,\\frac12,-\\frac12,\\frac12\\), with mean \\(\\frac14\\). Reflecting gives \\(2\\cdot\\frac14-\\frac12=0\\) for the three unmarked items and \\(2\\cdot\\frac14+\\frac12=1\\) for \\(10\\). One iteration finds the marked item with certainty. The negative amplitude lies far below the mean, so the reflection throws it far above."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-6", "text": "On two qubits, build the phase oracle \\((-1)^{x_1\\lor x_2}\\) for OR. (a) Show that \\(X\\otimes X\\), then controlled-\\(Z\\), then \\(X\\otimes X\\), multiplies only \\(\\ket{00}\\) by \\(-1\\). (b) Explain why this circuit implements the OR phase oracle up to a global phase. (c) When would that global phase matter? (a) \\(X\\otimes X\\) sends \\(\\ket{00}\\) to \\(\\ket{11}\\), controlled-\\(Z\\) multiplies it by \\(-1\\), and \\(X\\otimes X\\) returns it to \\(\\ket{00}\\). Every other basis state is mapped away from \\(\\ket{11}\\) and back without a sign. So the circuit has signs \\((-,+,+,+)\\) on \\(00,01,10,11\\). (b) OR is \\(0\\) only on \\(00\\), so its phase oracle has signs \\((+,-,-,-)\\), exactly \\(-1\\) times the circuit. Used by itself, as in a search iteration, the factor \\(-1\\) multiplies the whole state and changes no probability. (c) If the oracle is controlled by another qubit, the \\(-1\\) acts only on the control-\\(1\\) branch and becomes a \\(Z\\) on the control, so the correct sign must be restored, for example by a \\(Z\\) on that control."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-7", "text": "Grover search over \\(N=2^{20}\\) items uses about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) iterations, each compiled into \\(300\\) elementary gates. (a) Estimate the total gate count. (b) If each gate fails independently with probability \\(10^{-4}\\), estimate the probability that the whole circuit runs without a failure. (c) What failure probability per gate would give at least \\(0.9\\)? (a) About \\(\\frac\\pi4\\times1024\\approx804\\) iterations, so roughly \\(804\\times300\\approx2.4\\times10^5\\) gates. (b) \\(e^{-10^{-4}\\times2.4\\times10^5}=e^{-24}\\approx3\\times10^{-11}\\): essentially never. (c) We need \\(e^{-pG}\\ge0.9\\), so \\(p\\le0.105/(2.4\\times10^5)\\approx4\\times10^{-7}\\). An ideal algorithm whose circuit grows like \\(\\sqrt N\\) requires error rates that shrink like \\(1/\\sqrt N\\). Without error correction, a quadratic query advantage can be erased by noise long before \\(N\\) is large. This is the reliability requirement of Section 8.4.3 in numbers."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “CNOT and one-qubit gates are universal, so any unitary on \\(50\\) qubits can be compiled into a manageable circuit.” Use a parameter count to estimate how many gates a generic \\(50\\)-qubit unitary needs, and say what universality does and does not promise. A unitary on \\(n\\) qubits has \\(4^n\\) real parameters (up to a constant), and each one- or two-qubit gate contributes at most \\(16\\). A circuit for a generic unitary therefore needs on the order of \\(4^n/16\\) gates. For \\(n=50\\), \\(4^{50}\\approx1.3\\times10^{30}\\), so roughly \\(10^{29}\\) gates, far beyond any device. Universality guarantees that every unitary can be built, or approximated, from the gate set. It does not promise that the circuit is short. Useful algorithms use unitaries with structure, such as oracles, reflections, and Fourier transforms, that admit polynomial-size circuits."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-9", "text": "A search over \\(N=2^n\\) items uses \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) iterations. The oracle is compiled into \\(20n\\) gates and the reflection into \\(10n\\) gates. Give the total gate count as a function of \\(n\\), and evaluate it for \\(n=30\\). The total is about \\((\\pi/4)\\,2^{n/2}\\cdot30n\\). For \\(n=30\\): \\((\\pi/4)\\times32{,}768\\times900\\approx2.3\\times10^7\\) gates. The query count, about \\(2.6\\times10^4\\), hides a factor of \\(30n=900\\) gates per query."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-10", "text": "Use counting to bound the cost of approximation with a finite gate set. Single-qubit unitaries up to global phase form a three-parameter family, so at least about \\((1/\\epsilon)^3\\) circuits are needed to approximate every one within \\(\\epsilon\\). A gate set has \\(k\\) single-qubit gates. (a) Show that circuits of length at most \\(g\\) can meet this target only if \\(g\\gtrsim3\\log(1/\\epsilon)/\\log k\\). (b) Evaluate for \\(k=3\\) (\\(H,S,T\\)) and \\(\\epsilon=10^{-10}\\). (c) What does the bound say about how gate count grows as \\(\\epsilon\\) shrinks? (a) With \\(k\\) gates there are at most about \\(k^g\\) circuits of length \\(g\\) (a little more counting shorter ones, which does not change the estimate). Covering all unitaries within \\(\\epsilon\\) needs \\(k^g\\gtrsim(1/\\epsilon)^3\\), so \\(g\\gtrsim3\\ln(1/\\epsilon)/\\ln k\\). (b) \\(3\\ln10^{10}/\\ln3\\approx3\\times23.0/1.10\\approx63\\) gates. (c) The required length grows only logarithmically in \\(1/\\epsilon\\): each additional decimal digit of accuracy costs a few more gates. Good synthesis methods for Clifford+\\(T\\) achieve length proportional to \\(\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\), so this counting bound is attainable up to a constant factor, which is why rotation synthesis is cheap per gate but adds up over a long circuit."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § marked · predicate A predicate \\(f\\) checks a candidate: \\(f(x)=1\\) means marked, and zero means unmarked. We want to find a marked label among \\(N\\) possibilities. With exactly one mark promised, testing \\(N-1\\) distinct labels classically suffices in the worst case: if every test fails, infer the remaining label. Even allowing a fixed nonzero error probability, an unstructured classical method still needs order \\(N\\) queries. The promise explains the exact count; the scaling is what we will compare. Begin with four labels and mark \\(11\\). We will first calculate every amplitude , then find the pattern that survives when four becomes \\(N\\). The predicate is given through an oracle , so marking 11 in the worked example is not advance knowledge supplied to the search algorithm . A computational-basis input and a blank target implement one predicate test. M reads whether x is marked; this is classical value-query behaviour represented with the reversible oracle. Problems A predicate on three-bit labels marks the labels whose bits add to \\(2\\). How many labels are marked, and what is the probability that a uniformly random label is marked? One label among \\(N=8\\) is marked. A classical search queries labels in a uniformly random order, without repetition, until it finds the marked one. What is the expected number of queries? One label among \\(N\\) is marked, in a uniformly random position. Show that any classical algorithm making \\(k\\) queries finds it with probability at most \\(k/N\\)."}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § uniform superposition Can a quantum search do better than testing candidates one at a time? It can begin with all candidates at once. Two Hadamards prepare the uniform superposition \\[\\ket s=\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2.\\] How should a query act? Phase kickback turns a query into a sign: \\[O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x.\\] With \\(11\\) marked, the amplitudes become \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Has this helped? Not yet. Every probability is still \\(1/4\\), and measuring now finds \\(11\\) with probability \\(1/4\\), as a guess would. The query has created a relative sign, and a further operation must turn that sign into probability. The phase oracle \\(O_f\\) may be supplied directly, or built by phase kickback or by compute, phase, uncompute. Its cost in queries must be stated accordingly. Two H gates prepare the uniform state. Controlled-Z marks 11 by changing only its sign; it does not change immediate measurement probabilities. Problems For \\(N=4\\) with the label \\(01\\) marked, write the amplitudes after preparing the uniform superposition and applying the phase oracle. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, what are the amplitudes after preparation and marking, and what is the probability of measuring the marked label? Show that applying the phase oracle any number of times, with nothing in between, never changes any measurement probability."}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § reflection about the mean Which operation turns a negative sign into a larger amplitude? Look at the amplitudes \\((1/2,1/2,1/2,-1/2)\\). Their mean is \\(\\mu=1/4\\). The marked amplitude lies far below the mean, and the others slightly above it. Reflect every amplitude about the mean, \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\): \\[\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14-\\tfrac12=0,\\qquad-\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12=1.\\] One marking and one reflection find \\(11\\) with certainty. Is reflection about the mean a valid quantum operation? It is the matrix \\[D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I.\\] Indeed, \\(\\braket s a=\\sum_xa_x/\\sqrt N\\), and every entry of \\(\\ket s\\) is \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), so the entries of \\(2\\ket s\\braket sa\\) are \\(2\\mu\\), and \\(D\\ket a\\) has entries \\(2\\mu-a_x\\). Since \\(D^2=4\\ket s\\bra s-4\\ket s\\bra s+I=I\\) and \\(D^\\dagger=D\\), it is unitary . It does not estimate the mean from samples; it acts on the amplitudes directly. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one. Problems Reflect the amplitudes \\(\\tfrac12(1,-1,1,1)\\) about their mean. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, compute the amplitudes after one marking and one reflection about the mean, and the success probability. Show directly from \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\) that reflection about the mean preserves the sum of squared amplitudes, for real amplitudes."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § good and bad subspaces The four-item calculation looks expensive to repeat for millions of labels. Its symmetry saves us. Starting uniformly, all marked labels always share one amplitude and all unmarked labels share another. Group the \\(M\\) marked labels into the normalized vector \\(\\ket G\\), and the \\(N-M\\) unmarked labels into \\(\\ket B\\). Their supports are disjoint, so they are orthogonal. For \\(0 \\[\\ket s=\\sqrt{M/N}\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-M/N}\\ket B.\\] Why the square roots? The good group contains \\(M\\) terms of squared magnitude \\(1/N\\), so its total weight is \\(M/N\\). Its coefficient on a normalized good vector must have that squared magnitude. These vectors are tools for analyzing the algorithm. Constructing \\(\\ket G\\) explicitly would require knowing the marked set, which is precisely what we do not know. The available oracle and uniform-state reflection preserve their plane without requiring that knowledge. The bundled wire carries the n-qubit search register. Good and bad components group its basis labels; they are not separate physical qubits. Problems For \\(N=8\\) with the marked labels \\(011\\) and \\(101\\), write \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), write \\(\\ket s\\) as a combination of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). Show that the oracle \\(O_f\\) and the reflection \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) both map the plane of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) into itself, by computing their action on \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\)."}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 Initial success amplitude § success probability Where does \\(\\ket s\\) lie in this plane? Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0\\lt\\theta\\lt\\pi/2\\). Then \\[\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket B:\\] the state makes the angle \\(\\theta\\) with the bad direction, and its success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), or \\(30^\\circ\\). Why is one angle enough, however many labels the register has? Both the oracle and the reflection map the plane to itself, so the state never leaves it. Measuring the uniform preparation succeeds with probability M/N. The angle in the text describes this circuit’s initial amplitudes, not a second register. Problems Find the angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(N=16\\) with \\(M=1\\), and for \\(N=8\\) with \\(M=2\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), how many iterations give success with certainty? Show that, for \\(0\\lt M\\lt N\\), exactly one Grover iteration gives certain success precisely when \\(M/N=1/4\\)."}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Reflections and rotations § Grover iteration What does the oracle do in this plane? It negates \\(\\ket G\\) and keeps \\(\\ket B\\): a reflection across the bad axis. The operation \\(D\\) is a reflection across \\(\\ket s\\). Two reflections in a plane make a rotation, by twice the angle between their axes. Here the axes are the bad direction and \\(\\ket s\\), at the angle \\(\\theta\\), so each Grover iteration \\(DO_f\\) rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\) toward the good direction. For \\(N=4\\), the state starts at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\), and the reflection across \\(\\ket s\\) sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\), the good direction: the certain success found above. This calculation also predicts what happens if we continue. The rotation does not stop at the good direction; it carries the state past it. Predict first. For \\(N=4\\) the state starts at \\(30^\\circ\\). Predict its angle after \\(O_f\\), then after \\(D\\). Where is it after a second full iteration? Two reflections make a rotation Search space 4 16 64 Apply \\(O_f\\) Apply \\(D\\) One iteration \\(DO_f\\) Reset The plane of \\(\\ket B\\) and \\(\\ket G\\) for one marked item, with \\(\\ket s\\) at angle \\(\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). \\(O_f\\) reflects across \\(\\ket B\\) and \\(D\\) reflects across \\(\\ket s\\). The highlighted line is the mirror of the last reflection, and faint arrows show earlier states. Each pair turns the state by \\(2\\theta\\). After trying it: compare your explanation \\(O_f\\) sends \\(30^\\circ\\) to \\(-30^\\circ\\), and \\(D\\) sends \\(-30^\\circ\\) to \\(90^\\circ\\), the good direction, so success is certain. A second iteration turns it another \\(60^\\circ\\), to \\(150^\\circ\\), where the success probability is \\(\\sin^2 150^\\circ=1/4\\). Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), by how many degrees does each Grover iteration rotate the state? For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), give the angle of the state and the success probability after \\(1\\), \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(4\\) iterations. In the plane with the bad direction as the first axis and the good direction as the second, reflection across the line at angle \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\alpha&\\sin2\\alpha\\\\\\sin2\\alpha&-\\cos2\\alpha\\end{pmatrix}\\). Verify that reflecting across the bad axis and then across \\(\\ket s\\) is a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\)."}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Amplitude evolution § Where is the state after \\(k\\) iterations, and how likely is success? It starts at the angle \\(\\theta\\) and turns by \\(2\\theta\\) each time, so it reaches \\((2k+1)\\theta\\): \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket G+\\cos\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr).\\] Which marked label do we find? All marked labels share the amplitude of \\(\\ket G\\), so given success, the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction \\(M/N\\), not on which labels are marked. The repeated gate denotes k uses of the oracle-and-reflection pair, not one elementary gate. Measure only after these coherent iterations. Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute \\(p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=3\\). For \\(N=64\\) and \\(M=1\\), which number of iterations maximizes the success probability, and what is that probability? Show that, given success, the measured label is uniformly distributed over the \\(M\\) marked labels, and that, given failure, it is uniform over the unmarked labels."}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § overshooting · stopping rule Stop near the first time the rotating state reaches the good axis: \\[(2k+1)\\theta\\approx\\pi/2,\\qquad k\\approx\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12.\\] Choose a nearby nonnegative integer. For a small marked fraction, \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), giving order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) iterations rather than the classical order \\(N/M\\) tests. The four-item case happens to land exactly on the axis after one iteration. Most cases do not. With \\(N=16,M=1\\), three iterations give success about 0.961. The point is a high success probability with fewer queries, not a universal promise of certainty. Continuing is not automatically helpful. For four items the probabilities at \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4,1,1/4\\). The same reversible rotation that increases the good component eventually decreases it. A stopping rule is therefore part of the algorithm’s proof, not an afterthought about implementation. Predict first. Set Search space to 4 and reset. There is one marked item; start from equal amplitudes. Predict the success probability after one iteration and after a second. Does another iteration always help? Experiment: choose the iteration count There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 Adjust the repeated Grover circuit |0…0⟩ H on each (D O_f) × 0 M x Each repetition applies O_f, then D; measure after all repetitions. Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items. After trying it: compare your explanation One iteration gives success probability 1. A second gives \\(1/4\\): continued rotation can move the state away from the marked direction. Compare this with the initial probability \\(1/4\\). Problems For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute the success probability after three iterations. A search has \\(N=16\\) items. You plan three Grover iterations assuming one marked item, but there are actually four. What success probability do three iterations give? How many iterations would work if you knew there were four? Show that the sequence of success probabilities \\(p_0,p_1,p_2,\\dots\\) is periodic when \\(\\theta\\) is a rational multiple of \\(\\pi\\), and find the period for \\(N=4\\), \\(M=1\\)."}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § verification What if we do not know \\(M\\), and so cannot compute the stopping point? A fixed guess may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random number of iterations from a range that grows gradually, measures, and checks the candidate with one query to \\(f\\). When \\(M\\gt 0\\), such schedules still need an expected number of queries of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\). What if nothing is marked? Then no check ever succeeds, and an unbounded search never ends; it cannot prove that nothing is marked. A bounded version stops after a fixed budget and reports failure, with a stated probability of being wrong when marked labels do exist. That is a statistical guarantee, not a proof of absence. If every label is marked, the very first measurement succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching . After a search run returns candidate x, a separate basis-input query checks it. A failed check calls for another run with the prescribed iteration schedule and budget. Problems An algorithm succeeds with probability \\(p\\) per attempt and checks its candidate with one query. How many attempts does it need on average to find a marked label? For \\(N=1024\\), the iteration count tuned for \\(M=1\\) is \\(k=25\\). What is the success probability if in fact \\(M=4\\)? Each verified attempt succeeds with probability at least \\(1/4\\), independently. Show that \\(t\\) attempts all fail with probability at most \\((3/4)^t\\), and find the smallest \\(t\\) for which this is below \\(10^{-6}\\)."}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Was the uniform start essential? Suppose a unitary \\(A\\) prepares \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and \\(P_G\\) projects onto the outcomes we count as successes, with success probability \\(p=\\|P_G\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\), where \\(0\\lt p\\lt 1\\). Normalizing the good and bad parts writes \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sqrt p\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-p}\\,\\ket B.\\] This is the same plane as before, with \\(\\sin^2\\theta=p\\). Where might \\(A\\) come from? A classical randomized procedure can sometimes be made coherent and serve as \\(A\\), but only through an explicit reversible implementation, including its random bits and its workspace. Access to a routine that produces samples is not enough. A is the full reversible preparation circuit. Its success probability is the total weight on good outputs; the bundled wire includes any required coherent workspace. Problems A preparation succeeds with probability \\(p=1/9\\). Find \\(\\theta\\) and the amplitudes of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(p=1/9\\), how many amplification iterations maximize success, and what is the success probability? A one-qubit preparation \\(A\\) gives \\(A\\ket0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\), and success means outcome \\(1\\). Show that one amplification iteration succeeds with certainty, even though this preparation is not uniform."}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § coherent access The search reflected about \\(\\ket s\\). How do we reflect about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) without knowing it? Reflection about \\(\\ket0\\), \\(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\), is easy: it negates every configuration except \\(0\\). Bring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) with \\(A^\\dagger\\), reflect there, and return with \\(A\\): \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A\\bigl(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\bigr)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to a state, the rightmost factor \\(A^\\dagger\\) acts first. Together with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates the successful part, \\(R_\\psi\\) rotates the state toward the good direction exactly as in search. What does this require? Coherent access to \\(A\\), to its inverse, and to the success test. Measuring whether a sample succeeded is no substitute: it destroys the superposition instead of reflecting it. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state. Problems For \\(A=H\\) on one qubit, write \\(R_\\psi=A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger\\) as a matrix. Show that \\(A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I\\) when \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and that it is unitary. Why can the reflection \\(R_\\psi\\) not be replaced by measuring whether the prepared state succeeded? Show that measuring after each oracle step makes the success probability stay at \\(p\\)."}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § amplitude amplification Independent trials with success probability \\(p\\) need order \\(1/p\\) repetitions for constant success. Coherent amplitude amplification needs order \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) rounds when \\(p\\) is small and the required reflections are available. Each round contains the preparation \\(A\\), its inverse, and the success reflection. If preparation costs \\(C_A\\) and the reflection costs \\(C_G\\), a useful estimate is \\[\\text{cost}\\sim\\frac{2C_A+C_G}{\\sqrt p},\\] up to constants, the initial preparation, and verification. Chapter 10 gives a precise notation for such scaling estimates. The denominator is the benefit; the numerator is the work that must be paid for each time. A more successful but much more expensive preparation need not win overall. The gate constructions give a way to count the work inside these operations, rather than treating each as a free box. One amplification round applies the success reflection, inverse preparation, zero-state reflection, and preparation. Repeat the round coherently before measuring; the zero-reflection cost must also be included when significant. Problems For \\(p=0.01\\), compare the number of independent trials needed for a reasonable chance of success with the number of amplification iterations. Preparing \\(A\\) costs \\(100\\) gates and the success test \\(20\\) gates. For \\(p=10^{-4}\\), compare the total cost of about \\(1/p\\) independent trials, each a preparation and a test, with about \\(\\tfrac\\pi4/\\sqrt p\\) amplification iterations, each using \\(A\\), \\(A^\\dagger\\), and the test. Preparing an arbitrary list of \\(N\\) amplitudes costs about \\(N\\) gates. For a search among \\(N\\) items with one marked item, so that \\(p=1/N\\), compare the cost of amplitude amplification with a classical scan of the list."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and the initial success probability. \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{2/32}=1/4\\), and the initial success probability is \\(1/16\\), the same as for one marked item among sixteen."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-2", "text": "For \\(N=8\\) with one marked item, find \\(\\theta\\), the number of iterations \\(k\\) that maximizes the success probability, and that probability. What happens at \\(k=3\\)? \\(\\theta=\\arcsin(1/\\sqrt8)\\approx0.361\\) rad (about \\(20.7^\\circ\\)), and \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12\\approx1.67\\), so \\(k=2\\). The success probabilities for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are about \\(0.125,\\ 0.781,\\ 0.945,\\ 0.330\\). The best choice gives \\(0.945\\) with two oracle queries, against a classical guess-and-check that needs about four queries for even a \\(50\\%\\) chance. At \\(k=3\\) the state has rotated past the good direction, and the success probability falls to \\(0.33\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-3", "text": "For \\(N=4\\) with three marked items, find \\(\\theta\\) and the success probability before and after one Grover iteration. What should a search algorithm do when most items are marked? \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt3/2\\) gives \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\), and the initial success probability is \\(\\frac34\\). One iteration turns the state to \\(3\\theta=\\pi\\), so \\(p_1=\\sin^2\\pi=0\\): every outcome is unmarked. The state started closer to the good direction than one rotation step, and the rotation overshot completely. When the marked fraction is large, a random guess already succeeds with high probability, so the algorithm should simply sample and check. The randomized schedules of Section 9.3.3 include zero iterations among their choices, which covers this case."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-4", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find the number of Grover iterations that maximizes success, and the success probability. With \\(\\theta\\approx14.48^\\circ\\), the success probabilities for \\(k=2,3,4\\) are about \\(0.91\\), \\(0.96\\), and \\(0.58\\). The best is \\(k=3\\), with success about \\(0.96\\). Only the marked fraction matters, not \\(N\\) or \\(M\\) separately."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-5", "text": "A one-qubit preparation \\(A\\) gives \\(A\\ket0=\\cos\\alpha\\ket0+\\sin\\alpha\\ket1\\) with \\(\\alpha=\\pi/10\\), and success means outcome \\(1\\). (a) Find \\(p\\) and \\(\\theta\\). (b) How many amplification iterations give certain success? (c) How many independent trials of \\(A\\) are needed for success probability \\(0.99\\), and how do the two counts of calls to \\(A\\) or \\(A^\\dagger\\) compare? (a) \\(p=\\sin^2(\\pi/10)\\approx0.0955\\) and \\(\\theta=\\pi/10\\). (b) \\((2k+1)\\frac{\\pi}{10}=\\frac\\pi2\\) gives \\(k=2\\), so two iterations succeed with certainty. (c) Independent trials fail with probability \\((1-p)^t\\le e^{-pt}\\). Requiring \\(e^{-pt}\\le0.01\\) gives \\(t\\ge\\ln100/0.0955\\approx48\\) trials. The amplified version uses one initial \\(A\\) plus, per iteration, one \\(A^\\dagger\\) and one \\(A\\): five calls in all, together with two success reflections. A non-uniform preparation is amplified exactly like search, because only the angle \\(\\theta\\) matters."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-6", "text": "When \\(M\\) is unknown, choose the number of iterations \\(k\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). For \\(N=16\\), compute the average success probability when \\(M=1\\) and when \\(M=4\\). Why does a random choice protect against not knowing \\(M\\)? For \\(M=1\\), the probabilities for \\(k=0,\\dots,3\\) are about \\(0.063,\\ 0.473,\\ 0.908,\\ 0.961\\), averaging \\(0.60\\). For \\(M=4\\), they are \\(0.25,\\ 1,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.25\\), averaging \\(0.44\\). A fixed choice tuned for \\(M=1\\), namely \\(k=3\\), would give only \\(0.25\\) when \\(M=4\\), and the choice tuned for \\(M=4\\), \\(k=1\\), gives \\(0.47\\) when \\(M=1\\). Averaged over a range of \\(k\\), \\(\\sin^2\\) of a steadily advancing angle is close to \\(\\frac12\\) once the range covers about a quarter turn. So each random attempt succeeds with constant probability for every \\(M\\) in the range, and checking the candidate with one query confirms success. Growing the range gradually keeps the total query count of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-7", "text": "Two preparations are available for the same search task, each with a \\(20\\)-gate success test. \\(A_1\\) costs \\(10\\) gates and succeeds with \\(p_1=10^{-6}\\); \\(A_2\\) costs \\(1000\\) gates and succeeds with \\(p_2=10^{-3}\\). Using the estimate \\((C_A+C_G)/p\\) for classical repetition and \\((2C_A+C_G)/\\sqrt p\\) for amplitude amplification, compare the costs of both preparations under both strategies. Classical repetition: \\(A_1\\) costs \\(30\\times10^6=3\\times10^7\\) gates; \\(A_2\\) costs \\(1020\\times10^3\\approx1.0\\times10^6\\). The expensive but better preparation wins by a factor of about \\(30\\). Amplification: \\(A_1\\) costs \\(40\\times1000=4\\times10^4\\); \\(A_2\\) costs \\(2020\\times31.6\\approx6.4\\times10^4\\). Now the cheap preparation wins. Amplification takes a square root of \\(1/p\\) but pays the preparation cost in full each round, so improvements in \\(p\\) are worth less than they are classically. The best design depends on the whole cost formula, not on the success probability alone."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “Grover search always finds the marked item with certainty if you choose the right number of iterations.” Test the claim for \\(N=16\\), \\(M=1\\). What condition on \\(\\theta\\) would certainty require, and what is known about when it can hold for one marked item? For \\(N=16\\), the success probabilities are about \\(0.063,0.473,0.908,0.961,0.582,\\dots\\), so the best is \\(0.961\\) at \\(k=3\\), not \\(1\\). Certainty needs \\((2k+1)\\theta=\\pi/2\\) exactly, that is \\(\\sin^2\\big(\\pi/(4k+2)\\big)=M/N\\). With \\(M=1\\) the right side is \\(1/N\\), a rational number. By a theorem of Niven, the only rational values of \\(\\sin^2\\) at rational multiples of \\(\\pi\\) are \\(0,\\frac14,\\frac12,\\frac34,1\\), so among \\(N\\ge2\\) only \\(N=4\\) gives certainty with standard iterations. Grover’s algorithm guarantees success probability at least \\(1-M/N\\) at the best \\(k\\). Exact success is possible, but it needs modified phases in the final iteration, a technique developed in Quantum Algorithms."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-9", "text": "For \\(N=4\\) with two marked items, show that Grover iterations never change the success probability. \\(\\theta=45^\\circ\\), so the angles \\((2k+1)45^\\circ\\) are odd multiples of \\(45^\\circ\\), and \\(\\sin^2\\) of every one is \\(1/2\\). Iterating never helps: when half the items are marked, a random guess is already as good as the algorithm can do."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-10", "text": "Prove the guarantee behind the stopping rule. Let \\(k\\) be the integer nearest to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12\\). (a) Show that \\((2k+1)\\theta\\) lies within \\(\\theta\\) of \\(\\pi/2\\). (b) Deduce \\(p_k\\ge\\cos^2\\theta=1-M/N\\). (c) Show that \\(k\\le\\frac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}\\), using \\(\\theta\\ge\\sin\\theta\\). (a) Let \\(k^*=\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12\\), so \\((2k^*+1)\\theta=\\frac\\pi2\\) exactly. The nearest integer satisfies \\(|k-k^*|\\le\\frac12\\), so \\(|(2k+1)\\theta-\\frac\\pi2|=2\\theta|k-k^*|\\le\\theta\\). (b) Writing \\(\\varphi=(2k+1)\\theta=\\frac\\pi2+\\delta\\) with \\(|\\delta|\\le\\theta\\), we get \\(\\sin^2\\varphi=\\cos^2\\delta\\ge\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta=1-M/N\\), since \\(\\theta<\\pi/2\\). (c) \\(k\\le k^*+\\frac12=\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}\\le\\frac{\\pi}{4\\sin\\theta}=\\frac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}\\). So at most \\(\\frac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}\\) queries achieve success probability at least \\(1-M/N\\), which is the quantitative form of the quadratic speedup for search."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-1-1", "text": "10.1.1 Input size and success criteria § worst case When can we say that one method is faster than another? Only when both solve the same task on the same terms: the same encoding of inputs, the same measure of input size, the same access to data, and the same standard of success. Why does the encoding matter? Size is measured by the length of the input. An integer \\(N\\) written in binary has about \\(\\log_2N\\) bits, so a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps takes a number of steps exponential in the input length. Which cost do we report? The worst case over all inputs, the average over a stated distribution of inputs, or the expected cost of an algorithm that uses randomness. Each is legitimate, but they must not be mixed. In a query model, we must also say what one query returns. Changing the interface changes the problem, not merely the method used to solve it. Cn is the complete circuit selected by the input length n. The bundled wire includes input and initialized workspace; the measured output is interpreted according to the stated task. Problems How many bits does \\(N=10^9\\) have in binary? A procedure takes about \\(\\sqrt N\\) steps. Is that polynomial in the input length? An algorithm takes \\(5\\) steps on half of the \\(n\\)-bit inputs and \\(2^n\\) steps on the other half. Give its worst-case cost and its average cost over uniformly random inputs. Trial division finds a factor of \\(N\\) by testing the divisors \\(2,3,\\dots,\\lfloor\\sqrt N\\rfloor\\). Express its worst-case number of divisions in terms of the input length \\(n=\\log_2N\\). Is it polynomial-time?"}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-1-2", "text": "10.1.2 Asymptotic notation § asymptotic notation · \\(O(g(n))\\) · \\(\\Omega\\) · \\(\\Theta\\) To compare growth, write \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) when some constant \\(C>0\\) bounds \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\). Write \\(\\Omega\\) for the corresponding lower bound and \\(\\Theta\\) when both hold. For \\(n\\geq1\\), \\[3n^2\\leq3n^2+7n+2\\leq12n^2.\\] The cost is therefore \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). This describes the growth of that cost expression; it does not prove that every algorithm for the task needs quadratic time. Asymptotic notation suppresses constant factors, lower-order detail, and finite-size crossover behavior. It does not supply the input encoding or computational model: those must be stated separately. Every fixed polynomial eventually grows more slowly than an exponential with base greater than one. “Eventually” matters. A large exponent or constant can make a polynomial method impractical over the entire range of inputs we can use. Scaling and actual costs answer related but different questions. An abstract Cn box stands for a full circuit whose cost has been counted separately. The growth estimate describes that construction, not a lower bound on every algorithm. Problems Is \\(100n=O(n^2)\\)? Is \\(n^2=O(100n)\\)? Give constants, or explain why none exist. Two algorithms take exactly \\(1000n\\) and \\(n^2\\) steps. Which is faster at \\(n=100\\), \\(n=1000\\), and \\(n=10{,}000\\)? Which scales better? Explain why these are different questions. For which input lengths \\(n\\geq2\\) is an algorithm costing \\(n^{10}\\) steps faster than one costing \\(2^n\\) steps? What does this show about asymptotic comparisons?"}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-1-3", "text": "10.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § query complexity · gate complexity · space complexity Which resource are we counting? Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. Physical running time depends further on scheduling, error correction, and the device. Does an advantage in one resource carry over to another? Not automatically. A quantum query advantage may survive, or disappear, once the cost of implementing each query and of preparing the input is counted. For unstructured search, the quadratic reduction in queries is a theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster overall. This illustrative schedule uses two queries. U0, U1, U2 and the oracle implementations may each contain many elementary gates; the bundled wire includes workspace. Problems A Grover search over \\(N=10^6\\) items uses about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) queries. If each query costs \\(1000\\) gates and each reflection \\(50\\) gates, estimate the total gate count. A classical scan costs one operation per item. For \\(N=10^6\\), below which cost \\(c\\) per query does a Grover search, costing about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\cdot c\\) gates, beat the scan? Algorithm A uses \\(n^2\\) queries and \\(n^3\\) other gates; algorithm B uses \\(n\\) queries and \\(n^4\\) other gates. If each query costs \\(n^k\\) gates, which algorithm has the smaller total gate count, depending on \\(k\\)?"}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-2-1", "text": "10.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § decision problem · P · NP · certificate · polynomial-time reduction Return to the distinction between finding an argument and checking one. P consists of decision problems solvable in polynomial time by a deterministic algorithm. NP consists of decision problems whose yes-instances have polynomial-size certificates checkable in polynomial time. A proposed nontrivial factor of an integer illustrates the difference: division checks the candidate, but the checking procedure does not tell us how to find it. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine can guess a certificate and accept if some branch verifies it. This says nothing about the probability that a random guess succeeds. A polynomial-time reduction from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) converts an instance of \\(A\\) into one of \\(B\\) while preserving the answer. An efficient solver for \\(B\\) would then give one for \\(A\\). Notice the direction: the solver for the target problem helps solve the source problem. Whether every efficiently verifiable decision problem is efficiently solvable—whether P equals NP—remains open. We need these definitions to state computational claims precisely, not to suggest that quantum computation has already settled them. The reversible implementation of a classical verifier preserves x and certificate w and writes V(x,w) into a blank bit. This checks a supplied certificate; it does not find one. Internal workspace is suppressed. Problems Which of these are decision problems: (a) is \\(N\\) prime? (b) find a factor of \\(N\\); (c) does a graph \\(G\\) contain a path from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\)? For the problem “does \\(N\\) have a factor \\(d\\) with \\(1\\lt d\\lt k\\)?”, give a witness for \\(N=91\\) and \\(k=10\\), and explain how a witness is checked in polynomial time. Prove that if \\(A\\) reduces to \\(B\\) in polynomial time and \\(B\\) is in P, then \\(A\\) is in P. Why must the reduction itself run in polynomial time?"}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-2-2", "text": "10.2.2 BPP and BQP § BPP · BQP What if an algorithm may use randomness and occasionally err? BPP is the class of decision problems solved in polynomial time by a randomized classical algorithm whose probability of error is at most \\(1/3\\) on every input. Why \\(1/3\\)? Any constant below \\(1/2\\) would do: repeating the algorithm and taking the majority answer drives the error down quickly. BQP is the quantum counterpart: decision problems solved, with error at most \\(1/3\\), by a uniform family of polynomial-size quantum circuits. How do the classes compare? A quantum circuit can simulate a randomized classical computation, so \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). Is BQP larger? This is not known, and neither is whether BQP contains NP. These are classes of decision problems; factoring is usually stated as a task whose answer is a number. A BQP circuit family must be uniformly generated with polynomial gate count and workspace. Each run yields a decision bit; repeated fresh runs can reduce its error. Problems An algorithm errs with probability at most \\(1/3\\). Run it three times independently and take the majority answer. What is the error probability? How many independent runs, with a majority vote, bring the error below \\(0.01\\) when each run errs with probability \\(1/3\\)? Show that an algorithm that ignores its input and answers by flipping a fair coin has error \\(1/2\\) on every input, and that majority voting over repeated runs never improves it. Why is the condition “error at most \\(1/3\\)” essential?"}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-2-3", "text": "10.2.3 Factoring and search § period finding What evidence is there that quantum computers are more powerful? There are two kinds, of different strength. Factoring: quantum period finding gives a factoring algorithm whose running time is polynomial in the number of digits; its derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical algorithm is known. No one has proved that none exists, however, so this is an advantage over known methods, not a proven separation. Search: Grover’s algorithm has a proved quadratic advantage, but only in the unstructured query model. Neither kind of evidence suggests that quantum computers speed up every task. The search circuit has a proved quadratic query advantage. This does not establish a general computational-class separation or give a factoring construction. Problems Trial division of a \\(2048\\)-bit number may test up to \\(\\sqrt N\\approx2^{1024}\\) divisors. Write this roughly as a power of ten. A rough estimate for Shor’s factoring algorithm is \\(n^3\\) operations for an \\(n\\)-bit number. Compare \\(n^3\\) with \\(2^{n/2}\\) for \\(n=2048\\). The best known classical factoring method, the number field sieve, takes time roughly \\(\\exp\\bigl(1.92(\\ln N)^{1/3}(\\ln\\ln N)^{2/3}\\bigr)\\). Evaluate it for a \\(2048\\)-bit \\(N\\), and explain why this comparison does not prove that quantum computers are exponentially more powerful."}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-3-1", "text": "10.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § upper bound · lower bound An upper bound is constructive: exhibit one algorithm and bound its cost. A lower bound is universal: show that every algorithm allowed by the model needs at least a certain resource. An unsuccessful search for a faster method is neither proof nor evidence sufficient for a lower bound. Query models isolate the obstacle. The hidden input can affect the computation only through oracle calls; the intervening operations must be independent of that hidden input except through what the calls make available. This restriction can make a lower-bound proof possible even when gate-by-gate analysis of every imaginable algorithm would be hopeless. Its conclusion is strong within the model. It does not determine the physical cost of a query, or automatically apply when a different interface reveals more structure. A query algorithm alternates oracle calls with operations independent of the hidden input. The two-query drawing is one instance of this general form; a lower bound must cover every allowed sequence. Problems Which of these establishes a lower bound on query complexity: (a) an algorithm using \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (b) a proof that every algorithm needs at least \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (c) many failed attempts to find an algorithm with fewer queries? Using the fact that \\(k\\) classical queries find a single marked item among \\(N\\) with probability at most \\(k/N\\), show that success probability \\(1/2\\) needs at least \\(N/2\\) queries. In the query model, an algorithm may compute without limit between queries. Explain why a query lower bound still applies to algorithms with limited computation, but a query upper bound need not give a fast algorithm."}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-3-2", "text": "10.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § hybrid argument Can a quantum algorithm search faster than Grover’s? No. Finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) with constant success probability requires \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s algorithm uses \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\), so the query complexity of the task is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Why can it not be faster? A single query can change the state substantially only through the amplitude on the marked label, and averaged over all possible positions of the mark, that amplitude is small. The hybrid argument makes this precise; we state its conclusion as a theorem. The geometry of the Grover iteration proves an upper bound, not optimality. The two circuits use identical intervening gates and differ only in the oracle. The lower-bound argument compares their final states; the diagram itself is not a proof of the bound. Problems For \\(N=10^6\\), compare Grover’s query count, about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\), with the classical average \\(N/2\\). If some quantum algorithm searched with only \\(N^{1/3}\\) queries, how many would it use for \\(N=10^6\\)? Which result rules this out? Use the lower bound to show that no quantum algorithm finds a marked item among \\(N=2^n\\) with a number of queries polynomial in \\(n\\). What does this imply for solving NP problems by unstructured search?"}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-3-3", "text": "10.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § approximate degree Optional derivation · polynomial lower bounds Is there another route to lower bounds? Write the hidden input of the oracle as bits \\(z_1,\\dots,z_N\\). After \\(T\\) queries, every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in these bits, and every acceptance probability is a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\). An algorithm that is correct with probability at least \\(2/3\\) therefore yields a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\) that approximates the function it computes on every Boolean input. If every such approximating polynomial needs degree at least \\(d\\), the approximate degree , then \\(2T\\geq d\\), so \\(T\\geq d/2\\). For OR on \\(N\\) bits, which detects whether any item is marked, the approximate degree is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). This gives a second proof of the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound. Appendix E explains why the degree grows by at most one per query; the approximate degree of OR is a separate theorem. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials . The two-query example gives amplitudes of degree at most two in the hidden Boolean bits and acceptance probabilities of degree at most four. The text generalizes this to T queries. Problems After \\(T=3\\) queries, what is the largest possible degree of an amplitude, and of an acceptance probability? The approximate degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits grows like \\(\\sqrt N\\). For \\(N=10^4\\), what lower bound on queries does \\(T\\geq d/2\\) give, up to a constant factor? Show that the exact degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits is \\(N\\), by writing \\(\\mathrm{OR}(z)=1-\\prod_i(1-z_i)\\). What does this imply for algorithms that must be correct with certainty?"}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-4-1", "text": "10.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § What do the algorithms of this book have in common? Each uses coherent operations to make the property we want affect the probabilities of the final outcome. Deutsch’s algorithm turns the equality of two function values into a relative phase , and then into a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward the successful outcomes. What do their advantages depend on? Specified oracles, coherent access to inverses where they are used, and a measurement chosen to expose the answer. The large state space gives room for these constructions, but size alone explains nothing: correctness is established by calculating the evolution and the readout. The preparation A and its associated reflection must be implemented coherently. The success reflection changes phase; the prepared-state reflection converts that phase change into a change in success probability. Problems For Deutsch’s algorithm and for Grover search, name the property that is encoded in phases, and the operation that turns it into probability. For each of the two algorithms, list costs that its query count does not include. An algorithm prepares the uniform superposition of \\(n\\) qubits, applies an oracle once, and measures immediately. Show that it does no better than one classical query, whether the oracle is a phase oracle or the reversible \\(U_f\\)."}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-4-2", "text": "10.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § Quantum computation changes some resource costs without removing all limits. In the standard effective circuit model, the gates and their required approximations have computable descriptions. A classical computer can simulate the resulting finite computations, generally at enormous cost; bounded-error decisions can be resolved by sufficiently accurate simulation. This does not furnish a general halting decider. Other restrictions have different origins. Linearity prevents universal cloning. Measurement returns a sampled outcome rather than a readable list of all amplitudes. Input preparation, oracle construction, and protection from noise consume resources even when an ideal query count is small. These are not reasons to dismiss an algorithm. They tell us what a convincing claim must contain: a specified task, an allowed access model, an observable answer with a success guarantee, and the costs of producing it. A computably described finite circuit can be simulated classically, potentially at enormous cost. Its measurement returns a sample, not its complete amplitude list or a general halting decision. Problems Can a quantum computer decide the halting problem? Why not? A classical simulation of an \\(n\\)-qubit circuit stores all \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. How does its memory grow, and why does the simulation nevertheless show that quantum computers compute nothing beyond Turing machines? A source promises to prepare either \\(\\ket0\\) on every run or \\(\\ket+\\) on every run. After obtaining \\(k\\) copies, the alternatives are \\(\\ket0^{\\otimes k}\\) and \\(\\ket+^{\\otimes k}\\). Compute their overlap. Can any finite number of copies guarantee perfect discrimination? How does that differ from making the error very small?"}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#topic-10-4-3", "text": "10.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § quantum Fourier transform Two natural continuations now have a clear starting point. Period finding uses controlled operations, phase, and a quantum Fourier transform to make periodic structure observable. Error correction uses joint states, subspaces, and measurements to protect information while computations proceed. Neither requires abandoning the rules developed here. Each requires new constructions and a proof that its measurements reveal the intended information without losing what is still needed. When you meet such a construction, ask three questions: What property has been encoded in the state? Which operation makes it visible to measurement? What did preparing and protecting that process cost? If you can answer those, a large circuit becomes a chain of understandable claims. This repetition encoding is a concrete continuation toward error correction. It creates one encoded joint state, not three clones. Syndrome extraction and recovery still need further circuits; period finding is a separate continuation. Problems What is the period of \\(f(x)=2^x\\bmod15\\)? Use the period \\(r=4\\) of \\(2^x\\bmod15\\) to factor \\(15\\), by computing \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}-1,15)\\) and \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}+1,15)\\). Let \\(a^x\\bmod N\\) have even period \\(r\\), with \\(a^{r/2}\\not\\equiv-1\\pmod N\\). Show that \\(\\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,N)\\) is a factor of \\(N\\) other than \\(1\\) and \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-1", "text": "An algorithm uses \\(O(N)\\) steps on an integer \\(N\\) written in binary. Is that polynomial in the input length? Not in general. The input length is about \\(\\log_2N\\), so \\(N\\) steps can be exponential in that length."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-2", "text": "Which of these costs are \\(O(n^2)\\)? (a) \\(5n\\log_2n\\); (b) \\(n^2/1000+10^6n\\); (c) \\(n^{2.01}\\); (d) \\((\\log_2n)^5\\); (e) \\(2^{\\log_2n}\\). For any that is not, explain why no constant works. (a), (b), (d), and (e) are \\(O(n^2)\\). For (a), \\(n\\log n\\le n^2\\) for \\(n\\ge1\\). For (b), the ratio to \\(n^2\\) tends to \\(1/1000\\). For (d), every power of \\(\\log n\\) grows more slowly than any power of \\(n\\). For (e), \\(2^{\\log_2n}=n\\). (c) is not: \\(n^{2.01}/n^2=n^{0.01}\\) grows without bound, so no constant \\(C\\) satisfies \\(n^{2.01}\\le Cn^2\\) for all large \\(n\\), although it exceeds \\(10\\) only when \\(n>10^{100}\\). Asymptotic classes can disagree with practical experience over every input we will ever use."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-3", "text": "Classify each statement as proved, open, or false according to Section 10.2: (a) \\(\\mathrm P\\subseteq\\mathrm{NP}\\); (b) \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\); (c) \\(\\mathrm{NP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\); (d) a quantum algorithm factors in time polynomial in the number of digits; (e) no classical algorithm factors in polynomial time. (a) Proved: a polynomial-time solver can serve as its own checker, ignoring the certificate. (b) Proved: quantum circuits can simulate randomized classical computation. (c) Open: no one knows whether quantum computers solve every NP problem efficiently, and the search lower bound shows that brute-force search will not do it. (d) Proved: Shor’s algorithm, cited but not derived here. (e) Open: no polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm is known, but none has been ruled out. The evidence for a quantum advantage in factoring rests on (d) together with the absence of a known classical method, not on a proof of (e)."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-4", "text": "One algorithm uses at most \\(3n^2+7n+2\\) operations, and at least \\(n^2\\). Give constants showing that its cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). Does this prove that every algorithm for the task needs quadratic time? For \\(n\\geq1\\), \\(n^2\\leq3n^2+7n+2\\leq3n^2+7n^2+2n^2=12n^2\\), so the cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\) with constants \\(1\\) and \\(12\\). This describes one algorithm. A lower bound for the task would have to hold for every algorithm, which needs a separate argument."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-5", "text": "Let \\(D(N,k)\\) be the decision problem “does \\(N\\) have a factor \\(d\\) with \\(1<d<k\\)?” Show how an algorithm that answers \\(D\\) can find the smallest nontrivial factor of \\(N\\) using about \\(\\log_2N\\) calls. Carry out the search for \\(N=91\\). Why does this show that the decision version captures the difficulty of finding a factor? As \\(k\\) increases, \\(D(N,k)\\) changes from false to true exactly when \\(k\\) passes the smallest factor \\(d\\), so the smallest \\(k\\) with \\(D(N,k)\\) true is \\(d+1\\). Binary search over \\(k\\in[2,N]\\) finds that threshold in about \\(\\log_2N=n\\) calls. For \\(N=91\\): \\(D(91,47)\\), \\(D(91,24)\\), and \\(D(91,13)\\) are true; \\(D(91,7)\\) is false, since none of \\(2,\\dots,6\\) divides \\(91\\); then \\(D(91,10)\\) and \\(D(91,8)\\) are true. The threshold is \\(k=8\\), so \\(d=7\\). Dividing, \\(91=7\\times13\\). Repeating on the cofactor finds every prime factor. A polynomial-time algorithm for \\(D\\) would therefore give a polynomial-time factoring algorithm, so stating complexity results for decision problems loses nothing essential here."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compute the acceptance probability of Deutsch’s algorithm as a polynomial in the hidden bits \\(z_0=f(0)\\) and \\(z_1=f(1)\\), using \\(s_x=1-2z_x\\). Check that its degree is at most \\(2T\\) with \\(T=1\\), and identify the function it computes exactly. The probability of outcome \\(1\\) is \\(\\big(\\frac{s_0-s_1}{2}\\big)^2=(z_1-z_0)^2=z_0+z_1-2z_0z_1\\), using \\(z^2=z\\) for a bit. This has degree \\(2=2T\\), the maximum the polynomial method allows for one query. On the four inputs it takes the values \\(0,1,1,0\\), so it equals \\(z_0\\oplus z_1\\) exactly: the algorithm computes parity with certainty. The polynomial view explains why one query is enough here: the exact degree of two-bit parity is \\(2\\), which one query can reach. For OR on many bits the approximate degree grows like \\(\\sqrt N\\), which forces many queries."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-7", "text": "A satisfiability instance has \\(n=100\\) variables, and Grover search is run over all \\(2^{100}\\) assignments with one satisfying assignment. (a) About how many Grover iterations are needed? (b) At an optimistic \\(10^6\\) iterations per second, how long does it take? (c) Express the query counts of brute-force classical search and Grover search in terms of the input length, and say what the quadratic speedup does and does not change. (a) About \\(\\frac\\pi4\\cdot2^{50}\\approx8.8\\times10^{14}\\) iterations. (b) At \\(10^6\\) per second that is about \\(8.8\\times10^8\\) seconds, roughly \\(28\\) years, before counting the gates inside each iteration or error correction. (c) Classical brute force needs order \\(2^n\\) evaluations and Grover search order \\(2^{n/2}\\). Both are exponential in \\(n\\): the quadratic speedup halves the exponent, which is valuable, but it does not make brute-force search polynomial. The lower bound of Section 10.3.2 shows that no unstructured quantum search does better, and practical classical solvers exploit the structure of satisfiability instances, so they must be part of the comparison."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “Searching needs \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) quantum queries, so no algorithm, quantum or classical, can find an item in a list of \\(N\\) faster than \\(\\sqrt N\\).” Give a counterexample using a sorted list and binary search, count its queries for \\(N=10^6\\), and state precisely what the lower bound covers. If the list is sorted and a query at position \\(i\\) returns the stored value, a classical binary search compares the target with the middle entry and halves the candidate range each time. It finds the item in about \\(\\log_2N\\approx20\\) queries for \\(N=10^6\\), far below \\(\\sqrt N=1000\\). There is no contradiction: the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) bound concerns unstructured search, where the oracle reports only whether a label is marked and reveals nothing about where else to look. A lower bound is a statement about a model. When an interface reveals structure, a different problem results, and both classical and quantum algorithms can exploit it."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-9", "text": "A quantum search uses \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, but each query requires \\(\\Theta(N)\\) gates in a chosen implementation. What do the query and gate counts say about this implementation, and about the task? The queries alone contribute \\(\\Theta(N^{3/2})\\) gates, worse than a classical scan of \\(N\\) items. The quadratic query advantage is still a true statement about oracle access. It does not show that this implementation beats a classical method, and a cheaper oracle implementation could change the comparison."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-10", "text": "Section 10.4.2 says a classical computer can simulate any quantum circuit, at enormous cost. Compare two strategies for a circuit on \\(n=50\\) qubits with \\(h=100\\) Hadamard gates. (a) Store the state vector, with \\(16\\) bytes per amplitude: how much memory is needed? (b) Use the path sums of Chapter 3: each Hadamard splits every path in two, so how many paths contribute to one output amplitude, and how much memory does following one path need? (c) What does the trade-off suggest about the source of the cost? (a) \\(2^{50}\\times16\\approx1.8\\times10^{16}\\) bytes, about \\(18\\) petabytes. (b) A final amplitude is a sum over up to \\(2^{100}\\approx1.3\\times10^{30}\\) paths. Each path is evaluated by following one basis configuration through the circuit and multiplying the weights, which needs only enough memory for \\(n\\) bits and a running product; the sum can be accumulated one path at a time. (c) One method trades exponential memory for less time, the other uses little memory but exponential time. Neither removes the exponential cost; it moves it between resources. Both show that quantum circuits compute nothing beyond what Turing machines can, while leaving open whether some quantum computations are inherently expensive to simulate, which is the question behind BQP and BPP."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory finite automaton · pushdown automaton · linear-bounded automaton How much memory does a computation need? Start with the least: a finite automaton , which reads its input once while keeping one of finitely many control states. Two states suffice to track parity, whether the number of ones read so far is even or odd: reading a one swaps the states, and reading a zero leaves them unchanged. Can a finite automaton check that a string consists of some number of \\(a\\)’s followed by the same number of \\(b\\)’s, the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\)? No. A fixed automaton has finitely many states, so two different long prefixes \\(a^i\\) and \\(a^j\\) must reach the same state. Appending \\(b^i\\) then forces the same decision on \\(a^ib^i\\), which is in the language, and on \\(a^jb^i\\), which is not. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. What extra memory is enough? A stack , which can hold arbitrarily many pending symbols: push once for each \\(a\\), then pop once for each \\(b\\). Reject an \\(a\\) after the first \\(b\\), or a \\(b\\) with nothing left to pop. Accept when the input and stack run out together. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has a writable tape no longer than a constant multiple of the input, and a Turing machine removes even that bound. Do these models form a sequence of ever better machines? Not in that sense. More memory changes which configurations are available, not what a single step means. The models describe different classes of languages; they are not successive generations of hardware. Reading \\(aabb\\) pushes for each \\(a\\) and pops for each \\(b\\). The empty stack at the end certifies matching counts in this example; checking order is also required. Problems Design a two-state finite automaton that accepts the binary strings with an even number of \\(1\\)s, and trace it on \\(1011\\). Run the stack procedure on \\(aaabbb\\) and on \\(aabbb\\), giving the stack height after each symbol. Show that no finite automaton recognizes the strings over \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) with equal numbers of \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s, in any order."}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus grammar · lambda calculus · Church numeral Instead of recognizing strings, can we describe a set of strings by how its members are produced? A grammar does this. It specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), where \\(\\epsilon\\) is the empty string, generate exactly the strings of matching \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s: applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). How do grammars relate to machines? Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a given string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linear-bounded automata, and unrestricted grammars to the languages a Turing machine recognizes, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Can computation be described without any machine at all? Lambda calculus uses only functions, application, and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces the free occurrences of \\(x\\) in \\(t\\) by \\(u\\), renaming bound variables where necessary to avoid capture; for example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). How can functions alone represent numbers? A Church numeral \\(n\\) is the function that applies a given function \\(n\\) times to a given starting value, so three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. The functions computable in lambda calculus are exactly the Turing-computable ones. That equivalence between formal models supports the broader Church–Turing thesis; it does not prove it. Apply \\(S\\to aSb\\) twice, then \\(S\\to\\) empty string. This demonstrates generation of \\(aabb\\); it does not yet give a procedure for rejecting arbitrary strings. Problems Derive \\(aaabbb\\) from \\(S\\) with the rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), writing each step. Reduce \\((\\lambda x.\\lambda y.x)\\,a\\,b\\) step by step. With Church numerals, show that \\(\\mathrm{SUCC}=\\lambda n.\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(n\\,f\\,x)\\) applied to \\(2=\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f\\,x)\\) gives \\(3\\)."}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference operator norm This appendix collects the linear algebra used in the book; each identity is explained where it is first needed. How are complex vectors compared? Through the inner product \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\) is the conjugate transpose. The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix \\(B\\) satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and \\(B^\\dagger\\) converts old coordinates to new ones. In which order do matrices act? From right to left on column vectors, and the adjoint reverses the order of a product: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Three properties of a matrix are easily confused. A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian matrix equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). How do tensor products behave? \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. How large can a matrix make a vector? The operator norm \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\) answers this. It gives uniform error bounds, because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector \\(v\\). For the complex vector \\((3,4i)\\), the squared norm is \\(9+16=25\\). Dividing by five gives a unit vector; the imaginary coordinate contributes positively to its norm. Problems What is the operator norm of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(3,1)\\), and of a unitary \\(U\\)? Verify \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\) for \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Show that a projector \\(P\\), with \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\), has only the eigenvalues \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and that \\(I-P\\) is also a projector."}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements density matrix · reduced state · effects What if we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states, \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\)? In general, no single state vector describes that preparation. The density matrix \\[\\rho=\\sum_rq_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\] does. Its trace—the sum of its diagonal entries—is one. It is positive semidefinite, meaning \\(\\bra v\\rho\\ket v\\geq0\\) for every vector \\(\\ket v\\); this ensures nonnegative measurement probabilities. A pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\). Different ensembles can give the same density matrix, and then they give the same predictions for every measurement. The measurement rule becomes \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution sends \\(\\rho\\) to \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). What is the state of one part of an entangled pair? Its reduced state \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\), computed by summing over the unobserved basis label: \\((\\rho_A)_{a,a^\\prime}=\\sum_b(\\rho_{AB})_{ab,a^\\prime b}\\). The same \\(b\\) appears in the row and column because we ignore which outcome occurred in \\(B\\). For either half of a Bell state , it is \\(I/2\\). Can an operation on \\(B\\) change \\(\\rho_A\\)? Not when its outcome is ignored. A trace-preserving operation on \\(B\\), with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves \\(\\rho_A\\) unchanged; inserting the sum into the partial trace shows this. Conditioning on the outcome \\(r\\) changes the description only for someone who knows \\(r\\). Are projective measurements the only ones? No. A general measurement has positive operators \\(E_j\\), called effects , that sum to the identity, with probabilities \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). The effects fix the probabilities but not the state after the measurement; measurement operators supply that further information. Can we sometimes identify either of two nonorthogonal states with certainty, provided we allow an inconclusive result? A measurement with three outcomes makes this possible. Suppose the state is either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\). With \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), take \\[E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-,\\qquad E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1,\\qquad E_?=I-E_0-E_+.\\] The outcome \\(0\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the outcome \\(+\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket0\\), and the outcome \\(?\\) is inconclusive. \\(E_?\\) is positive because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Whenever this measurement gives an answer, the answer is certain; the price is that it sometimes declines to answer. An equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and an equal mixture of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) both yield \\(I/2\\). No measurement on the qubit alone identifies which ensemble was used. Problems Write the density matrix of the ensemble that prepares \\(\\ket0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). What is the probability of outcome \\(0\\)? Show that the ensembles \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\), each with equal probabilities, have the same density matrix. For the measurement with effects \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_+\\), where \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), compute the probability of the inconclusive outcome for \\(\\ket0\\) and for \\(\\ket+\\)."}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds approximate degree Why must a search algorithm make many queries? In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\), and a query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) into \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). How does each query change the amplitudes? Every new amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or by \\(1-z_i\\). The amplitudes therefore start as constants and gain degree at most one per query. Operations that do not depend on the input only form linear combinations, which do not raise the degree. By induction, after \\(T\\) queries every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in the input bits. What about the probability of accepting? It is a sum of squared magnitudes of amplitudes, so its degree is at most \\(2T\\). On Boolean inputs, any power \\(z_i^k\\) with \\(k\\geq1\\) equals \\(z_i\\), so the polynomial can be taken to be multilinear. The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial that approximates it, within a fixed error below one half, on every Boolean input. An algorithm with bounded error yields such a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\), so its number of queries is at least half the approximate degree. Proving that OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) needs an argument from approximation theory, beyond this short derivation. Together with the polynomial representation, that theorem gives the lower bound for detecting a marked item. Neither follows from the observation that known algorithms are slow. Starting from amplitudes independent of hidden input bits, each Boolean query raises their degree by at most one. Squaring magnitudes gives the factor of two for acceptance probabilities. Problems With two hidden bits and the query \\(\\ket{i,b}\\mapsto\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\), start from \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Write the amplitudes after one query as polynomials in \\(z_1,z_2\\). Use \\(z_i^2=z_i\\) on Boolean inputs to reduce \\(z_1^2z_2+z_1z_2^2\\) to a multilinear polynomial. Write the parity of two bits, \\(z_1\\oplus z_2\\), as a real polynomial, and show that its degree is \\(2\\). What lower bound on queries does this give for an exact algorithm, and is it achieved?"}, {"title": "Adjoint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adjoint", "text": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix, written with a dagger. It is not generally the inverse."}, {"title": "Algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#algorithm", "text": "A finite set of precise instructions for carrying out a task. Whether it terminates on every allowed input must be established."}, {"title": "Amplitude", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude", "text": "A complex coefficient used to describe a quantum state or transition. The squared magnitude gives the corresponding probability in the specified measurement basis."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "A sequence of reflections that increases the amplitude in a designated successful subspace, given suitable preparation and inverse operations."}, {"title": "Bell state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bell-state", "text": "One of four maximally entangled two-qubit basis states. The book begins with the equal superposition of 00 and 11."}, {"title": "Boolean function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#boolean-function", "text": "A function whose inputs and outputs are bits. A truth table specifies its value for every input."}, {"title": "BQP", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bqp", "text": "The class of decision problems solvable by uniform polynomial-size quantum circuits with bounded error."}, {"title": "Circuit depth", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#circuit-depth", "text": "The number of sequential layers of operations when compatible operations can run in parallel."}, {"title": "Computability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#computability", "text": "Whether a task can be performed by an effective procedure, without imposing an efficiency requirement."}, {"title": "Consistency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#consistency", "text": "A formal theory is consistent if its rules cannot prove both a statement and its negation."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required answer is yes or no for each allowed input."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#entanglement", "text": "For a pure joint state, the inability to write it as a product of subsystem states. Mixed-state entanglement requires a broader definition."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common phase factor multiplying an entire state vector. It leaves all measurement probabilities unchanged."}, {"title": "Grover iteration", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iteration", "text": "A phase reversal on marked items followed by a reflection about the prepared uniform state in the standard search algorithm."}, {"title": "Halting problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#halting-problem", "text": "The task of deciding, for every program and input, whether the program eventually stops. No algorithm solves it in full generality."}, {"title": "Hermitian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hermitian", "text": "An operator equal to its adjoint. Its eigenvalues are real and its eigenspaces can describe a projective measurement."}, {"title": "Inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inner-product", "text": "The overlap of two state vectors, obtained by conjugating the coordinates of the first and summing their products with the second. With the final state on the left and the initial state on the right, it is the amplitude for that final-state outcome."}, {"title": "Interference", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#interference", "text": "The addition of amplitudes for indistinguishable alternatives, which can increase or decrease an outcome probability."}, {"title": "Lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#lower-bound", "text": "A limit on the resources required by every algorithm within a specified model, rather than the cost of one construction."}, {"title": "NAND", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#nand", "text": "The Boolean operation that returns zero exactly when both input bits are one. NAND gates suffice to build every Boolean function."}, {"title": "Normalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#normalization", "text": "The requirement that a state vector has squared norm one, so the probabilities of a complete basis measurement sum to one."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "A specified operation that provides access to a function. Counting its uses does not by itself account for the cost of building it."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "When a controlled operation acts on a target eigenstate, its eigenvalue appears as a phase on the corresponding control component."}, {"title": "Product state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-state", "text": "A pure joint state that can be written as a tensor product of one state for each subsystem."}, {"title": "Projective measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#projective-measurement", "text": "A measurement described by mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. Each outcome probability is the squared norm of the projected state."}, {"title": "Quantum advantage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-advantage", "text": "An improvement over classical computation for a specified task, resource measure, and success guarantee. Its assumptions must be stated."}, {"title": "Quantum circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-circuit", "text": "An ordered description of operations on quantum registers, with any measurements and classical control specified."}, {"title": "Quantum parallelism", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-parallelism", "text": "One reversible evaluation of a function applied to a superposition of inputs, producing a state in which every input appears beside its own value. A measurement returns only one input–value pair; a useful algorithm must follow the evaluation with interference."}, {"title": "Qubit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qubit", "text": "A two-state quantum system. Its state is described by two amplitudes, one for each of the labels zero and one."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle calls required for a task under a stated success criterion and oracle model."}, {"title": "Relative phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#relative-phase", "text": "The phase difference between two components of a state. It can affect probabilities after further operations."}, {"title": "Reversible", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible", "text": "A transformation is reversible when its output determines its input uniquely on the allowed state space."}, {"title": "Superposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#superposition", "text": "A linear combination of basis states. Its components depend on the chosen basis; measurement does not reveal all coefficients at once."}, {"title": "Tensor product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#tensor-product", "text": "The construction of a joint vector space from component spaces. Basis labels combine in pairs and dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Transition weight", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#transition-weight", "text": "The number attached to an arrow from one configuration to another: 1 for a deterministic or allowed arrow, a probability for a probabilistic rule, a complex amplitude for a quantum operation."}, {"title": "Turing machine", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#turing-machine", "text": "A mathematical model with a tape, a read/write head, and finitely many control states. A transition rule specifies each step."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Applying the inverse of a computation to clear temporary workspace while retaining the needed result or phase."}, {"title": "Unitary", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#unitary", "text": "An operator whose adjoint is its inverse. Unitary transformations preserve inner products and describe closed-system quantum evolution."}, {"title": "Universal gate set", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#universal-gate-set", "text": "A set of gates sufficient to construct arbitrary quantum operations in the stated model, exactly or to a specified approximation."}, {"title": "Notation: not P", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-not-p", "text": "¬ P The negation of the sentence \\(P\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: f from n-bit strings to bits", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-f-from-n-bit-strings-to-bits", "text": "f:{0,1}ⁿ→{0,1} A Boolean function: one yes/no answer for each \\(n\\)-bit input string."}, {"title": "Notation: a XOR b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-xor-b", "text": "a⊕ b Exclusive OR: one when exactly one input is one. It is addition modulo two."}, {"title": "Notation: a and b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-and-b", "text": "a∧ b AND of two bits: one only when both are one."}, {"title": "Notation: k B T log two", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-k-b-t-log-two", "text": "k_BTln 2 Landauer’s minimum heat for erasing one equally likely bit at temperature \\(T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: x maps to y", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-maps-to-y", "text": "x↦ y The rule that sends each input \\(x\\) to the output \\(y\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: U sub f", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-u-sub-f", "text": "U_f The reversible oracle for \\(f\\): it keeps \\(x\\) and XORs \\(f(x)\\) into a target, \\(U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sum over j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sum-over-j", "text": "Σⱼ Add the following term for every value of \\(j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: probability of j given i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-probability-of-j-given-i", "text": "P(j| i) Conditional probability: the chance of next state \\(j\\) when the current state is \\(i\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: r e to the i phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-r-e-to-the-i-phi", "text": "re^(iφ) An amplitude with magnitude \\(r\\ge0\\) and phase \\(\\phi\\). Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-i", "text": "i The imaginary unit, with \\(i^2=-1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: z star", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-z-star", "text": "z* The complex conjugate: for \\(z=a+ib\\), \\(z^*=a-ib\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: magnitude of z", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-magnitude-of-z", "text": "|z| The size of a complex number. \\(|z|^2=z^*z\\) turns an amplitude into a probability weight."}, {"title": "Notation: v transpose", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-v-transpose", "text": "vᵀ Turns a row into a column or a column into a row; \\((a,b)^T\\) is a column vector."}, {"title": "Notation: ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-psi", "text": "|ψ⟩ A configuration or state, named \\(\\psi\\). It first records weighted configurations, as in \\(\\sum_iv_i\\ket i\\); from the chapter on states it is a column vector. The brackets name it; they are not an operation."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero, ket one", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-ket-one", "text": "|0⟩, |1⟩ The computational basis: \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra j S ket i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-j-s-ket-i", "text": "⟨j|S|i⟩ The weight of the arrow from configuration \\(i\\) to configuration \\(j\\) under the step \\(S\\), read right to left. For an operation \\(U\\) on vectors it is the matrix entry \\(U_{ji}\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi", "text": "⟨φ| The final configuration or state in a weight or amplitude, as in \\(\\bra jS\\ket i\\); as a vector, the conjugate-transpose row of \\(\\ket\\phi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi-ket-psi", "text": "⟨φ|ψ⟩ The inner product \\(\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\): the transition amplitude with final state \\(\\phi\\) on the left and initial state \\(\\psi\\) on the right."}, {"title": "Notation: norm of v", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-norm-of-v", "text": "|v| The length of a vector, \\(\\sqrt{\\braket vv}\\). A state vector has norm one."}, {"title": "Notation: ket a tensor ket b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-a-tensor-ket-b", "text": "|a⟩⊗|b⟩ The joint state of two systems, also written \\(\\ket{ab}\\). Dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Notation: Bell state phi plus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bell-state-phi-plus", "text": "|Φ⁺⟩ The entangled state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero to the n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-to-the-n", "text": "|0ⁿ⟩ All \\(n\\) qubits in state zero."}, {"title": "Notation: identity", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-identity", "text": "I The matrix that leaves every vector unchanged."}, {"title": "Notation: A dagger", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-dagger", "text": "A† The adjoint: the conjugate transpose \\((A^*)^T\\), defined by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: diagonal a, b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-diagonal-a-b", "text": "diag(a,b) The diagonal matrix with entries \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket e bra e", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-e-bra-e", "text": "|e⟩⟨e| The projector onto the unit vector \\(\\ket e\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: P sub j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-p-sub-j", "text": "Pⱼ The projector for measurement outcome \\(j\\). The Born rule gives \\(p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: X, Z, H", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-z-h", "text": "X, Z, H The bit flip, phase flip, and Hadamard gates."}, {"title": "Notation: A tensor n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-tensor-n", "text": "A^(⊗ n) \\(n\\) copies combined by the tensor product. \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) applies \\(H\\) to each of \\(n\\) qubits; \\(\\ket\\psi^{\\otimes2}\\) is two copies of \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: rho", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-rho", "text": "ρ A density matrix, describing a preparation that need not be pure."}, {"title": "Notation: trace", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-trace", "text": "tr The sum of diagonal entries. With \\(\\rho\\), the Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: minus one to the f of x", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-minus-one-to-the-f-of-x", "text": "(−1)^(f(x)) The sign a phase oracle attaches to input \\(x\\): \\(+1\\) when \\(f(x)=0\\), \\(-1\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sine squared theta equals M over N", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sine-squared-theta-equals-m-over-n", "text": "sin²θ=M/N Defines the search angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(M\\) marked items among \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: big O of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-o-of-g-of-n", "text": "O(g(n)) An eventual upper bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big omega of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-omega-of-g-of-n", "text": "Ω(g(n)) An eventual lower bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big theta of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-theta-of-g-of-n", "text": "Θ(g(n)) Both an upper and a lower bound: growth exactly like \\(g(n)\\) up to constants."}, {"title": "Notation: BPP is contained in BQP", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bpp-is-contained-in-bqp", "text": "BPP⊆BQP Every problem in the first class is also in the second."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda x dot t, applied to u", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda-x-dot-t-applied-to-u", "text": "(λ x.t)u In lambda calculus, a function with parameter \\(x\\) and body \\(t\\), applied to \\(u\\)."}];
